      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>The popularity of the blouse is still ot the increase, if one may judge by appear ances, for every woman who prides hersel upon lacing well dressed has this season pro vided herself with as large and varied E quantity of this useful piece of dress as hei allowance will permit. Blouses should be chosen to serve as many purposes as pos- sible, so as to avoid a plurality, and should go with any sort of dress and skirt and be ol not too perishable a nature. Indeed, a white and black blouse of silk, taffetas, ot viyella finished at the throat by a dainty</p>
        <p>.06- AtINL w. .-7 , . . e (.1 • loi• I': 0 ,•• . 74? . •. , . / la/ .._ 4 . , k 1: . ./. .._, 7. .• . • i 111/ \ (-, P .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>NEAT BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A NEAT BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>collar and silk stock tied in a smart butter- fly bow, should always be included in one's outfit. A blouse of this description will harmonise with any dress, and the leading note of colour in the toilette can be em- phasised in the colour of the cravat and waistband worn. A good and well-fitting waistband is a necessity for the success of the blouse, and it is worth while spending a larger amount than usual to obtain a really good pattern from which one can at home manufacture others out of odds and ends of silk or satin. So long as there are women who are forced to carefully consider ways and means so long will blouses of all kinds remain in fashion.</p>
        <p>The Darmstadt wedding.—The Royal Wedding at Darmstadt, which is fixed to take place on Wednesday, October 7th, will bring together a brilliant gathering of Royal personages, as the bride and bridegroom are re- lated to practically all the reigning houses in Europe. Prince Andrew of Greece is a nephew of the Queen, and Princess Alice, the daughter of Prince and Princess Louis of Battenberg, is a grand niece of the King. The young couple are equally nearly related to the Russian Royal family, and it is expected that the Tsar and Tsaritza will be present at the wedding. Queen Alexandra, and the Emperor of Germany will also pro- bably be among the guests. The ceremony will be according to both the Greek and Lutheran churches,ane the spectacle cannot fail to be an impressive and magnificent one. Afterwards the bride and bridegroom will leave for Athens, to spend the honeymoon in Greece. Marriage Statistics.—We hear so much about the dis- - .2.1 appearance of domestic life and contentment, that one Total 3 the part of modern man, to assume the responsibilities BOWLING ANALYSIS. of matrimony. The modern man is supposed to be so WARRINGTON. ASHTON'. busy, so wrapped up in himself, as to forget women, 0. m. R. W. O. M. R. W. manners, and social amenities, but the statistics of the Bailey Hewetson P. Kershaw 7 1 13 0 Horsfall of all we hear about a growing distaste for home life G. Radcliffe Kershaw bowled two no balls. Registrar-General's returns refers to the small number SANKEY WIRE MILLS V. WEAVERHAM.—PIayed on of widows that re-marry. They have been generally re- Saturday at Weaverham, and ended in a win for the garded as a designing class of persons, even long before visitors by 26 runs. Score : Mr. Weller was heard of, but statistics do not support WEAVERHAM. WIRE MILLS. I the theory that widows exercise a prejudicial influence W. Johnstone and b Woodcock lbw bW. _ over the chances of unmarried girls. Doody Moreton not out A. Johnston at H. Cook b Taylor c Holt b Gandy 8 at Gordon Castle, Banffshire, early next month, before Doody Catley run out 0 Gandy Gandy c Clarke b Doody 0 Turton cA. Johnson b lis not the home of the 'Cock o' the North" the proud Holt c Doody b Parton 2 W. Johnson °„ name of the hereditary head of the Gordon clan. This Woodward c Turton b HawthornbCatley ° title belongs to the Marquis of Huntly, who is descen- Woodcock' 8 Doody b Catley 0 ded on the male side from the old Dukes of Gordon. A Portman b Doody P. Johnson c Taylor b R. Cook run out 13,.. the Duchess, who is supposed to have kissed the Gordon Woodcock 3 Bell b Catley ,s 2 Highlanders into the King's service, secured the estates, Moss run ont F. Johnson cN. Cook b in favour of thepresent Duke some twenty-five years Woodcock 0 , ago, though the Marquis of Huntly also claimed it. It Extras ' was thesecond Earl of Huntly who built Gordon Castle some four centuries ago, the great feature of which is the quadrangular tower of six stories which still remains. All the rest has been rebuilt and extended. The great building has a frontage of close upon 600 feet, and it would accommodate a hundred guests, or more, without any inconvenience. Harvest Celebrations.—The custom of holding harvest festivals in the churches, has this year been the subject of some comment among those engaged inagriculture, some of whom think there is little to be thankful for. But bad as things are, it is seldom that they are so bad that they could not be worse, and even so, one would not like to think of abandoning the festival service. It is practically all that we have to take the place of the old-time harvest customs which began with the first-sheaf feasting, and wound up witha,the harvest home, and general rejoicing over the ciMipletion of a common task. The revelry and good fellowship amongst village classes, at harvest time, has almost entirety dis- appeared. The old fashioned harvest home now exists only in rural tales, and old-time pictures, and with its decadence has gone one of the most pleasing concep- tions of rural life. Dangerous Fashions.—Among other inconveniences of the huge picture hat, is its liability to take fire, especially when secured by a celluloid hat pin. An occurrence of this kind happened only recently in a London shop, when a lady wearing a large ostrich feather hat, went quite unwittingly too near an open flame, happily with no worse result than a dispute between the customer and the shopkeeper, as to who should pay for the damage. It recalls the fact that it was not only ugliness and inconvenience that killed the crinoline, of which there is some talk of a revival, but the risk of fire had also much to do with its disappearance. The crinoline was at the height of its popularity in the sixties of last century, and was then a common cause of disaster, both of falls, and of deaths from burning, as , the spreading out of the clothing greatly increased the risk of catching fire, especially in those days of open *grates and ranges. The Crinoline.—The hooped skirt hails from Paris and Vienna. It is not, of course, the hideous bell- shaped inflation of past generations' but the skirt is partially held out with whalebone and as the tendency is always to exaggerate fashionable models, it is of more serious import than any of the crinoline scares of recent years. Fdrtunately we are much too sensible now to be much afraid of having such an incumbrance thrust upon us by the leaders of fashion, and the hooped I skirt, so far, is really only part and parcel of another revival that of the dolman, or enormous mantle capes that are worn this winter. The hooped skirt is intended to continue the lines of these somewhat stiff and heavy looking garments, instead of leaving the dress to hang flat, and look as if there was something lacking. Nothing , however, can make such a garment look reallyneat and graceful. It is what it looks, a sacrifice both to appear- ance and comfort, which, one may hope, will prevent it becoming the mode this winter. Old Fashions Revived.—There have been rumours for! some time past of a revival of the jersey, which was so I popular some 20 or 25 years ago. We should be sorry to ; see the blouse ousted from its premier position, but no one would object to the return of the jersey, which is both a comfortable, and to a good figure a becoming I garment. It is an American revival, and the new jerseys instead of being close fitting are fastened down the front' or back, with small felt buttons. They can thus be put on and taken off without disarranging the hair, a great convenience if the jersey once more returns to popular favour. The Servant Problem.—lt is to be feared that middle class mistresses, in want of a good and respectable ! servant, will not find much" light and guidance " on the subject from the London newspapers. One declared I with cheery enthusiasm that the solution of the servant I problem was to be found in importing maids from Norway. They were faithful, honest, hardworking, and i willing to come and do the entire housework for mod- erate wages. It is just such a girl that hundreds of mistresses are in want of, but unfortunately another journal proceeds to cast ridicule upon the prospects of obtaining in a treasure, and says that the servant i ' difficulty s just as acute n Norway as ever here, and folds of silk defining the termination of the even if the Norwegian girls could be induced to come, yoke. From between the points of the lace they would be greater failures and disappointments than springs an accordion-pleated frill of silk. those we are already familiar with. One is inclined to 'The sleeves are partially tucked, and the sorrowfully admit that the latter view is likely to be the more correct. Since Norway has become a popular fulness of the bodice is caught in at the holiday resort, some Norwegian girls have taken situ_ waist by a folded band. The neck is ations here to learn the language, so as to obtain better rounded out and is collarless. wages in the hotels of their own country, but the num- • The smart toilette illustrated is of navy ber so far is very limited, and even those who come do blue serge, with the skirt cut walking lengt:;, not stay more than a year or two, and a plain little tight-fitting Eton coat. The hat is a French sailor of a cerise colour, tilted over the face by a lsandeau at, the back RUNCORN SHOP ASSISTANTS. covered with twists of ribbon the same The shop assistants of Runcorn have decided to es- tablish a branch of the National Amalgamated Union of colour as the hat. The blouse worn under- Shop, Assistants, Warehousemen and C:erks. The Union neath the trim little coat' is of white lawn has 280 branches and a membership in the United King- spotted with blue and finished at' the throat dom of 13,000. Sixteen members were enrolled and by a dainty muslin collar and smartly-tied seven transferred from other branches, a total of 23. It cravat, while the waist is finished by a ceri :e- -was decided to hold two meetings monthly, alternately coloured leather belt. For immediate and at Runcorn and Widnes. The objects of the Union are cOntinuous wear nothing could be smarter- to secure earlier closing in all shops, adequate and de- finitely fixed meal times, proper supervision of the sani- looking than this toilette, whether for the tation and ventilation in shops, offices, warehouses liv- town, country, or seaside. It also lends itself ing and sleeping apartments provided for emplo4s, a readily for variation, as by substituting limitation to 14 of the age of young persons employed another hat of less pronounced colour than in shops, abolition of unjust fines, fair payment for the red and wearing a more dressy blouse services rendered, the gradual abolition of the system and belt, together with the addition of a of boarding and lodging of employe, and legal aid to d ainty aplapceearaoncoemucosloinld collar he alotre ruffle, altered. N Navy be memrs where necessity arises in their relations with whole employers, blue in light or dark shades is a very fav- ourite material at this time of the year, although cloth of a smoother and light make is also much in evidence.</p>
        <p>The neat blouse sketched is of white viyella patterned with groups of large black spots. The box pleat down the front is piped with black taffetas, and fastens down the centre with pretty enamel studs, while the neck is finished off with a French muslin collar and a well-tied cravat. • Enamel or jewelled studs are a valuable possession, for they transform many a simple skirt into one of distinction. A blouse nowadays is an article of dress which requires respectful and much consideration, and the time has long gone by when it was viewed more as a means to wear out an old skirt to its end. No it has to fit the figure .neatly and in some instances</p>
