        <p>DAMAGE TO PROPERTY AT WESTON PORs.:T.</p>
        <p>Thomas Carter, of the narrow boat Amy Jane, Middle- with, was summoned at Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday with doing wilful damage on the 6th inst., to property belonging to the River Weaver Trustees, at Weston Point.—A man named HarrylPain appeared, and said defendant was unable to be present, and bad asked him to represent him and to plead guilty.—Mr. Rosen- berg, agent It Weston Point for the River Weaver Trustees, said that considerable damage had been done to the property there from time to time. S 'me three or four weeks ago one of the weighing machines at Marsh Lock was smashed. It was tho practice of youths to go there on Sunday afternoons and throw flints at the cargo boats, barrows, itc. It had been going on for a long time, and he was instructed by the engineer to take steps to prevent it. The company did not wish to appear severe, but an example should be made in this case.—P.C. Rowlands said he saw the defendant throw- ing stones at a wheelbarrow. He spoke to him and defendant replied that he did not know he was doing any harm. Witness had had numerous complaints about this sort of conduct.—The Chairman (Mr. W. Timmins) said it was a serions matter for the company to have their property damaged in this way, and defendant would be fined 4s. and lls.costs, or go to gaol for 14 days.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
