      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WHY THE CLOCK STOPPED.</head>
        <p>WHY THE CLOCK STOPPED.</p>
        <p>The clock at No. 1, Priory-street, Ware, (Hefts). had been stopped. Why? Simply because the ticking of it was maddening to Miss Clara Day, who lived there, and who was just racked with headache, and prostrated with biliousness. Chas. Forde's bile beans have now restored her to health, and in gratitude for her cure she has told the whole story of her agony, and her relief, to a repre- sentative of the "Hants Guardian." "From the age of fourteen," she said, "I suffered from violent headaches and frequent bilious attacks. Morning after morning I would wake up with my head aching most terribly. Then I would turn very sick, tremble violently, and be covered with perspiration. After this I would shiver with cold, and my head would be so bad that I could not bear to hear people speak to me. I lost all my colour and became quite yellow, in consequence of liver disorder which was the real cause of the biliousness. Attacks like these occurred every two or three weeks, and would last a whole day. Sometimes I used to go out feeling quite well, but come home again very ill, so sudden were the attacks. I have even had to stop the kitchen clock, because I could not bear the noise it made. Till I was 23 years of age I also suffered a great deal from anmmia. I consulted many doctors, who did me no good what- ever. I tried all sorts of pills, medicines, and every remedy I could get hold of, without my sufferings being at all relieved.</p>
        <p>However, one fortunate day T read about Chas. Forde's bile beans for biliousness, and how similar cases to mine were permanently cured. I procured a supply, and soon found myself greatly relieved. The sickness and headaches came less frequently, then stopped alto- gether. lam now quite cured, and never know what it is to have a headache or an attack of biliousness. This I owe to bile beans." Theymay be had of all chemists, at ono and three-half-pence or two and nine.</p>
      </div>
