      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>EVEIUTS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>EVEIUTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN IN DENMARK</p>
        <p>At present Queen Alexandra is paying her tsual autumn visit to her. father, King Christian of Denmark. There is no more united royal family than the Danish, and at least. once a year they make a point of all meeting under the paternal roof at Fredens- borg Castle. The only thing that has of late years marred these essentially family gather- ings was the death of the Queen of Den- mark three or four years ago, which leaves a big gap in the circle. At Fre- densborg the life is of the simplest, far simpler indeed than in many English country houses,and there is very little formality or stiffness. Yet, with all this, and</p>
        <p>OUR QUEEN'S FATHER, RING CHRISTIAN OF DEN- MARK, WHOM HER MAJESTY</p>
        <p>despite the poli- tical insignifi- cance of Denmark itself, Fredensborg has been many times of recent years the neutral and unofficial centre of European diplomacy owing to the important connections by marriage of the Danish royal family. For instance, it has been the strong connecting link between England and Russia when diplomatic rela- tions have otherwise been somewhat strained. The Queen has in this way always been able to keep up a close and warm friendship with her sister, the Dowager-Empress oi Russia' who makes a point of meeting her each autumn at their old home. The King and the Gzar, also, have often spent many quiet, unofficial days together at Fredensborg. Queen Alexandra, who went over on the Victoria and Albert, was unfortunate in a rough crossing, but was met at Copenhagen by King Christian and the other members of the family. It is always a great rest to her, as there are practically no Court functions at this time of the year. The "season" at Copenhagen is from the beginning of February till the King's birthday, April Bth—short and simple, though bright and gay. Since the Queen's death, however, it has missed an official leader, as the Crown Princess is a very seriously minded and deeply religious woman, who will attend neither dances nor theatres. Nevertheless, the King gives many entertain- ments, including Court balls, at the Palace. One peculiarity of the Danish Court is that black is not permitted. People in mourning have to wear white. But the Queen's visit is apart from this life of the Court—it represents the life of the family, which collects for a thorough domestic holiday from all corners of Europe.</p>
        <p>IS NOW VISITING</p>
