      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>EXTRAORDINARY LEBAUDY.</head>
        <p>THE EXTRAORDINARY LEBAUDY.</p>
        <p>M. Jacques Lebaudy, son of a French multi-millionaire sugar refiner, takes himself seriously. No one else does. He .inherited, with his brother Robert, more than twelve millions sterling from his father, and with this money he has enjoyed to the full every pleasure that Paris and Europe can provide, and now he has turned his attention to founding an empire amidst the wastes of the Sahara. His life has been one long revel of eccentricities. He is probably one of the most unhappy men</p>
        <p>LEBAUDY PROCLAIMING HIMSELF EMPEROR HIS NAVY IS HIS YACHT, FRASQUITA.</p>
        <p>OF THE SAHARA</p>
        <p>in Europe, for his millions are a burden to him, and he spends his .life in trying to get rid of them in the most extraordinary manner. On lartling at Cape Juby, on the north- west coast of Africa, he immediately pro- claimed himself Emperor of the Sahara. The district where he landed is one long, series of sand hills and wastes. He and the fifteen men who accompanied him, armed with old revolvers and antiquated guns, had not long been in the new kingdom before they were surrounded by a number, of Moors, who demanded by what right his Majesty had taken up.. his position there. His Majesty, finding hauteur was not effective, tossed broad- cast franc-pieces to his inquisitive visitors. This for the time being had the desired result, as the Moors, with the francs in their pockets, graciously bowed to his Majesty, and looked for more gifts from the Imperial chest. How- ever, his Majesty was the first to get tired, and returned to his yacht without making much impression. Incidentally, his Majesty left five of his sailors on the shore. These were captured by the Moors, and rescued by .a French man-of-war, which bombarded the Moors encampment at the expense of the 'French Government. The country which he has annexed is sparsely populated considering its enormous area. The inhabitants are .about two million souls,' and consist of Moors, Inereg, Tibbn,- Negroes, Arabs, and •Jews. His Majesty thinks that this population needs the leaven of Europeans, so he has invited sc.x) Breton and Corsican families to kingdom. has ordered a crown, means to make a railway across the desert, and has eN-en, got -so far as choosing the uniform for his •army. He has alS'o engaged 24 - priests and 20 nnnti to at tend to the 'spiritnal needs' .of the empire.</p>
        <p>KING PETER'S LONDON REPTILSENTATIVE.</p>
        <p>Such an event as recently .took place in Servia :naturally has many far-reaching consequences. One of the is naturally the necessity of ',changing the country's diplomatic representa- lives, as the Minister of the assassinated King AleNancler can hardly continue to represent his successor, King Peter. To Lon- don, therefore, there will shortly come a new Servian Minister, in M. Alexander Z. Yovitchitch, who is already well known in the Metropolis, RS he has pre- viously held the position of Charge! d'affazres in this country. His selection is considered a wise one, as he is one of the few Anglophiles in Servia, which M. ALEX. YOVL is in the main , strongly op- posed to Great Britain, and is very popular over here, where he has many fren.l3. indeed, Madame Yovitchitch is a Scotswoluan of a well-known Edinburgh family, and :licir Children have received a thoroughly English education. Their eldest son is a lieutenant in the Servian army, and was educated at the Servian Military Academy. With M. Yovit- chitch and his wife will come their four daugh.</p>
        <p>ters, who will be well fitted to take part in and enjoy English life and society: King Peter's selection is thus in every way a wise one. His Excellency M. Alexander Yovitchitch is a man of many parts, and has lived almost the whole of his life in the atmosphere of foreign politics and diplomacy. Upon leaving school he studied law, completing his educa- tion at German universities, and for a while he practised as a judge. Then he entered the diplomatic arena, and represented his country in turn at Rome, Vienna, Athens, and London: He returned to Belgrade after the marriage cf the late King Alexander to Queen Draga, and was appointed Chief of the Department in .the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, a post he now vacates to return to London. M. Yovitchitch is a man not only of conspicuous ability, but the utmost probity, and he is equally respected for his discretion and his straightforwardness. He is a man of strong religious sentiments and marked literary tendencies. He should, there- fore, be well fitted to serve his country success, fully in the sphere of 'Western civilisation.</p>
