      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>DISAPPEARING DICKENS'S LONDON.</head>
        <p>DISAPPEARING DICKENS'S LONDON.</p>
        <p>Another bit of Dickens's London is doomed. At the south-eastern corner of Exeter Street and Wellington Street is an old house, with a comparatively new front, which of late years was incorporated with the Gaiety Theatre, and bears over its bow window—the successor of a humbler window of the same type—the words " Gaiety Theatre. Lessee and Manager, Mr. George Edwardes."- Our artist's drawing, made from the Western side of Wellington Street, shows the corner, the original wall and windows of the old house at the side, in Exeter Street, and the newer front towards Wellington Street. This house was once the office of "Household Words," the magazine founded by Charles Dickens in iB5O, and it</p>
        <p>was here that he was constantly to be found during his later years. Mr. Percy Fitzgerald, writing in the Times, says of the house that "it was, in the sixties, a charming little editorial sanctum, daintily designed and ele- gantly furnished. The bow-windowed drawing- room was quite boudoir-like. In fact, the Master always used it as a sort of bachelor home when he had to stay in town. How often did my eyes, and the' eyes of other postulants travel to that magic bow window, behind which, as we assumed, the great man was enshrined." The bow window of the editor of "Household Words" is not the more imposing but far less magical bow window of the manager of the Gaiety Theatre, but it is interesting to know that the later window itself, now doomed, kept to the traditional form. Here, before the Gaiety had absorbed the house, and after Dickens and "Household Words" had left it, Dr., now Sir W. H. Russell, founded and edited the Army and Navy Gazette. All about here, indeed, were, and still are, a number of offices of magazines and newspapers, and even a hundred years before Dickens and his followers settled iv the district it was a literary quarter.</p>
