      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>BRITISH ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>THE BRITISH ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The British Association has just held a very successful annual meeting at Southport, under the presidency of Sir Norman Lockyer, the great astronomer and well-known Director of the Solar Physics Observatory at South Kensington. His presidential address fol- lowed the old lines of impress- ing the need of increased atten- tion to science: but for a change he reduced his ideas to a prac- tical and con- crete form. "Let £24 000 , 000 , " he urged, "be set apart from one asset, our national wealth, to inci ease the other national asset, brain power." What we require, in Sir Norman Lockyer's ;IR NORMAN LOCKYRh, THE opinion, arc NEW PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>more universi-</p>
        <p>ties and plenty of them, and not only as many as Germany or even the United States, but more still. These modern, up-to-date, practical homes of higher education are what help to bring the advance of science to bear upon the commer- cial details of everyday life, and give America and Germany the pull over us in many indus- tries. ' To raise this immense sum Sir Norman Lockyer would duplicate the Navy Bill of tBBB-9, and "do at once for brain power what we did so successfully then for sea- power." It was a loan, he concluded, which would bear a high rate of interest. Mr. Bal- four, the Premier, was elected President of th 3 Association for next year, and the meeting will be held at Cambridge.</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>RUSSIAN NAVY.</head>
        <p>THE RUSSIAN NAVY.</p>
        <p>Russia continues to pursue a vigorous naval policy, which has nowadays practically become a necessity for a big Power. Whatever their strength on land, no first - class Power can afford to neglect its Navy, as Germany has of late years found out. England owes its great.</p>
        <p>THE SLAVA, AS SHE WILL APPEAR WHEN</p>
        <p>FINISHED</p>
        <p>ness to the fact that it discovered this politi cal fact some centuries ago. Recently under the personal auspices of the Czar and Czarina the latest Russian battleship was launched from the Baltic shipbuilding yard at St. Peters- burg. This great frond-ad, which vas christened the Slava, is a sister ship to the Alexander 111. She has a displacement of 13,600 tons, and is 376 feet in length and 76 feet in the beam. Her engines are of 16,000 horse power, and she is designed to be up to 18 knots an hour. She is armed with four 12-iFich, twelve 6-inch, twenty 3-inch, twenty 3-pounders, and six I-pounders. At the same time the Czar launched the new Russian Imperial yacht, the Alexandria, which did not impress experts so favourably. She is a small ship built on unpleasmg lines, and was in an unusually advanced condition when launched, her two funnels being actually in position when she took the water. She is not a screw steamer but a paddle-wheel vessel. After the launchings had been concluded the Czar and Czarina inspected the latest submarine tor- pedo boat which has just been added to the Russian Navy after highly satisfactory trials.</p>
