      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 19, 1903.</p>
        <p>—Warrington, 7 points; Hull, 2 points. RUNCORN v. BROUGHTON RANGERS</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, before 5,000 spectators. Teams : Runcorn: Houghton, back; Jones, Butterworth, Price, and Dutton, three-quarter backs; Jolleyand J. Richard- son, halfbacks; S. Walker, J. Walker, Woods, Poole, A. Richardson, Moulton, Langley, and Tomlinson, for- wards.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers: Williams, back; Widdeson, Harry, Wilson, and Hogg, three-quarter backs; S. James and Barrett, half backs ; Woodhead, Warwick, Bonn, Scott, Templeton, Beetham, Ruddick, and Vowles, forwards. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. W. IVlcCutcheon, Oldham. Touch judges, Messrs. A. Jones, Warrington, and A. Critchley, Widnes. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>• Runcorn won the toss, and Scott started for Brough- ton. Jolley returned to Hogg, and scrummaging ensued. Jolley, Price, and Butterworth gained ground for Runcorn, but James found relief. Richardson made an unsuccessful drop at goal. Resuming,Price knocked forward when he had a splendid opening, and James, Barrett, and Widdeson brought temporary relief with a round of passing. Then Jolley got possession, and dropped a goal. A faulty kick by Houghton gave the visiting forwards a chance, and the ball was dribbled to the Runcorn line, where scrummaging ensued, but Houghton relieved. Runcorn againattacked, andJolley getting possession dropped another goal. Resuming, the Rangers were on the attack. Butterworth, how- ever, cleared his line, and the ball being sent to Hough- ton, he dropped a goal almost from midfield. Half- time score :—Runcorn, 3 goals (6 points), Broughton Rangers nil. After the interval Walker started for Runcorn, and Broughton were forced on the defensive, but Wilson and Barrett relieved. Passing by Jolley, Richardson, Price, and Butterworth ended in the latter knocking forward. Houghton effected a fine clearance a moment later when surrounded by his opponents James broke away with a neat run, but was stopped by Houghton. The Broughton mm t retired hurt, but resumed play. No sooner had he re-appeared than Benn was carried off, being hurt in a general scramble. Just before the finish Price picked up, and easily passed the visiting custo- dian, scoring a fine try, Jolley adding the goal. Result: —Runcorn, 4 goals and 1 try (11 points) ; Broughton Rangers, nil.</p>
        <p>the place of Davies, and acquitted himself very credit- certain. But they obtained recompense immediately ably. afterwards as Edmonds dropped a grand goal. One last Hunslet Dressed at the start and Leeds were fortunate chance Hull hid, but they failed to take the opportunity. to escape Dickenson kicked a penalty goal. Hull made a last a score against them. Then Evans relieved by a strong handing-off run, but Place was equal to the despairing effort, and Parry was almost over, but once occasion when tested. Hunslet rushed the ball up the again the weakness of the halves was apparent. War- field, and Albert Goldthorpe all but dropped a goal. rington again attacked, and play got rough, two cautions Again Leeds fought their way to the Hunslet 25, and being necessary, but the finish came with Hull well beaten. some smart passing by Jenkins, Mosley, and Grace nearly produced a try. When half-time was called Final neither side had scored. , In the second half Hunslet speedily pressed, and Stead was injured and did not resume. Weakened by his loss the Leeds pack were forced back, and, at length, from a scrimmage nearly underneath the Leeds posts, the ball was left by the Hunslot pack for Albert Goldthorpe to pick up and promptly pop over the cross-bar amid the cheers of the Hunslet partisans. After this Leeds were a beaten side, and from a scrim- age near the line Albert Goldthorpe passed to Jackson, who dashed over by the touch flag with a try. The place kick failed. Soon after, Hannah secured the ball and, making light of the opposition, ran over. The kick at, goal was again a failure. Nothing further was scored, so that Hunslet ran out winners by one goal two tries (8 points), to Leeds nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WIGAN v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>WIGAN v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Central Park, Wigan,before 7,000 people. Wlgan—Sharrock, back; Green, Ball, Wilcock, and Rouse. three-quarter backs; Anderson and Brown, half backs; Ellison, Wolfenden, Collier, Whittaker, Aspey, 3. Brown, Hatton and Halliwell, forwards. Widnes—Tilley, back; Peacock, Brown, Whelan, and Barber, three-quarter backs; Lalley and Taylor, half backs; Aspey Lynch, Leyshon, McDonald, McCabe, Power, Webster, and Cochran, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. A. Squires, Broughton Rangers, Touch judges, Messrs. Lee, Leigh, and Hancock, Runcorn. In the early stages the contest was waged mainly in the scrummages, but ultimately Widnes carried the encounter to the Wigan "25," where as the result of an infringement in thepack, Tilley had a favourable oppor- tunity of opening a score. The effort h'owever, was futile. Later on Rouse, for Wigan got away, and almost reached the goal line. With these exceptions even play ruled to the interval.</p>
        <p>In the second half Widnes attacked in vigorous fashion, but were beaten off, and the home fifteen suc- ceeded in keeping hostilities in their opponent's quar- ters Rouse and Green making several unsuccessful attempts to penetrate, but Widnes- defended in fine style, and time arrived with nothing scored. •</p>
        <p>LEIGH v. BATLEY. At Leigh, before 2,500 spectators. Burgess, who had recovered from his injury received whilst play- ing against St. Helens, re-appeared at half for Leigh; but Oakland, who had been placed on the Batley team, did not turn out, Midgley playing instead. Leigh faced the wind and slope in the first half, and Batley attacked strongly in the first ten minutes, William Davies and "Wattle" Davies both making ineffectual. attempts to score. Sutcliffe tried a drop at goal, but failed, and then brilliant passing by the Leigh backs forced Batley back. Leigh swarmed round the line, and for several minutes the defenders had to exercise great vigilance, Neville, Bennett, and Johnson being repeatedly stopped just outside. At length,Batley were penalised for off-side, and Molyneux dropped a goal for Leigh. The interval arrived just afterwards, with Leigh leading. Thesecond half opened in desultory fashion, and for twenty minutes or so little of interest transpired. Then Leigh started, passing brilliantly, and Boardnaan scored near the posts, Molyneux improting. Johnson next got away, but was tackled. Eventually, after more smart work by the Leigh backs, Johnson gave to Neville, who scoredat the corner, and with a splendid kick from the touch-line,Molyneux recorded a goal. Result :—Leigh, 4 goals 2 tries (12 points), Batley nil.</p>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS. These teams met at Fartown, bef,•re 6,000 spectators The only change in the selected teams was Reed, for Barry, in the Rovers' ranks. Sutcliffe kicked off against the wind, and the visitors had the best of the opening stages. The home backs indulged in a fine round of passing, which carried play to midfield. The Rovers again pressed, and Guy and Reed made repeated attempts to break down the home defence. Reed got possession and transferred to Gordon, who scored a fine try. Starks failed at goal. The visitors kept up the pressure and Reed essayed a drop at goal, the ball twavelling wide. Half-time: Kingston Rovers, 1 try (3 points); Huddersfield, nil. Starks re-started, and Walton was prominent with some fine kicking. Thomas mulled badly, and Reed kickeil the ball down the field. Gordon followed up smartly, and scored in a good position. The same player added the extra points. The homesters then had a spell of attacking, and Wimpenny was all but over. Holroyd was tackled on the line. Dewhirse passed to Wimpenny, who was only pushed into touch in the nick of time. Result: Kingston Rovers, 1 goal 2 tries (8 points); Huddersfield, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HALIFAX V. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>HALIFAX V. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Halifax in fine weather before about 8,000 spectators. The visitors were at full strength, but owing to injuries, neither Morley, the captain,nor Joeley,the cup final ball-back, could play for Halifax, and their places were taken by Nettleton and Gledhill. The visitors early gained ground by some smart passing bouts, Dixon making several openings for those behind him. After a pass from Dixon,PhilThoznas broke through. He and Spottiswoode had only Little to deal with, and the latter scored somewhat easily near the posts, and Ferguson landed a goal. This was the extent of the Oldham lead at half-time.</p>
        <p>• On the resumption, Oldham were pinned on their own line for some time, but they maintained a stubborn defence. At length R. Thomas relieved, and afterwards Halifax were seldom dangerous. Result: Oldham, 1 goal, 1 try (5 points); Halifax, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SALFORD v. REIGHLRY.</head>
        <p>SALFORD v. REIGHLRY.</p>
        <p>The visit of Keighley to Weastel on Saturday drew together 8,000 people. In the visitors' forward line Senders came in for Gellings; while Mitchell, the old Hunslet player, was included among the three-quarters. Lomas started for Salford, and for atime Keighley well held their own. From a fair catch near the centre by Jaggax, Walker kicked a fine goal. Soon afterwards Lomas scored an unimproved try for Salford; while before the interval Norris twice, from passes by Thomas, crossed the line, and Lomas from the places landed the goal points. Half-time: Salford 2 goals 3 tries (13 points); Keighley, 1 goal (2 points). On resuming, the home side pressed, and Dan Smith dropped a good goal. Thomas followed with a try, which Brown failed to improve upon. Salford had much the best of matters, and although Keighley drew Sanders out of the pack to make a fifth three-quarter, the Reds were persistent inattack. Jaggar and Mitchell made two good Put vain attempts to break through for the visitors. In succeeding play Bedford twice scored for Salford, no goals accruing from the place 3. Result: Salford, 3 goals 6 tries (24 points); Keighley, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>BRADFORD v. SWINTON.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>There would be between nine and ten thousand spectators to witness this match at Park Avenue. Swin- ton had the advantage of the wind in the first half, and were not long in attacking. There was some warm work in the home "25," but eventuallyMarsden relieved with a strong run. Swinton were 60011 again on the attack, and Booth sending the ball to Cooper, the latter ran along the touch-line, but Ward brought him to the ground before he really got dangerous. Then Booth sent the ball to Valentine, who dashed for the line, but he also was held up. Forward rushes and runs by Mars- den again relieved, but a wild pass by Rees gave the visitors a scrimmage on the home goal line, but the forwards cleared and carried the ball to the centre. Marsden then got possession and ran to the. visitors' "23 "where he passed to Sinton, who scored a try: At half-time the position was: Bradford 1 try (3 points), to nothing. On resuming, the Swinton men made a better fight of it than had been expected, and indeed were often in the home quarters. Preston had a shot at goal without success, and after Bradford had made a dead ball,Mars- den started passing to Handford, who sent the ball to Mosby, and the latter to Sinton, who scored a second try. .Mosby failed at goal. Later the visitors got to the home goal line, but had to retire without making points, and at the call of time the position was: Brad- ford 2 tries (6 points), to Swinton nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>LEEDS v. HUNSLET.</head>
        <p>LEEDS v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>Leeds openea their home season with a match against their rivals from Hunslet. There was a heavy shower shortly before the match, but the weather fortunately faired, and a great crowd, numbering upwards of 14,000, gathered at the Headingley ground. Leeds had to do without Llewellyn and Davies, both of whom were on the injured list, Davies having damaged his knee-cap, playing against Broughton, on the previ- ous Saturday, Mosley again appeared in the three- quarter line, and Smithson, from the second team, took</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <p>F_- •</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BEAT HULL.</p>
        <p>Warrington made the long journey to Hull on Saturday,and won asreditable victory. It was the first appearance this season of the Hull team atthe Boulevard, and there would be an attendance of at least 8,000 when operations commenced. Warrington played as selected. Hull had C. Lewis (three-quarter) and Fulton (forward) away, the positions being filled by Johnson and Sedg- wick, two A team players. Teams : Warrington.—Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dicken- son, and Brooks, three-quarter backs; Davies and Edmonds, half backs; Boardman, Morrison, J. Swift, Naylor, Cook, Thomas, Edmondson, and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>Hull.—Rodgers, back; Parry, Morgan, Cook, and C. H. Johnson, three-quarter backs; Hall and Goddard, half backs; Lewis, Harrison, Longhorn, Sedgwick, Ellerington, Sowerby, Carlisle, and Rhodes, forwards. Referee, Mr. T. Kennedy, Broughton. Touch judges, Messrs. H. Tomes, York, and W. Emmott, Leeds. Warrington won the toss, and had a strong breeze and the sun behind them. J. Lewis opened for Hull from the Hessle-road end. No reply being made,scrums were formed near the half-way line. Rhodes was injured, but was soon able to resume. Hull gained possession -from the first two scrums, but the passing machinery was badly out of order, and the visitors gradually en- croached on the home preserves. Rodgers badly mis- fielded a kick by Fish, which left the visiting forwards with a clear walk in, but they muffed the chance, and Goddard was enabled to save—a most lucky escape for Hull. Cook kicked to Hallam when u pass to Johnson would have been more useful, but the visiting custodian kicked too hard, and the ball went dead. Warrington' attacked strongly, but, amidst great enthusiasm, the Hull forwards broke away, and Parry picking up and passing to Morgan, a score seemed certain, but Morgan failed to gather a bad pass. Although the home forwards gained possession very frequently, the halves had not got "wound up," and the attempts at passing were woefully weak. Play had so far been entirely confined to the Hull half, but a rapid change came over the game, and passing between Goddard, Morgan, Cook, Johnson, and Hall saw the latter reach within an ace of the coveted crease. Scrimmages were formed in the corner, and a penalty gave Warrington relief, but not for long, as Parry, gaining possession from Morgan, 'lade a dash- ing run down the touch, but force of numbers prevented him from getting over. Hull attacked hotly, but Hall passed wildly, and another chance was lost. Pretty passing by Hull backs saw Cook in possession, and, beating all but the full backs, he passed to Johnson, but the pass was bad, and the A temper failed to gather. Quarter-time: Hull, nil; Warrington, nil. The weakness of Hull at full back was painfully ap- parent, and Rodgers let his team down repeatedly. A wide pass by Edmonds to Fish saw the visitors' crack wing gather finely, and beat Parry in grand style, but, foittinately for Hull, he stepped into touch when he appeared dangerous. Weak kicking by the Hull backs gave Dickenson and Isherwood a glorious chance, and a high kick by the latter looked like materilising until Langhorn rushed up and knocked dead. Rain now descended, but fortunately it soon cleared. A run and cross kick by Johnson raised the enthusiasm of the spectators, and Hull once again reached the home quarter. NVhilst play was very open, it cannot be said that it was highly interesting, as the lack of finish by the backs was painfully apparent. A rush by the visit- ing forwards, supplemented by a dash by Isherwood, sent Hull back, and severe pressure was again brought to bear upon their line. After Johnson had saved from Brooks, Fish was well stopped by Parry, but the homesters were unable to relieve the pressure. Hull had now the misfortune to lose Rhodes, who had to leavethe field as the result of an accident, and their chances (never bright) were not improved. Scrum after serum was fought out near the home line, and ultimately Edmonds getting away initiated a passing movement, which resulted in Fish scoring, but the same player failed to convert.</p>
        <p>The restart brought no relief to Hull, as Dickenson ran into the Hull half before being stopped and the Hull line was again threatened. Edmonds and Dicken- son again combined, but Rodgers this time effected a splendid save, only a minute later to knock on near his own line. From the ensuing scrum, Davies got away but his pass to Dickenson went awry and Hull again escaped. Half-time :—Warrington, 1 try (3 points) Hull nil. .</p>
        <p>The three points lead which the visitors enjoyed at half-time was richly deserved, and did not fully repro- sent the difference between the two teams, as Hull had been almost continually on the defensive. The loss of Rhodes for half the time was a most serious blow to them, and the Hull supporters were relieved to see him return at half time.</p>
        <p>Warrington re-started, and kicking by the backs sa w Fish reach touch near Hull's 25. Passing by Edmonds and Fish further encroached on the Hull preserves, and after Hull had missed a pass from Goddard, the latter kicked into touch. The relief gained was bat temporary as the visitors were soon swarming around the Hull line. Edmonds broke away from a scrum, and reaching Rodgers passed to Isherwood right under the shadow of the posts, but the latter knocked on, and Hull were again lucky in escaping. There was no doubt as to the superiority of the visitors, and it was marvellous how they failed to augment their score. For a change play by easy stages was taken to the other end, and Hull enjoyed the pleasure of being on the attack. • Rodgers was loudly applauded for two smart pieces of play,but the visitors were not in difficulties long,and soon raised the seige.Rhodes had at this stage once again to retire,be having to be assisted from the field. Smart play. by Goddard almost put Johnson in,but apenalty immedi- ately afterwards saw Hull rub off two points by means of a good kick by Goddard. Although one short, Hull played gamely, but they failed to make headway, and the visitors once again forced them to their own quarters. Three-quarter time :—Warrington, I try (3 points) ; IlulLi goal (2 points). AlThnugh the Hull forwards were playing fairly well, the backs were far from satisfactory. If they passed it was slow and badly judged, whilst their kickting was always straight to an opponent's open arms. At all points of the game Hull were well beaten. Warrington would have been most unlucky not to have gained two points. Warrington, by means of a good rim by Fish, again reached the Hull line. A fruitless drop at goal gave the homesters relief. The relief was but temporary as the Warrington backs were again soon making tracks for the homesters' line, and Dickenson, after a grand rim, passed forward, otherwise a score would have been</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Original Cocoa, and a Speciality.</head>
        <p>The Original Cocoa, and a Speciality.</p>
        <p>Epps's being distinguished from all others by its invigorating nutritious qualities &amp;loci its delicious flavour. This Cocoa, con- taining as it does all the substance of the Cocoa Nib, maintains its leading position . after three-quarters of a Century as</p>
        <p>_ C _ • Ae- ''• • • ta , •</p>
        <p>the best form or Cocoa for every-day use.</p>
        <p>Second Division.</p>
        <p>RESULTS TO DATE.</p>
        <p>For Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts. Rochdale H. 2... 2... 0... 0... 4 7 29... 0 1— 3... 4 St. Helens 2... 2... 0... 0... 4 8— 32... 1 1— 5... 4 Barrow Castleford 2.- 2... 0... 0... 2 2 10... 0 1— 3... 4 Millom 9 9 0. 0. 6 7 33... 4 2 14... 4 York 9 1. 1. 0. 3 1— 9... 0 1— 3... 2 Waketi'dT'y 2... 1... 1... 0... 3 5 21... 2 3 13... 2 Brighouse R 1... 1... 0... 0... 0 1— 3... 1 0— 2... 2 So'th Sh'lds 1... 1... 0... 0... 0 1— 3... 1 0— 2... 2 Holbeck .. 2... 1... 1... 0... 2 3 13... 1 4 20... 2 Pont efracl 2... 1....1... 0... 1 3 11... 3 5 2L.. 2 Normanton 2... 0... 2... 0... 5 1— 13... 2 14— 46... 0 Lancaster 2... 0... 2... 0... 0 1— 3... 3 3 15... 0 Dewsbury 2... 0... 2... 0... 1 1— 5... 4 8— 32... 0 Bramley - 2... 0... 2... 0... 1 0— 2... 3 5 21... 0 Morecambe 2... 0... 2... 0... 0 0— 0... 3 3 15... 0 Birkenhead 2... 0... 2... 0... 0 0— 0... 1 5 17... 0</p>
        <p>SUMMARY OF RESULTS. Pontefract liolbeck Normanton 4 1 11 Millom Rochdale Hornets 1 2 8 Lancaster . .... Barrow 3 2 1 Wakefield Trinity 2 1 t St. Helens Brighouse Rangers 0 1 3 York 1 0 2 Castleford -- _ BIRKENHEAD v. ST. HgLENS.</p>
        <p>Though he-.7y ran ha:Cl-fallen-a-t-liirke-r&amp;a:Ci,rendering the turf very heavy, there was a capital attendance for the match. The game opened in favour of the visitors, Liversags scoring a try for them after a lengthy spell of attacking. After change of ends, play was of a more even description, but, although Birkenhead had plenty of chances,- they failed to profit by them, and suffered a defeat by one try to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>SUMMARY OF RESULTS.</p>
        <p>Swinton A Warrington A 2 7 25 St. Helens A 0 1 3 Oldham A 1 10 32 Werneth 0 0 0 Wigan A Brght'n Rangers A 3 3 15 Runcorn A</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" TEAM'S VICTORY AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" entertained St, Helens " A " at Wilderspool on Saturday, when about 2,000 spectators had an opportunity of witnessing a match under the new ' twelve-a-side conditions. The visitors had the assist- ance of a slight breeze in the first half. Play was fairly, even for a time, but eventually Jordan receiv- ing the ball from a scrummage passed to Mahoney who after eluding several opponents gave to Townson, that player scoring near the corner. The kiok at goal failed. The visitors pressed but the home team was equal to the occasion and fine work by Fairhurst, Swift and Forster ended in Jackson adding a second try. Forster added the extra points. Half-timearrived with Warrington leading by eight points to nil. The open- ing of the second half saw Warrington early on the aggressive and Jackson scored in the first five minutes. The kick at goal proved unsuccessful. After this St. Helens showed an improvement and some clever drib- bling culminated in a score. On restarting the locals wentaway in fine style and Watkins after brushing aside several opponents had only to pass the full-back,but the Saints' custodian proved altogether too much for him. Play continued to go in Warrington's favour and Town- son serving Mahoney at the opportune moment the latter further increased the score. The kick at goal again failed. Jackson was applauded for a fine run nearly half the length of the field, but the visitors' full- back again averted disaster. Several scrimmages near the posts ensued and Jordan getting hold of the ball beat several opponents and notched a capital try. The same player failed at the goal kick. Near the close St. Helens gained relief by the aid of a free kiok, but the stay was only brief, and Townson gaining possession took play to the Saints' "25," where he served Mahoney who put on the finishing touch, and scored in such a position that Jordan had little difficulty in adding the goal. Final: Warrington "A," two goals six tries (22 points); St. Helens "A," one try (three points.)</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS "A" v. RUNCORN "A"</p>
        <p>These teams mat at Broughton.before 3,000 spectators The visitors started and at once began to press,and early on Flanagan scored an unimproved try. This seemed to arouse the hone side, and hostilities raged in the visi- tors half, where I,severe 'scrimmaging took place. Upon the ball becoming loose neat passing by the Runcorn backs forced the Rangers to fall back. Bailey next pat inn capital run and almost scored, the visiting full-back saving by kicking dead. Soon afterwards neat passing between Horton, Parker, and :Salley ended in the last- named scoring a good try,Kiley landing a goal. Half-time: Broughton Rangers, one goal one try (five points) ; Runcorn, one try (three points). Upon changing ends Jewitt started for the Rangers, and early on Horton put in a brilliant run which enabled the home side to attack. and a moment later Kiley dropped a goal. The Brough- tonians again forced !matters, and Kiley breaking away passed to Parker who scored, Kiley failing at goal. Inglesfield next scored for Runcorn after a long run, the same player failing at goal. Following thisJewitt scored forthe Rangers from a pass by Kiley, the last- named kicking a goal. Final: Broughton Rangers, three goals three tries (15 points) ; Runcorn,two tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>•ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>•ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>FROGHALL ALBIONS V. WARRINGTON- ST. LUKE'S. These teams met on the ground of the latter on Satur- day. The Albions were first to attack, good work by H. James and Inglis ending in the latter shooting wide. Ashcroft got away on the left, but was cleverly robbed by Leah. The latter player then made a tricky run down the centre, and giving to J. Barton that player opened the score for Froghall. The homesters then broke away, but a strong kick bylVotterill gave relief. L eah shot in and struck the goalkeeper, and from the re-bound Foster scored. Then the Saints did a bit of pressing, and after Clay had saved from Bibby and .Ashcroft he was beaten with a low shot from Evans. Half-time: Albions, two goals; St. Luke's, one. The Saints went away with a rush, but onlya fruitless corner resulted. The visiting forwards, headed by Leah, broke away, and from a melee in front of goal Leah further increased the lead. Froghall were now having matters all their own way, for immediately afterwards, from combined play between Forster, James and Inglis, the latter scored another. Just before the finish Leah got possession and made no mistake with a long shot. Final: Froghall Albions, five goals; St. Luke's one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>PILES CURED BY Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills</head>
        <p>PILES CURED BY Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills</p>
        <p>PILES are the result of imperfect circulation of the I blood. Venoui blood settles in the veins of the rectum, I and expanding these veins, produces the nodules known as PILES. Such a condition is often the outcome of an inactive liver, which does not permit the blood to returnfreely to the heart. Often it is the result of a weak action of the heart itself. Sometimes it is duel to large masses of feces lodging in the bowels, which, pressing upon the veins, keep. the blood from returning freely to the heart and lungs, PILES are not only most painful, but they are very dangerous. The inflamed nodules are very apt to take a malignant action. What are known as BLEEDING PILES are most to be dreaded. They are an aggravated form of the disease, which often results, if left alone, in cancer of the rectum, and are amendable to the knife only. To ensure ft permanent cure, the blood must be put into good condition. Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills do this; and if you suffer from PILES you will' find in them a cure, the same as Mr. Thomp- on, of 1, Ferndale, Nottingley, Yorks, did. It was his wife who told the story of his recovery to perfect health. "My husband," she said, "suffered from PILES for 25 vears.-For weeks at a stretch he could not work. At night it was impossible for him to lay in bed; by day no rest could be obtained. Remedies enough to fill a house,were takenbut they one and all failed to give relief. Doctor after doctor was sonsulted without effect. It was im- possible, we weretold, for him to ever recover without the aid of an operation. He underwent the ordeal at the Leeds Infirmary, but our hopes of a cure were doomed to disappointment. The hospital proved useless The pain, if anything, was worse, and my husband once more had to lay up at home. At last we bought a bottle of Dr. Morse's Indian• Root Pills. They com- pletely cured him, and ended his 25 years of agony." Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills, a perfect blood purifier, cure liver and kidney complaints,biliousness,indigestion constipation, piles, dyspepsia, foul breath, sallow com- plexion, female ailments, eczema, pimples and blotches. Soldby leading chemists and stores, price is 1-.ld per bottle. If unable to obtain where you reside, they may be had on receipt of price from the W.H. ComstockCo., 21,Farrington Avenue,London, E. C. FREE SAMPLE. Mention this paper and enclose ld, stamp for postage.</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>SUICIDE'S PATHETIC LETTER. -- Could not be like Other People. DROWNED IN THE BRIDGEWATER CANAL.</head>
        <p>SUICIDE'S PATHETIC LETTER. -- Could not be like Other People. DROWNED IN THE BRIDGEWATER CANAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, conducted an in- quest at the Warrington Police Station on Wednesday afternoon touching tbe death of Sarah Alioe I3arker,(3o,) a single woman living at 7. Robson street, whose body was recovered from the Bridgewater Canal at Latchford on Monday morning. Jane Jennings, 6, Robson street, said she had known the deceased for the past three years and last saw her alive at noon on Saturday when she appeared to be in her usual health. Deceased had been very quiet and strange in her manner for some time.</p>
        <p>Detective Barlow (coroner's officer) said he had taken the statement of Margaret Barker, deceased's step mother, who stated that she last saw deceased alive at three dclock on Sunday afternoon when she left home with the intention of going fora,walk.She:did not return and the following morning her body was recovered from the canal. For a number of years deceased had suffered from pains in the head and had been very despondent. She had on several occasions talked of drowning herself, but witness took no notice of her. William Deakin, who resides at Manor Lock, stated that at 5.30 on Monday morning he was in the vicinity of the canal and was informed that there was a body floating in the water. With assistance he recovered the body and at once recognized deceased as being the woman he had seen walking on the canal bank at seven o'clock the previous evening. Acting-Sergeant Curiliffe said he examined the body but found no marks of violence. The following note was in the deceased's dress pocket which had been pinned up "I cannot live any longer the life I live. I am not happy nor never will be, as I don't do and be as I ought to be. I have wished myself dead a good many years as I cannot be like other people. I cannot think as I should do. My mother has always been good and kind to me and it has always been me that has been at fault. I hope she will not trouble about this, as I _can-</p>
        <p>, "tit net &amp;alp it, and meet wi someone to look after her better than Ico d do. I want her to have my money and bel gings." A verdict of "Suicide during temporary insanity" was returned. ,</p>
        <p>, In reply to a juror tl e Coroner said the wish ex- pressed! in deceased's let er in regard to her belongings was useless from a legal )oint of view. • —la.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LIVERPOOL DISTRICT WESLEYAN SYNOD.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL DISTRICT WESLEYAN SYNOD.</p>
        <p>May Synod to be Held at Warrington. [ At the annual meeting of the Wesleyan Synod of the Liverpool District at Liverpool, on Wednesday, the [ Rev. T. Galland Hartley presiding, the following were [ appointed the new members of the District Home IMissions and Chapel Committee for the New • Con- nexional year :—Rev. C. B. M'Cullagh, Ralph Green, and James Lewis. The Home Mission grants in the district are: Mold, £3O; Carnarvon, £5O; Garstang, £6O; and Aberystwyth, £5O. Mr. J. Cory Monkhouse (Liverpool) was elected a trustee of the Central Hall, Manchester. The new members elected to the Foreign Missionary Committee were the Revs. Mackenzie Cobban and R. M. Gantrey, and Mr. J. J. Cook (St. [ Helens). Mr. Norval W. Helme, M.P. (Lancaster), was ; appointed the District Foreign Missionary treasurer, and the Revs. R. M. Gantrey and T. A. Turney, with Mr. Charles Pearson (New Brighton), were elected secretaries. For the great meetings of the spring anniversary, the deputation will be the President of the Conference, one of the General Foreign Missionary secretaries, and the Rev. William H. Hart, of Calcutta. Mr. J. J. Mack (Liverpool) was re-elected treasurer of the Children's Fund. The Rev. W. Russell Maltby was re-appointed secretary, and the Rev. F. Platt, M.A., (Colwyn Bay), and Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P. (Runcorn), were chosen new members of the District Education Committee. The Rev. H. H. B.A.,was re-appointed Probationers' Examina- tion secretary; the probationers' examination will be held in Liverpool during December of this year, and March, 1901. October 18th is to be observed as "Children's Sunday," November 2.9 th as "Temperance Sunday," and the first Sunday in October as intercession day for Foreign Missions. The Revs. G. Talalun Newton, and H. C. J. Sidnell, together with Mr. Batty (Southport) were elected to the vacancies on the district Temperance Committee, and the Rev. A. T. Kinnings and Mr. I. T. Holden were re-elected secretaries.</p>
        <p>The Synod unanimously endorsed a resolution de- claring that the number of licensed houses was far in excess of the needs of the population, and that legisla- tion was urgently demanded to cope with the evil. A protest was also recorded against the recognition of a vested interest in any licensed house beyond the specific time for which the licence was granted. The invitation of the Warrington Circuit to the Synod to hold its annual meetings there next May was cordially accepted. A special afternoon session of the Synod was held to consider the report of the Select Committee formed in May to dell with the question of aggressive work in town populations. This special session proved to be one of considerable interest, and several able addresses were delivered. In the end the Synod adopted the report, and earnestly recommended that the grave question should be brought without delay before the people of every circuit both in quarterly and special society meet- ings. The committee urged that opportunities for this wider workamong the people might be found in various directions, such as more open-air preaching. Better use might be made of the P.S.A. and Adult Bible Class movement to hold services on neutral premises, and to build up causes in connection therewith. The com- mittee earnestly recommended that the trustees of town chapels consider whether some modification of the sys- tem of pew mnts may not be made, and believe seat- holders would not object to an announcement being made that all seats would be free from five minutes be- fore the commencement of the evening service. The committee approves of the 'rigid rule of itinerancy being to some extent relaxed where special cause is shown and a minister permitted to continue his work beyond the three S'ears' term. The committee desire to emphasise the great opportunity which the Sunday schools offer for the extension of the work of God.</p>
      </div>
