      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ST. HELENS LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Transfers and Alterations.</p>
        <p>A special session for the transfer of licenses, and the consideration of proposed alterations to licensed premises was held at the Town Hall, St. Helens ota Monday. Mr. E. W. Swift (Messrs. Swift and Garner) made a formal application for the final transfer of the licenses of the Nelson Inn, Market Hotel, and Plicenix Inn, the three new houses erected in Bridge-street, and Canal- street in connection with the street improvements recently carried out bythe corporation. The whole of the applications for transfers were granted. The Deputy Mayor (Mr. Alderman J. Forster) in stating the decision of the bench, said that the magistrates wished to convey to those license holders, who were not devoting the whole of their time to the business of their houses, a'word of warning that they were expected to leave some really responsible person in charge when they were absent from their premises. Mr. E. W. Swift applied on behalf of Edward Fildes for magisterial sanction to proposed alterations to the Liverpool Arms, 107 Liverpool-road. He said that the owners, Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co., had recently acquired from the corporation a plot of land adjoining the house, and it was proposed to provide better living adcommodation for the tenant. The alterations, he explained, would inn° way increase the public drinking • accommodation.</p>
        <p>On behalf of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley. and Co. the owners of the York Hotel, Park-road, Mr. J. Hallam Fox applied for sanction to proposed alterations to that house. The alterations affected the domestic arrange- ments, and the plan had been approved by the health Mayor committee of the Corporation.—The Deputy, pointed out that the public staircase to the clubroom or the house led also to the bedrooms, and the magistrates did not like that state of affairs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fox also applied for sanction to intended altera- tions to the Queen's Head, Warsley-brow' occupied by Emily Alice Barrow; also to the Rolling Mill Tavern, Watery-lane, tenanted by Richard Leadbetter; and to the Cowley Hill House, 50, Cooper-street, kept by John Jolley.</p>
        <p>Similar applications wqre made by Mr. E. W. Swift in regard to proposed alterations to the Swan Inn, 88, Baxter's-lane (tenant, Thomas Leyland), to off-licensed premises at 68, Pigot-street, kept by Ben. Holt. The bench considered the whole of the applications, after which the Deputy Mayor announced that the mag,istrates had decided to grant all the applications with the exception of that referring to the York Hotel, which they refused. In regar.l to Cowley Hill House, the bench insisted that the alterations should be carried out in the manner suggested by the magistrates at the annual general licensing sessions. They asked that the alterations should be carried out at once as several months had elapsed since the licensing sessions.</p>
        <p>Owing to the gale at Scarborough, the ball on the Spa, arranged for the officers of the Channel Fleet anchored in the bay, had to be abandoned. On i Monday evening between six and seven n the evening Lord Charles Beresford signalled to the Mayor as follows "To our lasting regret, lam sorry to say it will be im- possible to land to-night. rcannot hoist out steam- boats, and pulling boats would surely get stove and the men damaged. The disappointment is bitter throliOonii the fleet that the weather prevented us coming to the splendid entertainments you were good enough to offer us."</p>
