      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>FOUND DEAD.</head>
        <p>FOUND DEAD.</p>
        <p>N4k—IANFORD'S POISON for KiDine Rats answers well. " After one dressing I found 135 dead next morn- ing."—J. Bus.. Helsthorpe, Leighton. "I killed 200 rats with Sanford' Poison."—e. Coox, Farmer. Ellesmere. " I destroyed 145 rats with it."—O, Vitt.soN. Offord.—Send le 2d to SANFORD &amp; SONS. Sandy St. Be. old by Cherniati</p>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR'S pamph- THE UNIONIST let must have been CONFUSION. inspired by the desire to</p>
        <p>clear the minds of his supporters, and restore at least some semblance of unity to his party. Its immediate effect rather tends to increase the confusion. True, the" Daily Telegraph" tells us that it is a " thoughtful and philosophic essay," and the "Morning Post" that it must be" helpful to the inquirer." But from the "Standard" we learn that it leaves the inquirer in a state of " exasperating uncertainty" as to the way in which retaliation can b ) employed. The " Times " dismisses this as a " subordinate but the " Stsadard " with a much keener perception of realities, points out that Mr. BALFOUR makes no effort " to show hov threats Of reprisals, or the actual execution of them, could be rendered effective," To the mind of any practical inquirer this is a rather serious omission, We do not need any instruction from Mr. BALFJUR in the mere generalities of the case. Everybody knows the theory which the Prime Minister expsuals —as the " Standard unkindly puts it—as if he were a "sympathetic teacher building up his class in the kilo aledge of the rudiments." The DUKE of DEVONSHIRE and Mr. RITCHIE must feel grateful for the philo- sophic freshness with which Mr. BALFOUR has invested such a well-worn theme. But they cannot feel mach wiser. Tae "Standard " suggests tl,at Mr. BA.LFOUR would "favour rather the diplomatic use of our ability to resent grossly unfair treat- ' ment abroad than any deliberate adoption of methods antagonistic to the true doctrine of Free Trade." Bat how can you employ this " dip;o- matic ability"without resorting to those methods? That simple question destroys the airy structure with which Mr. BALFOUR proposes to bridge the gulf between Free Trade and Protection. In analysing his-pamphiet the "Standard" sees this clearly enough; and yet, in a separate article, it tells the Free Traders in the Cabinet that they need not be alarmed. "Even the most orthodox Free Trader should be willing to countenance an inquiry to deter- mine whether; retaliatory powers should • be asked for, or, if asked for, whether they should be exer- cised." One inquiry has been conducted to a suc- cessful issue. It has she,vn that Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN'S elan will not do. R.)joicocl by this victory the ortho lex Free Trsd ars of the Ministry may settle themselves comfortably again in their res- pective offices, and apply their inquiring ininda to Mr. BALFOUR'S plan. "If this is clearly recognised," adds the "Standard," with unaffected earnestness, "we may hope that the fears entertained of Ministerial SBO33SiOUS and resi.gaati Ins will grad- ually disappear." On the other hand, the" Daily Telegraph" cannot See how these resignations are to be averted, now that Mr. BALFOUR has de- clared frankly for Protection. On one point there is agreement in this other- wise discordant chorus. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is thrown over. His friends have been telling us that he had virtually delivered an ultimatum to his colleagues. Taey must accept his policy or he would leave them. His policy is not accepted, and apparently he is not resigning but resigned. The "Standard"assures him that there is no reason why he should " take up a position of unbending anta- gonism "to this shelving of his views. Some day or other he may "bring them forward with some prospect of success, but that time is assuredly not yet." Meanwhile, the Government should return to their" normal business." If they must "differ over tariff theories , let them differ as private individuals only." A more remark- able conception of Ministerial responsibility has never been submitted to the public. It is not easy to define the functions of the Cabinet according to the Constitution, for the Cabinet is one of those things which have been grafted on our political system, and defy all legal interpretation. BIG it has hitherto been affirmed with the utmost solemnity that the Cabinet is collectively responsible for the opinions of its members on matters of national importance. The DUKE of DEVONSHIRE is personally responsible to ParliaEnent and the country for Mr. BALFOUR'S pamphlet. He cannot regard it merely as " a thoughtful and philosophic essay." He has either to subscribe to its conclusions or to withdraw from the Cabinet. To pursue a middle course on the plea that Ministers must dismiss from their minds "the exciting discussions, the too animated debates, of the past few months, and return to their normal business," is simply to trifle mith public opinion. When questions of this kind</p>
        <p>issue"</p>
        <p>Since the above was written, the break- up of the ministry has been announced. Thrne ministers have sent in their resignations namely :—The Right Hon. JO3EPH CHAMBER.. LAIN,M.P., Secretary of State for the Colonies; The Right Hon. C. T. RITCHIE, M.P., Chancellor of the Exchequer ; and the Right Hon. Lord GEORGE HAMILTON, Secretary of State for India. These gentlemen may be described as protagonists of two contending parties that have been dividing the Cabinet. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN stands for a preferential agreement with our Colonies, whilst Mr RITCHIE and Lord GEORGE HAMILTON represent Free Trade. It was impossible for any :self-respecting Free</p>
        <p>Trader to sit in. the same cabinet with a Prime Minister who has adopted disguised protection under the name of reciprocity and retaliation. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN thinks he can best promote the cause he has at heart from outside and will shortly commence his task of explaining and popularising those principles of Imperial Union which he feels convinced are essential to our future welfare, if not to our very existence as a commercial nation. Mr. BALFOUR is therefore left with a miserable remnant to form an intermediate party, which within a very short time must crumble to pieces and pave the way for a Ganeral Election.</p>
        <p>With the prospect of an early dissolution it behoves the Liberal party of Warrington to be prepared for the impending contest. On the last occasion Mr.A.EI. Crosfield foughta hopeless battle with only a four days' candidature. It was fought at a time when the war fever was at its height, and Mr. Pierpoint was returned with a majority of 1,165. Few would care to assert that the majority obtained on the khaki ticket represents the rela- tive strength of the political parties in the Borough at the present time. The Conservative party is now comparable to a three-headed Cerberus with Free Traders at one extreme, Mr. Chamberlain at the other, and Mr. Balfour occupying a middle position as the leader of a modified protectionist policy of reciprocity and retaliation. The Liberal party on the other hand has been cemented to- gether by the reactionary proposals of the Govern- ment and presents a united front against the encroachfnents of protection in its various forms. The Liberals have an opportunity of winning Warrington, such as has not presented itself since 1880. It is time that the Liberal forces were marshalled together in battle array and a vigorous and determined effort made to win the seat.</p>
        <p>The proposals which have been submitted by the directors of the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany for the acceptance of the Warrington Cor- poration and traders are of an important char- acter and deserve serious consideration. The river at Warrington is to be impounded and mate practically into a dock by the construction of a river diversion with a lock and sluices. The level of water in the river around Warrington is to be maintained at lift. 6in, above ordnance datum. For these advantages, the Corporation is asked to pledge its credit for the whole cost of the works, the interest and sinking funi to be re- paid by th Canal Company in yearly instalments until the loan is liquidated. On this point we can only say that if the Canal Company is as tardy in meeting its financial liabilities as it has been in carrying out its river obligations at Warrington, the ratepayers may find themselves called upon to pay the cost of this extensive undertaking. It is rather cool, too, to ask the Corporation to pay the cost of the diversion of the sewers which the scheme will involve. It will be news to many to learn that the Corporation has a new drainage scheme which it has undertaken to carry out at the request of the Local Government Board. Supposing this to mean that we are bound to undertake an entirely new system of drainage at an early date, the outlook for the ratepayers is serious indeed. The rates are already at an abnormal figure, and if we are to face a now drain- age scheme they :will reach a point which must imperil the future prosperity of the town.</p>
        <p>Warrington has long been noted .for its good football club. In the old days of the South West Lancashire Cup competition the Warrington men secured the coveted trophy and proudly brought it home amid the rejoicings of thousands of their townspeople. Since then times have greatly changed in the rugby football world and what was then a purely local team is now chiefly com- posed of men drawn from all parts of the country. This change, notwithstanding, interest in the game is greater than ever and the prospects of the Warrington team for the season just entered upon are being freely discussed. Much of the success which has been achieved is due to the ex- cellent management of the club and on Tuesday evening practical recognition was made of the ser- vices of one of its chief officials. For 21 years Mr. Thomas Pemberton has jealously guarded the finances of the club and its sound financial position to-day is the envy and dHsp dr of most clubs. It seems strangs to hear of such well-known townsmen as Alderman Bennett, Councillor Tinnion,Mr. J. E. Warren, Mr. Thomas Pemberton and Mr. E. England figuring in pants and jerseys on the same football field, bit they probably feel all the better:physically, to-day for having rubbed shoulders together in such vigorous and healthy sport. Mr. Pemberton ;is to be con- gratulated upon completing such a long term of service in a difficult and exacting position.</p>
        <p>Coroner's inquiries have beOn painfully frequent in Warrington of late and especially among the rising generation has death claimed victims when leAst expected. The details of the recent sad drowning cases are too well-known to need recapitu lation, but on Tuesday evening a particularly dis- tressing street fatality created a sensation in the town. Returning home from school, with all the life and vigour of youth, a nine-year-old boy was crossing Arpley-road, and, in running 'after some companions he unwittingly rushed into danger. He was knocked down by a horse and cart and killed almost instantaneously. Such accidents as these are terribly sad, and it is but poor consolation to the bereaved relatives to be told that so long as English boys retain their present vivacity fatalities are bound to happen. Still, all will agree with Coroner Brighouse in giving expression to such an opinion. It is indeed marvellous that more fatali- ties have not to be recorded and many parents, we are sure, have had Cause to he thankful for the providential escapes of their offspring.</p>
        <p>A lady who witnessed the accident attributed it to the narrowness of Warrington Bridge, but her view was not borne out by the evidence. Never-</p>
        <p>theless the increasing danger of this bridge with its congested traffic cannot be too forcibly brought home to the authorities'. It is as.onishing that so all-imfortant a questioa should have been lost sight of, for it seems to us that as time goes on and traffic increases it will become more difficult than ever to carry out this necessary improvement. The coroner himself testified to narrow escapes which he and others have ex- perienced on the bridge,and he told the jury that he was surprised that he was not holding inquests every week on people who met their death when crossing it. We are afraid that unless speedy action is taken, the Corporation will ere long be ' having a rude awakening to their responsibilities I in the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. C. Hawkin, the t:ecretary of the Eighty Club, has asked us to remind those of our readers who are on the Coaimittees of Political or Literary Societies of the woik of the Eighty Club. The club consists of a number of gentlemen willing to assist the Liberal cause by speaking at local clubs, debating societies, or pubiic meetings,as well as by lecturing on subj Acts of current political interest. No fee whatever is charged. In view of the Educa- tion Acts, the war report, and the proposal to tax food and establish a ZJllverein, the club is pre- paring to send speakers not only to large meetings but also to ward meetings and to series of village meetings.</p>
        <p>The harvest reports in the agricultural papers are dolorous reading. In many districts, as everybody knows, the corn is still in the fields, mildewing or sprouting in the damp air. And not only is carrying in arrears, but a great deal of reaping has yet to be done, under very unfavour- able circumstances. No wonder the farmer's heart sinks within him as he contemplates the work which must somehow be done, and on which it seems hardly possible to make progress. No won- der, too, that he should be more than ever con- vinced that agriculture is going to the dogs. I Unfortunately, it will not only be a very late and a very small harvest, but also a very expensive one, owing to the protracted character of the operations. Still, the work will come to an end some time, and at all events by the end of the month the farmer may congratulate himself that the earth has at least given him Borne return for the labour expended. Unfortunately also with stories of irreparable damage to grain come accounts of injury also to root crops. Mau- golds and other roots have had quite as much rain as is good for them, and badly need the genial influence of warmth and light which are commonly not wanting at this season; light yields are there- fore anticipated, and farmers are advised by the experts to resort to catch crops wherever possible. The bright spot is in the abundance of herbs go for grazing, though, of course, its nutritive value is not the sam3 as earlier in the season. It is cheerless harvest, in which the utmost hope pos- sible is that things may not turn out quite so bad in realisation as in anticipation.</p>
        <p>The movement for some State action in ' regard to the drinking of methylated spirit is growing. The reports which come from Scot- land show that the trouble is manifesting itself there, and among a class of people who might be expected to have self-restraint. The Labourers Fechration at Aberdeen has passed a resolution on the subject and there are other signs that the Chancellor or the Exchequer will soon be called upon to increase the duty on this alcoholic blend. It is a sort of appeal to which a Chancellor is unaccustomed, but one to which he can hardly be unsympathetic. It is a little hard if invalids and gentlemen who want quick shaving water are to suffer for the misdeeds of people who will get intoxicants meanly and cheaply in a more or less nauseous form. Perhaps a remedy might be found by some arrangement; which would require that the spirit should be supplied only in a more nauseous form. One of the remedies for drunkenness nowadays is the addition te 'ordinary alcoholic liquor of a drug which makes the beverage unpalatable, and then 1)1 suggestion rnakei all beverages which are simile in aprearance unwelcome. These drugs are crenor ally rather dear, at least to judge from the eS." pense of the treatment involved. But chemists should be able to make methyl ated spirit thor- oughly unpleasant,and people should be forced to pay the full duty for alcohol in its recognised forms if they want it. They should be made to feel that taking methylated spirit in not playing the gan3e.</p>
        <p>As the latest contribution to the literature of the " inquiry " comes a volume of statistics issued by the Bard of Trade shewing the movements of the foreign trade of the United• Kingdom and its rivals during the first seven months of the current year as compared with 1901 and 19P, Fiscal "reformers,"one imagines,will not find these figurc at all to their taste, for they tell quite a cheerful tale. Those who have been drawing dismal con- clusions from the fact that for a brief period the United States stood at.the head of the exporting countries of the world will perhaps derive consols.- tion from the circumstance hrought to light in this return that the United Kingdom has now re- covered its supremacy, and, for the first six months of the year, leads the United -States in point of value of exports by £1,000,000 sterling, instead of being £9,000,000 behind, as was the case in 1901. This testimony to the essentially healthy condition of British foreign trade Is, from the Protectionist standpoint, really most inopportune.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. F. Monier Williams, in a letter to the Press, says that children are a very great trouble to motorists. They sit as the sides of the road and dare each other to run across the bows of an on- coming cu.. AU motorists are aware of this per- nicious game, and it is wonderful that more accidents do not occur. A habit that is even more dangerous than the above says "Mooring Illus- trated," is that indulged in by small boys who hang on behind a waggon or cart. If a motor-car meet 9 a vehicle on to which a boy Li hanging, the boy 1s almost certlin to let go and run to the off-side, and if he avoids being run over by the motor it is El miracle. Possibly the extra noise made by the car when passing the waggon frightens the boy oft; at any rate, he always chooses the wrong momest in which to cast himself loose. The Duke of Devonshire knocked down a boy in this way 3 week or so back, and no , possible blame' pan be attached either to his Grace or the car.</p>
      </div>
