      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>have been deliberately raised, to say that they are no part of the "normal busi- ness" is ridiculous. What is Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN'S " normal business," if it is not to persuade his countrymen that the security of the Empire is bound up with the policy of preferential tariffs How can it be normal for the Cabinet to inquire into Mr. BALFOUR'S proposals and abnormal to I inquire into Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S? The truth is that these frantic efforts to keep the Unionist party together at any cost have reduced the "normal business of the Cabinet to a chaotic I muddle. They ought now to be applying their minds to some definite plan for the reform of the War Office. They ought to give us an assurance that the moral support of England will not be given to the SULTAN'S method of restoring order in Macedonia by extermination. But at Mr. BALFOUR'S invitation they have resolved them- selves into an amateur debating society, to dis- cuss the possibility of blending Free Trade with Protection !</p>
      </div>
