      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coining Events. Anniversary services will be held in Bold street Wesleyan church to-morrow (Sunday) when the Rev. J. Shaw Banks, D. D. ex-President of the Wesleyan Conference, will preach. The rev, gentlemen is theo- ,logic.al tutor at: Headingley college, Leeds and was President of the Conference in the year which ended in July last: The choir will render special music. The thirtieth anniversary of Wycliffe church Will be celebrated to-morrow (Sunday). The Rev. -John Yonge will preach morning] and evening and there will be anthems by the choir. The autumnal sermons will be preached at Hill Cliffe Baptist Church to-morrow (Sunday) by the Rev. W. Knight Chaplin, of London, the editor of the ChriStia,n Endeavour Times " and Secretary of the National Council of Christian Endeavour Union of 'Great Britain and Ireland. The rev, gentleman is .not only well known in the Baptist Denomination, but he is a man of much larger reputation, mainly through his connection with the Christian Endeavour move- ment, of which he is both the pioneer and the leader- At each service the choir will render special music, and there will be special collections in aid of the 'Church funds. Miss Richardson, clairvoyant and psychometrist, -will speak'at the Spiritualist Hall, to-morrow (Sun- day) and on Monday. The harvest festival will be held on Sunday the 27th inst.</p>
        <p>_ _ The harvest thanksgiving services in connection 'with St. Barnabas' Church will commence on Thursday •evening ,vhen the Rev. W. J. Gardner, of Tyldesley, will preach. The offertory will be in aid of the choir fund. An An open meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society will be 'held to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon when Mr. J. Walley, of Manchester. will deliver an address. Solos .and duets will be contributed by Mrs. A. Booth, Mr. A. Griffiths, and Mr. • W. K Wallington. .Personal. Sir Peter Walker has again rented the celebrated 'deer. forest of Affaric, in Inverness-shire, which com- prises about 32,000 acres. The sport is first-rate and the scenery is magnificent. The lodge occupies a beautiful situation on Loch Affaric. Earl Cadogan Was Sir Peter Walker's predecessor in the tenancy of Affaric Forest, which is on the Chisholm estate. "Time was" says "The World," "when golfers bound-for the Continent left their clubs at home, for golf-links abroad were few and far between. In these days, however, • there is scarcely a continental resort of any importance which is not provided with a clubhouse and course, to whose privileges visitors are admitted on moderate terms. The Championship of ,the Engadine was held recently on the pretty course at St. Moritz, which was the first laid out in Switzer- land. Mr. A. H. Crosfield; the original donor of the handsome Cup which rewards victory, was successful for the fourth time." •</p>
        <p>County Revision Courts. On Wednesday, Mr. C. E. Lloyd held courts at Orappenhall and Stockton Heath for the revision of the lists of voters. The Liberals were represented by Ur. Joseph Smith, of Knutsford, and the Conserva- tives by Mr. J. H. Dean. The Grappenhall polling district includes Grappenhall, Latchford Without, and that part of Warrington in the geographical County of Chester. Mr. W. H. Woodcock (assistant overserr of Warrington) and Mr. S. J. Woodbock (assistant over- Seer of Grappenhall) presented the amended lists. The business was purely formal. At Stockton Heath, the assistant overseers present were Messrs. Ellison and Bradshaw. The only item of interest was the fact that 89 new names were added to the list for Stockton Heath.—A court was held at Daresbury on Thursday- Warrington Girls in Trouble at Widnes. Two young girls named Annie Stockton and Mary Ann Littlefair, both of Warrington were brought before the Widnes bench on Monday .charged with committing a breach of the peace in Birchfield--road on Sunday. It appears they caine from Warrington ally, with two other girls to Widnes with four youths; arriving in the town about eleven o'clock, there being no train back that night. Police-constable Stubbs saw the whole of them quarelling, and took defendants into custody. Sergeant Ratcliffe gave evidence, and said that for moral reasons he kept them in the police station overnight because they had nowhere to go. They were hound over. The other girls, along with the youths, will be smnmoned also.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-C.:LISS ArISERA. WATERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Runcorn Grappling Corps.</head>
        <p>The Runcorn Grappling Corps.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner for South-West Lancashire, has sent a letter to Superintendent Erinion, of Runcorn, appealing for subscriptions for the Run- corn Grappling Corps, who require a boat to more efficiently carry on their work for grappling for bodies in the waterways of Runcorn, Warrington, and district. It was suggested by Mr. By-field, the captain of thncorps, that the boat should be called the Police Grappling Boat. Superintendent Ennion has written to the coroner saying he is quite in sympathy with Mr. RYfield, and the scheme was deserving of support,par- ticularly by the inhabitants of Runcorn, Warrington, and Widnes. Mr. Byfield would receive every assist- ance from the police. He had no objection whatever to the boat being named the Police Grappling Boat. GIVE TIIEM A TRIAL.—ALP. TAYLOR'S wonder- ful boots for everybody. Antpobus Lady's Death. b.The funeral of the late Mrs. Gough, widow of Mr. Ichard Gough, Ashwood Brow Farm, '..kritrobus, took ~ce on Friday afternoon in St. Mark's churchyard. be deceased, who died on the Bth inst., after a pain- 'l!i illness, was 77 years of age, and by a wide circle of friends she had been held in great respect. The itte, arners were :.—Mr. Walter Gough, Mr. Owen 7Pugh, Mr. Clement Gough, and Mr. William G-ough 'sons), and Dr. , Smith, of Weaverham (deceased's kedical attendant), in the first coach. The second Coach contained the brothers and sons-in-law of the ceased, and the third her nephews and several litiends. Messrs. John Hignett, Joseph Bolshaw, .}:ter Frith, and Richard Stubbs (nephews) acted as are. The service was impressively conducted by the Rev. C. F. Cross, vicar of Stretton. A large num- er of floral tokens wore placed over the grave.</p>
        <p>U. M. ASHTON'S LIPE ToNrc is the KING OF -LONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. tancashire and Cheshire Institutes. The annual report of the above union is a record of Pl'egress all along the line, and especially in regard "3 the examinations. These were held last year under the auspices of 228 institutions, and in respect °I 384 sepdrate schools. The applications for papers teached the high total of 55,751, being an increase of :1,589 on the number for last year. Along with the I,.aereased number of applications for papers, the 'Paprovement in the number and proportion of exercises I,?,resented to the examiners was more than maintained. -uhe number of individual students presented for ex- amination was 31,882 ;males, 20,227 ;females,ll,6s5tn ; increase of 2,662. The total number successful 44 One or more subjects was 20,494, being a percen- tage of 83.1. Owing to the great changes effected :by the. Education Act, 1902,the number of separate insti- tutions in association has been somewhat reduced. le roll now presents a. total of 405 such institutions ;Including 196 centres in the rural division of Liancashire. The membership of the institutions Which were in association during the past year was Over 178,600 with upwards of 139,700 individual stu- lents under instruction at evening classes. EST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—Advt. 'Success of Local Bands. ~ A brass band contest for Liverpool, Birkenhead and (istrict was held at the Eastham Pleasure Gardens on. ISaturday afternoon. The following were the com- hetitors Litherland, Tranmere Gleam, ..;„rosfield's, Penketh, Parr Temperance, Ellesmere Connah's Quay, Widnes - Subscription, Port `lnalight, and Widres St. Mary. At two o'clock the bands assembled in the gardens, and forming one bind played the fanfare, "God save the King." rttte testpiece- was "A Casket of Gems" (Wright and °l-111c1). The competition, which lasted four hours, ',as followed with much interest by a large number 413,i s,Pectators. Mr. J. 0. Shepherd, musical director the Court Theatre, officiated as judge, and in making been Very said generally. speaking the playing had very good indeed, and much better than he had vxPected to hear. The prizes he awarded as follows: St (u), and Penketh ; 2 (4), Parr Temperance; 3rd 4th (.-E2 and !,1), divided between Crosfield's and ranmere Gleam.</p>
        <p>Re-opening of Evening Scho Is. During the past week the evening schools in the various parts of the borough have been re-opened and large numbers of students have attended. The Lifeboat Institution..</p>
        <p>A further effort to augment the funds of this institution will be made on Thursday, October Bth, when a fancy dress ball will ba held in Parr Hall. Full particulars appear in our advertising columns. Technical Institute Classes.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column the evening classes for science, art, com- mercial, domestic and technological subjects, and the certificate classes for acting teachers will re-open on Monday next. The day classes in the Municipal Technical Institute (art, needlework and dressmaking) and the School of Art .(art subjects) will re-open on Tuesday neKt.</p>
        <p>Oddfellowship. At the "Prince of Wales" Lodge on Saturday last, a presentation of a framed emblem was made to P.G. Atherton for services rendered. The presentation was made by the Grand Master of the District (P.G.11. Ellis Clare), supported by P.P.G.M. Steele, P.D.G.M. Halford, P.G. Wadsworth, P.P.G.M. Massey P. G. Bradshaw, and P.G-. Freeman. During the evening a social took place and Bros. Freeman, Hal- ford, Atherton, and Qlare added to the harmony with great effect. P.G-. Farrar accompanied. Votes of thanks concluded a happy meeting. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDNa the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Athit.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park. Walker's Brewery Band will render the following selection of music to-morrow —Hymn ." Blessed Home," Stainer; march "Tiger," Greenwood; anthem "Blessed are they," Spohr ; selection " Crown Diamonds," Auber • oratorio "Elijah," Mendelssohn ; selection " Di Rohan " Donizetti ; " God save the King."</p>
