      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Warrington P.Sll.—Address on the Modern</head>
        <p>Warrington P.Sll.—Address on the Modern</p>
        <p>Outlook.</p>
        <p>A special open meeting of this society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street (which has been recently decorated), on Sunday, when there.was:la large attendance of members and friends. The chairman was Mr. W. H. Sherburn, who has recentlyTheen ap- pointed acting vice-president of thesociety. The Rev. J. F. Matthews gave an admirable address on, The Modern outlook "which was greatly appreciated' He described it as one of gravity and seriousness. Hav- ing referred to the findings Of the Royal Commission and the gross mismanagement of our affairs in South Africa, the trouble in the near East, the remarks of Mr. Chamberlain which led one to infer that the food of the people might be taxed, and the trouble in the religious world, consequent upon the articles in" the Clarion," he said he regarded these as a call, first to the combined Christian citizenship of the nation, and secondly, a call to the individual to use his talents, especially his vote as a stewardship directly conferred by God. Mr. R. Wood was the soloist, and efficiently sang the recit. and air. " If with all your hearts" (" Elijah ") and" Land of Hdme." The accompanist was Miss Wood. A collection was taken on behalf of the funds of the society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Junior Conservatives at Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>Junior Conservatives at Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>The pleasantly situated grounds at Grappenhall Fleyes were on Saturday the scene of a garden party and fete in connection with the Warrington Junior Censervative Association. The Mayor's kindness in placing the grounds at the disposal of the Association was greatly appreciated and the attendance during the afternoon numbered about 700. A capital entertainment was given by the Warrington Star Pierrot Troupe, under the direction of Mr. Alfred Grounds, and there were also selections by the Pioneer Band. Interesting sports were held and resulted as follows :—Sack race: 1, E. Davies • 2, J. Forster; 3, E. Gleave. Three-legged race • 1, James Daniels and Joseph Armitage ; 2, W. H. Dutton and J. H. Armitage. Boot race: 1, H. Rogers; 2, John E. Eaton; 3, J. W. Box. Tea was partaken of in d large marquee, Messrs. Woodcock Bros. having charge of the catering.—ln the evening a meeting was held, 'Mr. Roger C. Parr, president of the Association occupying the chair.—Mr. Pierpoint ALP., in the course of an address, observed that on a question which concerned Fair Trade and Free Trade he could not believe there were any laws of the Medes and Persians " which altereth not," and which said that no inquiry should be made into the question. Referring to the War Office, he stated that it needed a great deal of alteration, and if the policy propounded by Lord Rosebery of having Lord Kitchener to take the supreme command of the War Office could be realized, it would be a good thing indeed. (Hear, hear.) At the same time he questioned whether if the offer were made to Lord Kitchener he would be keen to accept it, because he had a great career behind him, and a still greater career before him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PARR HALL RE-DECORATED.</head>
        <p>THE PARR HALL RE-DECORATED.</p>
        <p>Elaborate Scheme.</p>
        <p>Since the closing of the Parr Hall on June 20th, a transformation has been effected in the interior of the building. Messrs. Hesketh and Son, Limited, have carried out an elaborate scheme of re-decoration and !Warrington may pride itself upon possessing a hall alike commodious and beautiful In appearance. The walls and‘the whole of the woodwork have been entirely re- painted, but it is in the decoration of the ceiling that the most striking change is noticeable. Artistic and elegant scroll work has been executed in gold and van- colours which harmonise with a brilliant effect that cannot fail to please the eye of the visitor. In honour of the donor of the build- ing the armorial bearings of the Parr family are neatly worked in delicate tints immediately above the organ, and over the back gallery appear the Borough Arms. The whole scheml is a splendid example of the decorator's art and reflects the utmost credit on the firm. The ornamentation of the balconies has been carried out in green and gold, and there is some exquis- ite fresco work round the walls. A very important improvement is the enlargement of tne platform for theatrical performances. There is now a 24 feet open- ing in the proscenium front .with 25 feet depth backwards. The stage effects have been pur- chased by the Corporation and are con- structed so as to be capable of being removed in a few hours. The increase in the size of the stage necessarily entails a reduction in the public seating accommodation. Consequently when theatrical enter- tainments take place the number of seats available will be decreased by something like 126. This, however, will be compensated for to some extent by the fact that. additional standing room will be provided. We would remind our readers that, by invitation, the members of the Warrington Dramatic Society will give the re-openin".b performances on September 30th and October Ist and 2nd. Mr. H. J. Byron's original comedy "Our boys," which the Society played in 1886, will be revived and successful presentments are con- fidently anticipated. It is worthy of note that since its foundation in 1847 the society has been the means of raising over £l,OOO for the Inftrmary.Other institutions which have benefited by performances are, the Museum, School of Art, Gymnasium, District Nursing Association, and the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. This year the proceeds will be I handed over to the Infirmary and District Nursing i Association, and we are sure that Warringtonians will be willing to second the efforts of the society to swell Ithe funds of such worthy institutions. We are informed that it is the intention of the society to play oftener, land a new play will be produced early in the new year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HOWLING.</head>
        <p>HOWLING.</p>
        <p>THE LOVELY LANE BOWLING CLUB. The closing main of the season in connection with the above club was held on Saturday last. The prizes which were very valuable were kinily presented by the fol- lowing gentlemen: Mr. A. H. Crosfield. Councillors. Jas. Evans and Barton, Messrs. J. T. and Wo Barlow,J. J• Crozier, M. Finnerty, P. Lawton, and N. Cook. The bowling throughout the day was very good. The fol- lowing were the results :--ist, Mr. Walter Saunders 2nd, !tr. J. S. Barton ; 3rd, Mr. John J. Whet • 4th,Mr. Henry Cook; sth, Mr. Thomas Throssell ; 6th, Mr. W. H. Dutton; 7th, Mr. W. Bennett; Bth, Mr. Alfred Dennett ; 9th, Mr. J. Wrench ; 10th, Mr. William Saunders 11th, Mr. J. H. Dutton.</p>
        <p>(RAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>"CORNFLOUR." ,</p>
