      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday September 19, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday September 19, 1903.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton with that handsome testimonial, which was intended in some slight measure as an acknowledg- ment of his servioes and also to convey the kindly feel- ings of all the members of the club. He could only express the hope that Mr. Pemberton would live long and enjoy the fall use of the gift and that the service would bring to mind the many happy years their Treasurer had spent in his very faithful and prolonged service. (Applause.) The present was than handed to Mr. Pemberton amid loud applause and the company sang "For he's a jolly good fellow."</p>
        <p>MR. P.EMBERTCiN'S RESPONSE.</p>
        <p>MORE ABOUT THE BEGINNINGS OF' THE CLUB. In accepting the gift Mr. PEMBERTON made the fol- lowinginteresting remarks :—My first introduction to football was through Mr. Alderman Bennett, in the year 1878. Mr. Bennett at that time was serving his apprenticeship to the late Alderman Davies, and had established a club at Padgate, under the name, of the Padgate Excelsior Club, its object being to promote cricket and football, and there was also a very fair library. I induced a few of my friends to join, among others being J. E. Warren Fair Turner, T. Walsh, Alfred Ratcliffe, W. Hough, and R. Andrews. We soon got to work practising football, and we had a trial match with some Warringtonians at Padgate. So far as I recollect the players from Warrington . numbered among them W. Lomas, E. England, W, , Edwards' George Clarke, H. Grounds, and D. Tinnion. After a few weeks' practice we ventured on our first match, playing the Grammar School, who were assisted by some of their old boys. In this match we were suc- cessful, our team winning easily, and among the scorers was Mr. Bennett, the secretary. For a season or two we struggled on. Mr. Bennett's father was always willing to place a field at our disposal, but we never were certain which field it was to be, and, consequently, when we went down on a Saturday to play, we 'very often found that the goal posts had to be taken from one field to another. (Laughter.) In 1880 we decided to move to Warrington, and took Massey's field in Sankey-street, where the Conservative Club now stands. Mr. Bennett resigned the secretary- ship, and I was elected to take his place. , Our headquarters were at the White Hart, Sankey-street and we received great assistance from the landlord, Mr. Walter Brierley. After playing a few seasons on the Sankey-street ground, and on the ground under the Three Arches, playing all kinds of clubs, wo induced the Warrington Club to give us dates, and were success- ful in beating them twice during the season. (Hear, -- hear.) Then came about the amalgamation of the It is no mean honour to be elected treasurer of the Padgate and Warrington clubs, and the new organiia- Warrington Football Club for twenty-one years n tion was further strengthened by two very promising succession, but at the same time arduous duties are clubs joining, namely the St. Elphin's and the entailed which call for ability and tact. In Mr. Wanderers. With them came T. Barnes, the finest Thomas Pemberton the Warrington Club possesses a three-quarter player of his day, and two sterling half- model treasurer who has devoted himself whole- ers. E ailbert end W Speekman During the llaatctkeiPleanyd of 1.883-4 the club 'got in a very precarious heartedly to the work, and it was therefore fitting that state.. Association had sprung up in `the town, when he attained his majority, so to speak, in the and some of oar best players went over. service of the club, tangible recognition of the occ.a- This made it vary difficult to carry out sion should be made. At the last annual meeting the our engaeements, but we still had some backbone in the members readily assented to the proposal to club, and'our old friend Mr. ‘Henry Ashton, with a present Mr. Pemberton with a handsome testi- few others, made a special effort, and saved the club , from disbanding. To put fresh life in the club all ' monial, and a movement to carry their wishes members were allowed to attend the meetings, and into - effect was 'soon set on foot. It was decided that the presentation should take the form of discuss the position of affairs. For a short time we were without a secretary, until the office was taken a beautiful silver tea and coffee service with silver- up by Mr. J. R. Calvert, who threw great enthusiasm mounted oak tray,and on Tuesday evening Mr. Pember- into the work, and having plenty of timeeind being very ton was the recipient of this gift. The tray bore the energetic, we again began to progress, and he completed inscrip