        <p>.. . ------ - - ----- ----- England. He had seldom, if ever, missed a committee : Mr. E. ENGLAND, in proposing a vote of thanks to the meeting, and he had regularly attended the matches in Chairman (Mr. Neil), Mr. Councillor Tinnion, and all order to take charge of the gate ,money. and attend to who had assisted in the evening's programme, said there other matters. Another matter which reflected great was no doubt that the club was excellently served by its credit upon Mr. Pemberton was the way he had officials. Amongst Northern Union Clubs, Warrington organized and carried out the duties of his was looked upon not only as being one of the best, but office, and particularly in reference to the gates. as one of the best managed in the Union. It. was He (Mr. Tinnion) had heard a great deal of grumbling evident from the status of the club and its financial outside Warrington, especially among Yorkshire clubs, position that it was well managed. He was only stat- in respect of gates. He did not wish to say anything r, ithe wishes of many Warriugtonians when he said against Yorkshire men but they would not be done out they would be very pleased if the club could get to the of a halfpenny. They came to Warrington with the head of the league. (Hear, hear.) He did not think it Yorkshire feelings of suspicion,but such was Mr.Petuber- was impossible. There was no doubt the club had a ton's method in checking the gates that they were at capital team. (Hear, hear.) Already, the form exhibited once satisfied thatat Warrington they were downright was capitalond if they could only go on in the way they honest and the visitors would have fair play. (Applause.) had begun they were bound to get to the top. (Applause) The fact that the club was noted for this among the Nfr. Timm AS BARNES, in seconding the vote of thanks, Northern Union clubs spoke very highly indeed for the said the dab was very fortunate in having Mr. Neil as management. He had extreme pleasure in presenting I president. It was a distinct advantage to have a</p>
        <p>F president who was able to find employment for players. He believed the club was destined to rite to a higher- position in the league than hitherto. The committee and players were determined to acquit themselves to the best of their ability. (Applause.) The resolution was unanimously carried. The PRESIDENT, in responding, said nothing gave him greater pleasure than to be connected with. the War- rington Club. The excellent manner in which the club was conducted added to • the pleasure of the position. Theofficial they had honoured that night was one of the very best. It was his (the President's) desire to see Warrington in a better position than ever before. (Hear, hear.) He hoped this year they would secure the cup. (Applause.) Councillor TINNION, also spoke, and thought that if the members of the club could 'meet occasionally as they were doing that night, it would tend to the wel- fare of the club..</p>
        <p>An enjoyable evening's entertainment then concluded. The following long telegram was received by Mr. Harry Ashton, from Mr. Calvert, of Southport, formerly secretary of the Warrington Football Club. "Regret unable to be with you this evening. Please convey to Pemberton my sincere congratula- tion on his long services and for the manner in which heis appreciated by the members of the club.. They cannot too much appreciate his services. The present position of the club has much to thank him for. Long may he continue to retain his position and members' esteem. Hearty wishes for thorough success of cInb.—CALVERT, Southport."</p>
