      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>APPOINTAIENT FOR_ A .RUNCORN INSPECTOR</p>
        <p>Captain Wade, port sanitary inspector, Runcorn, has been appointed manager of the Cattle Wharf at the §hip Canal Docks, Manchester. There were 300 applicants forth° position. Captain Wade commences his new duties next month. FAREWELL SERMON AT WIDNES. . _</p>
        <p>; The Rev. Father Melia assistant priest 'LC the Very. Rev. Dean Finegan, preached a farewell sermon on Sun- day, in St. Bedo's Church, Appleton, to a crowded con- gregation. The rev, gentleman leaves Widnes shortly to take over Ay chaplain duties in South Africa. On Wednesday evening some well-wishers of the Rev. gentleman gathered in St. Bede's Schoolroom; Widnes, to witness the presentation of £54 as a mark of esteem. Mr. John Caldwell made the presentation, and Dean Fidegan,Father Clarke, Dr. Donnelly, and Mr. W. R. Clarke eulogised Father Melia's estimable qualities. The recipient responded, and said he felt proud of the ,; occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LOCAL EDUCATIONAL MATTERS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL EDUCATIONAL MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The Evening Classes.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of ilia Warrington Education Committee will be held at the Town Hall on Monday evening when the following among other business will, be under consideration.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMEST OF SCHOOL KEEPERS.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommend that the follow- ing appointments of school keepers be made viz.:— Hamilton-street N., Mr. John it. Shaw, £75 IGs. ; Latchford N., Mrs. Racha,el Smith, £52; Latchford St. Mary's (R.C.). Mrs. A. Jefferies, £52; St. Barnabas,Mr. John Hill, £5B 135.; Silver-street Wesleyan, Mr. John Wood £75 175.; Warrington Parochial, Mr. John Taylor, 95 Gs.; Wycliffe British, Mr. W. H.' Winstanley £B3 Bs. The committee also resolved that the Director be instructed to arrange, in the case of schools which accept the terms laid down by the local authority, that the gas supplisd thereto shall be registered through a separate meter, and that there shall be a separate coal and wood store for theheating apparatus at the schools; that,with a view to the proper checking of the teachers' salary sheets, it is imperative that returns should be made weekly by the head teachers of the elementary schools showing the attendance of all the members of their respective staffs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SCHOOL</head>
        <p>SCHOOL</p>
        <p>M ANAGEM ENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee it was rekdved that consent be given to the following appointments, viz.: Heathside National, Miss Edith _lsabel Cann, assistant (certificated), £B5; Heathside National, Miss Annie Weeks, assistant (certificated), £80; Trinity National, Miss Elizabeth Cooke, assistant (certificated), £75 ; Trinity National, Miss Jane Milan, assistant (certifi- cated temporary), £80; Wycliffe British, Miss Helen Dowler, assistant (certificated) £B5; St. Mary's (R.C.), Miss Mary W. Marsh, assistant (certificated), £80; Sacred Heart (R.C.), Mrs. Annie Fay, assistant (Art. 50 temporary), £37 10s.; Warrington Parochial, Mrs. Sarah I. Price, assistant (certificated temporary), £B5. Other resolutions of this committee are as follows :</p>
        <p>That an advertisement inviting tenders for the supply of school materials, &amp;c., to the various schools be issued, and that a special Sub-Committee consisting of Mr. Alderman Monks, Mr. Councillor Craik, Mr. A. J. Jolley, and Mr. John Upson, be appointed to open and consider the tenders when received, and report to the Committee thereon. •</p>
        <p>That prizes be awarded, in connection with the even- ing schools, on the, result of work done during the session, and by means of test examinations to be held for the purpose (provided that 85 per cent of the possible nutaber of attendances have been made, and that the students have joined the classes prior to the Ist November) as follows, viz :—Technical school, Commercial school and School of Art, first year course to the value of 7s. 6d.; second year course to the value of 10s. : third year course to tile value of 155.; other schools to the value of ss.</p>
        <p>That the following appointments of teachers for the evening schools be made, viz :—Lecturers and teachers in the Technical school, Mr. Frederick G. Ruddock, chemistry, iron and steel, and metallurgy, £lO3 135.; Mr. Albert Hall, applied mechanics, steam, and geometery, £65 10s.; Mr. Francis C. Herzog, German and French, £52; Mr. William H. Cummins, machine construction, £46; Mr. Thomas Hughes, plumbing, £42; Mr. William IL Stanstleld, mathematics (elementary) and arithmetic, £42; Dr. Alexander Hodgkinson, physiology and hygiene, £33; Mr. George J. Brown, shorthand, .£2B; Mr. William Ogden, book-keeping,22B ; Mr. James Young, mathematics (Stage II.), £2B; Mr. John \ H. Fisher, Latin, £26; Mr. John T. Rerfoot, cotton spinning, £2l; Mr. C4sar A. ()ie.% Spanish, £18; Mrs. Mary Dobson, dressmaking, £l3; Mr. J. Swift, woodcarving, £l2 10s. for the session. Superintendents of other Schools :—At a salary In each case, at the rate oflOs. 6d. per evening, together with a bonus of 2i. 6d. for every student making 75 per cent of the possible number cf attendances :—Fairfield National School, Mrs. Sarah I. Price: Hamilton-street school, Mr. Frank H. LOngshaw : Heath Side school, Mr. John W. Parker; Warrington British school, Mr. Sydney W. Jeffery; Warrington Parochial school, Mr. Charles Hale; Wycliffe British school, Miss Leah Clay. That no child of suitable age be required to pass the examinationfor admission to the Secondary school who has already passed the Oxford Local (or similar) exami- nation.</p>
        <p>That application be made for the approval of the Board of Education for the adoption of a uniform education school year mall the schools in the Borough; and that such year do, for the future, subject to the consent of the Board of Education terminate on the 31st July. That the resignation of Miss Gillard, as assistant mistress at the Warrington Parochial (infants') sch,ml, be accepted as from the sth October, 1903. That all future notices of resignations of teachers be given at such time as to secure that tbe engagements shall terminate on the last day of the month.</p>
        <p>That consent be given to the appointment of the following teachers :--Ladies' School of Industry, Miss G. Walmsley,assistant (certificated), £75; Sacred Heart (R.C.), Miss Margaret Kean, assistant (certificated), £75 • Sankey Bridges Wesleyan, Miss Annie Medd, assisant (certificated), k S 0; Silver-street Wesleyan, Mr. W. H. Outland, assistant (certificated), £lOO ; Silver-street Wesleyan, Mr. A. F. Goulden. assistant (certificated), £100; Nycliffe British, Miss B.J.Wilson, assistant (certificated), £65 (pending result of certi. exam.) ; Wycliffe British, Mr. W. Hargraves, assistant (certificated, £lOO. That the salary of Miss Emily Plinston, certificated assistant mistress at the St. James' school, Latchford, be increased from £9O to 295 per annum as from the Ist April, 1903, on account of her qualifications and previous service. That a cookery mistress be engaged for the Fairfield (Girls') evening school,at a, salary not exceeding 10s. 6d. per evening. That the resignation of Miss Forbes as Art mistress at the School of Art be provisionally accepted and that an assistant art master be appointed in place of Miss Forbes and that an advertisemqnt be issued for the post, at a salary at the rate of £lOl per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OLOCAL DRUIDISM.</head>
        <p>OLOCAL DRUIDISM.</p>
        <p>Presentation of Certificates at Warrington. -- There were two interesting presentations on Saturday in connection with the Trafalgar Lodge of the War- rington District of the Order of Druids, Bro. W. H. Pusill and Bro. J. Callon receiving certificates of merit for their services to the lodge. The district officers took part in the meeting which was held at the Blue Lion Inn, Market Place. N.G.A. Bro. Shaw invited the officers to take charge of the lodge business, the District Grand Master presiding. Deputy Grand Master Bro. Bibby was in the vice-chair, and these two gentlemen were supported by Bros. Eden, district treasurer; D. Houghton. D.C.S.; William Bibby, ; Danbobbin, P.D.G.M.; T. Eden, P.D.G.M.; S. Packer, P.D.G.M.; E. Pointon, P.D.G.M.; and C. Bebbington, P.D.G.M. , The GRAND MASTER said it was evident the services Bro. Push had rendered were appreciated by all the district, as they had conduced very much to bring Trafalgar Lodge into its present flourishing condition. it was very encouraging to find a member who had been in office of one sort or another for over 23 years, and to still find himactively engaged. In asking Bro. Pusill's acceptance of the beautiful illuminated certificates he heped he would be spared a long time to look-upon it as a'token of regard and appreciation of his services by the district.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY GRAND MASTER, in making the presenta- tion to Bro. Callon, said he was very pleased it had fallen to him to do so, because they had been com- panions nearly all their lives. The pleasure was en- hanced by the fact that, he had merited this beautiful certificate from the district for his energy in obtaining so many young members. He heartily wished him health to continue in good work for many years. (Applause.) The D.C.S., in adding his testimony to the recipients of the emblems, gave a very interesting account of the lodge's history, pointing out that although 63 years had passed since its inception, it waa one of the best and young^st in its average age connection in the district. Bro. Pusm, and Bro. CALLON thanked the district for their presentation, and the officers for their kind remarks, and said the proceedings would be an incentive to them to do greater things if possible in the future. Several members contributed to the harmony of the proceedings by singing capital songs, and the grand old man of Druids, Bro. Dunbobbin, was warmly applauded for his rendering of two 'songs. Bro. Eden gave a recita- tion," The Lifeboat," which was well received.</p>
        <p>Bro. SHA.W, N.G.A., proposed a vote of -thanks to the district officers for -attending, and the D.C.S. for his Interesting address.—The Lodge Secretary, Bro. OULTRNM,seconded.—Bro. HOUGHTON, on behalf of the officers, suitably acknowledged tho vote, and said he hoped at a return visit to make similar presentations to other worthy members.</p>
        <p>...............,,,110........................</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SINGULAR ACCIDENT TO A BOY. Cartridge_ Explodes. _</head>
        <p>SINGULAR ACCIDENT TO A BOY. Cartridge_ Explodes. _</p>
        <p>A singular accident fiefel Percy Barnett, the nine year-old son of Police Sergeant Barnett. 3G _Marbury- street. on Wednesday afternoon. The boy was standing in Bridge-street when passers-by noticed him press his hands together and shout as if in agony. P.C. Cooper went to his astistance and finding that his hands were severely injured rendered first aid and took the lad to the Infirmary. It appears that several days ago Barnett had the brass part of a Lee-Metford cartridge given to him, and he intended to use it for a pencil case. The cordite, it was thought, had been taken out, but on Wednesday afternoon the boy was examining the cart- ridge in Bridge-street and noticed what he termed "a bit of white stuff" at the bottom. He was trying to pick this out with a hairpin when the cartridge exploded the whole of the cordite evidently not having been extracted. On being taken to the Infirmary, it was found that Barnett's left hand was shockingly injured, and his right hand was also damaged, but not so severely. It AWLS found necessary to amputate the second and third finger of the left hand and also part of the hand itself. Unfortunately the whole of the hand may yet have to be amputated as the injuries are of a minus nature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR. T. PEMBERTON.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. T. PEMBERTON.</p>
        <p>TWENTY-ONE YEARS AS TREASURER OF THE FOOTBALL CLUB Interesting Reminiscences.</p>
        <p>ASKED THE POLICE FOR A COPPER</p>
        <p>Thomas Shannon was charged at the Runcorn Police Court, on Friday, before Mr. E. Imison, with begging on Thursday evening.—Superintendent Ennion said the prisoner came into the Police Station and asked for a copper. He was searched, and was found to have in his possession a very complete tramp's outfit and Thd: in money. Asked where he lived, he said somewhere between Over and Winsford, but that one place was as good as another:—Mr. 'raison described the prisoner's conduct as impudent in asking at the Police Station for a copper wh .n he had money upon him. He committed him to gaol for seven days. DEATH OP MR. SAMUEL HANDLEY. An old Runcornian has passed away in the person of Mr. Samuel Handley, of 8, Albert terrace, Runcorn who died on Monday morning. He had been ailing for several months. The cause of death was heart disease. The deceased gentleman, who was a native of Runcorn, was 71 years of age. He retired from business some fifteen years ago. A shipwright by trade, he was at one I time prominently identified with the shipwrights' society. He was a member of a family whose associa- tion with the town goes back a great number of years. ' The funeral took place at Runcorn Cemetery yesterday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>DRUNK ON LICENSED PREMISES,</head>
        <p>DRUNK ON LICENSED PREMISES,</p>
        <p>, William Henry Cawley was summoned at Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday: for having been drunk on the premises of the Brunswick Inn, Runcorn on the sth , inst. Police-Sergeant Morgan said that he and Con- stable Preston visited the house and on going into the _ _ _</p>
        <p>bar parlour saw the defendant lying aslee-p fill length on a bench. Witness called the licensee in to rosse him. He tried but could not. Witness did so and when he got up saw that he was drunk. The licensee ad- mitted that he was drunk. He said he lodged at, the house. He had had no drink there, and he did not know he was in. The licensee's wife made a similar state- ment: Witness had satisfied bituself that the defendant , did lodge at the house.—Defendant pleaded guilty. He , said he made it a practice to go to sleep in the room. i He had had a drop of beer.—The Chairman said he had placed the landlord in a very serious position. He , would be fined 10s. including WAS. ASSAULTING THE POLICE AT WIDNES. I William Tarleton, of no fixed abode, was on Monday sent to gaol for five months for violently assaulting Police-constable Platt and Police-constabla ; Grayson on Sunday night in Ann-street. Prisoner re- fused to quit the Victoria Vaults when requested by the landlord, Mr. J. Woods. He was handed over to Police- constable Platt, who had a great struggle to get the handcuffs on him. Both policemen were struck and kicked, Platt having a damaged nose and Gray- son a skin abrasion on the face. The bench sent prisoner to gaol for five months.—James Broadhurst, of Catherine-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly, and with assaulting Police-constables Platt and Cleal in Elizabeth-street on Saturday.—Police-constable Platt stated that he saw prisoner drunk and disorderly, and was about to take him into cuNtody when he became very violent, striking with hisi,ll,fists land kicking /right and left. In Ann- street he calk d upon his companions to get him free, ! but instead of molesting the police they threw stones at ! them all the way to the station.—Superintendent Keighley, after reading a scandalous record, the last i conviction placing prisoner on the black list, said he i was one of the worst characters in Widnes. The stone- throwers were becoming a menace to the police, and in consequence two men had to be sent on these beats . , Instead of one. He hoped the bench would make an example of the prisoner.—Broadhurst was sent to gaol for five months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTION. Mr. Thomas Ellison having decided to retire from the representation of the North Eccleston Ward of the borough of St. Helens the Conservatives have appointed Mr. J. Phythian, of Liverpool-road, who has consented to be a Conservative candidate. THEFTS AT ST. HELENS. . At St. Helens, on Monday, Walmesley Naylor, of 7, Vincent-street, was charged with stealing a number of articles, including a necktie, pipes, &amp;c., frorn the 3d. bazaar, Church-street, on Saturday night. He was seen by P.C. Griffiths to take the articles from the door, and was now sentenced to one month's imprisonment. ST. HELENS FREE LIBRARIES. Mr. H. H. Lacey presided at the monthly meeting of the St. Helens Libraries Committee on Wednesday when the Librarian (Mr. Lancaster) reported consider- able increase in the attendances at the libraries and in the issue of books. The minutes with reference to the offer of Mr. Carnegie to give £3,000 for two branch libraries were amended, and the matter was left over to another meeting. SUDDEN DEATH AT ST. HELENS. Margaret Case, aged thirty-two, wife of a glass bottle maker, living at 5, Critchley-street, Park-road, St. Helens, was found dead in her house on Wednesday. The husband said that when he arrived home on Tuesday' midnight he found her asleep on the sofa. He went to bed, leaving her in that position, and when he rose in the morning he found her lying on the floor dead. He tates that she wassubject to fits. FRESCOT WORKMEN'S COMPENSATION. Mr. H.L. Riley appeared before his Honour Judge Collier, at St. Helens County Court, on Wednesday, and asked for approval of an award of 10s. 10d per week against the British Insulated and Holsby Cables Com- pany, Limited, of the Wireworks, Prescot, in favour of Fletcher Norman, labourer, of Rainhill, who was injured on March 10 last by a large wooden drain falling upon his left leg and ankle, which were badly cut. A defence had been filed to the effect that the particular part of the works where the accident happened was not a fac- tory within the meaning of the Act. This defence had been withdrawn, and the respondents had agreed to an award of 10s. 10d. per week from the date of the acci- dent until further order of the Court. HANDOCK COMPENSATION CASE. At St. Helens County Court, on Wednesday, before his Honour Judge Collier, the adjourned case of Joseph Rakitza, collier, of 121, Station-road, Haydock, versus Messrs. Richard Evans and Company, was in the list for hearing. Mr. Edwin Peace represented the company. Rakitza was injured on February 16th last when his hand MS jammed between the waggons. The man lost a finger and was otherwise uninjured. Mr.H. L. Riley, who represented the applicant, stated that a certificate had been given by Dr. Hayward that the applicant had recovered mid was fit for employment. He hadbeen paid £4 lls, before the proceedings were instituted, and it had been arranged that an award should be taken for a total sum of £ll is., and an agreed sum for costs. ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS. Thomas James Macdonald, of 28, Milk-street, was charged at St. Helens, on Monday, with attempting to commit suicifle by taking poison. The police said that the man went home drunk, turned his wife and children out, and was afterwards found bleeding from the mouth, with a broken bottle lying by his side. Prisoner said he had been drinking poison, and wanted to die. The constable procured an emetic, but the prisoner absolutely refused to take it. A doctor had to be brought to use a stomach pump. The young man's wife and children appeared in the court, and prisoner said he was sorry for his actions. He had been driven to it by drink. He asked to be allowed to go home, as he wanted to go to work in the morning.—The Magistrate: "You are not fit to go."—Prisoner: "Yes I am, sii."—The magistrates sent him to prison for a week. SEQUEL TO A ST. HELENS WALKING MATCH. At St, Helens County Court, on Wednesday, James Bolan, of Hargreave-street, Parr, sued John Sankey and James Ranson, both of Chancery-lane, for £2, amount of a bet in connection with a walking match held at Parr on July 18. Mr. H.L. Riley said Bolan bet Ranson £2 that. a competitor named Carvety would finish before a man named Tarpey. Tarpey finished first, but neither man was "placed" in the race. Plaintiff said that Tarpey was disqualified for running on a complaint by a competitor named Norcross. -A. committeeman named Rigby said the committee disqualified Tarpoy, and he personally informed him of his disqualification. On the other hand, Tarpey, the two defendants, and other witnesses give evidence that there was no disqualifica- tion, and that Tarpey did not run. Judge Collier remarked that it was very diffioult to tell whether a man who was walking very fast was running or not. He asked the witness Norcross what was the difference? Witness said he did not know, but walking was walking and running was running. When pressed for a further definition, witness said he could not give any.—Judge Colliar said it had not been proved to his satisfaction that Tarpey ran or was disqualified, and he gave judg- ment for defendants with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATII AT EARLEsTOWN. Mr, S. Brighouse, county coroner, conducted an in- quest at the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday,touch- ing the death of John King, a labourer, sixty years of age, who died at the common lodging-house,Fairclough- street, Earlestown, early that morning. Deceased had been drinking heavily for some weeks, and shortly after midnight, having gone to bed at about 8.30, ho fell to the floor. On being picked up life was found to be ex- tinct. Sergeant Lings said there were no marks of violence on the body. A verdict of "Death from natural causes" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY</p>
        <p>MEETING.</p>
        <p>Important Business.</p>
        <p>Impending Resignation of the Rev. John Gunnell. Many Warrington Wesleyans visited Padgate on Thursday when the quarterly meeting in • connection with the WarringtolFcircuit was held. TILE REV. H. TREVOR DAVIES AND MA.RIOLATR.Y. A special service in the afternoon was well attended the preacher being the Rev. Trevor H. Davies, of Man- chester. The rev, gentleman based an eloquent discourse on the words "But we all, with unveiled fa.ce,reflecting as a mirror the glory of the Lord, are transformed into the same imam) from glory to glory, even as from the -Lord the Spirit "2 Corinthians, 3, 18 (revised version). His theme was the methodist doctrine of Christian perfection, and he pointed out how every soul was really a reflection, of that which it looked upon with admiration and affection. He impressed upon his hearers that it was the Christian ideal to reflect the glory of the Lord, and this could be attained by companionship with Christ. He showed how Christ's character embodied the strength and courage of man and the tenderness of woman and this led him to make a passing allusion to Mariolatry., They had heard a great deal, he said, about the revival of Romish doctrine in the English Church, They might have no sympathy with some forms in which protest had been made, and yet they might feel very strongly that to-day doctrines of the most dangerous and pernici- ' ous character were being slowly but surdly introduced into the thought and life of England.. He believed that one of the most dangerous of those teach- ings was that of the mediation and worship of the Virgin Mary. That was a doctrine which was not only a flagrant breach of a direct, specific commandment, but it was one that did irreparable mis- chief to the character of Jesus:Christ. Why did those men want the mediation of a woman? The suggestion was that the tenderest, a feminine •grace, must be •con- ferred upon their faith by the intervention of a woman's heart. God forbid that they should say one word in disparagement of her who was called to be the mother of our Lord. Blessed was she among women. But they earnestly insisted that, in going to her Son Jesus Christ they did not know, they would not have any mediation. Ever linked to the manliest strength the world had ever known there was the tenderness of His character that far surpassed the pleading even of His mother's heart. He was strong as a man, but soft as a woman.—At the close of the service a collection was taken on behalf of the local and circuit funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BUSINESS, MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE BUSINESS, MEETING.</p>
        <p>Afterwards the members of the Cquarterly meeting and a number of invited guests sat down to an •ecellent tea in the schoolroom, and the Padgate friends were heartily thanked for their hospitalit): The business meeting was held in the evening under the presidency of the Rev. G. H. Carnson (uperin- tendent of the Circuit) and there were also present/ the Revs. R. Wilkins Rees, W. C. J. Neville end John Gunnell.—A statement, of membership was submitted by the Chairman and the hope was expressed that there, would soon be a substantial addition to the numbers in the Circuit.</p>
        <p>The circuit stewards (Messrs. George Weaver and J. G. Witlainshaw) brought forward the financial statement which, on the whole, was considered satisfactory. It was announced that invitations had been sent to certain ministers to succeedthe Revs. Cr:ll.Carnsonand Wilkins Rees,,who will leave the circuit after the 1905 conference. The members heard with regret that at the next conference the circuit will lose the direct ser- vices of the Rev John Gunnell, who is resigning from active duties after thirteen years' useful work in the cir- cuit.—A recommendation, was introduced that the, residence of the third minister be changed from War- rington to Penketh after next year's conference, when the Rev. J. Manning, of Derby, succeeds the Rev. W. C. J. Neville. This, of coussei would involve certain other changes and it was felt that the subject was of such importance that a definite decision. could not be arrived at until the December quarterly meeting. The proposition will then receive further consideration. Mr. R. HENSIIALL was thanked for his services as circuit foreign missionary treasurer and unanimously re-appointed—Mr. Edwin Richardson also received the thanks of the meeting for the manner in which he had discharged the duties of circuit treasurer of the Aged Ministers' and Ministers' Widows Pundandhis re-election was agreed to.</p>
        <p>Mr.-W. H. SEIERBURN was appointed circuit temperance secretary in place of Mr. Isaac Barlow,who has resigned The importance of some vigorous action in temperance matters throughout the circuit was urged. . The Rev., R.. WILKINS REES reported that the arrange- ments for the forthcoming foreign missionary anniver- saries throughout the circuit were, with the exception of some minor details, completed. The meeting was in sympathy with the proposal which has been made that . next year there should. be a special effort at various places in the circuit in connection with the centenary of the British and- Foreign Bible Society. The CIIIIRM/N, as required by the conference, urged the importance- of a substantial and speedy increase being made in the amount contributed to the funds of the theological institutions as absolutely necessary extensions would be made witbout delay. At the Liverpool May Synod a special committee was formed for the consideration of the important question of aggressive work among town populations. As recom- mended by the September District Synod, the matter was brought before the quarterly meeting, in a compre- hensive speech, by the Rev. R. WILKINS BEES. It was felt that something ought to. be done at the earliest possible moment to mach the great masses of people who never attend places of worship. It was decided to hold the next quarterly meeting at Bold•strect Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>SHIP CLIO.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CLIO.</p>
        <p>How the Warrington Boys are Faring.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Moore Murray director of education has pre- pared the following interesting report on the recent visit of a deputation from the School Attendance Com- mittee to the training ship "Clio " : Gentlemen,—ln accordance with:the resolution of June 22nd, the Chairman, Mr. Bibby, Mr. Plinston, Mr. Walker, the School Attendance Inspector and myself visited the Training Ship "Clio " on the 13th of -August. Thirteen boys from the a,dminstrativearea of the County Borough of Warrington were on the ship, eight of whom are paid for by your Committee. The ship appears :to he well found and bhe boys were healthy and had every appearance of being well kept. The boys are well trained, and would, no doubt, be useful additions to the mercantile service, and it is to be regretted that, owing to their being under-sized, they cannot enter the Navy. One of the boys from Warrington during the temporary vacancy in the bandmaster-ship of the ship has been taking charge of the band and such a lad, if he entered the Navy, would have every prospect of a good career. Both the Captain and the Chief Officer expressed themselves strongly on the flct that, oftentimes, the good work i done on the ship s rendered valueless by the boys re- turning to their homes and former haunts. The mem- bers of :the deputation felt that this is a matter which requires atteii Lon, as not only is the valuable time spent inlearn. ,ft. on board ship 'lost, but there is the consequent w, Ll'Ee of money when the boys return to their former companions iind develop habits of lazi- ness. This is a matter which should receive the careful consideration of the School Attendance Committee and it is hoped presently to be able to place before you some suggestions in order to prevent the boys going home. In many instances whilst the children are on the ship they are completely forgotten by their parents but as soon as the boys show signs of being valuable assets they are only too anxious to get them back home. The members of the deputation discussed the question of the cost of maintenance with the Chief Officer, and in future the charge will be .2s. per week instead of 2s. 6d. The members of the deputation seemed well pleased with what they saw, and doubtless the visit will prove valu- able to them when oas9s no brought before them‘which. require sending', Oa it kastktuticin of this character,"</p>
