      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>THE BENEFITS OF SCIENCE. It is seldom that any discovery of science is such as will benefit all classes of people. Some of the greatest discoveries of the age, while they have proved directly oeneficial to thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Even some of the best medi- cinal preparations, however much they may do to remove one class of disease, or suit some constitutions, at the ;same time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it. entirely untouched, or if they benefit one class of patients, they may injure another. Now the great need of the age is some scientifically arranged preparation which will cope effectually with the prevalent diseases of this country, which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adapted to the needs of the mer- chant and the workman, the professional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk, the factory hand, the miner, and the roadside labourer. It should be, too, such a preparation a contains no injurious ingredients, and which may be taken with impunity by the weakly child, or the delicate lady, as well as by the stronger constituted man. Such a discovery would deserve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal Preparations, and would indeed be an Invaluable Boon to Suffering Humanity. Now this much needed boon has been found, has been tried, has been- proved, and wherever proved, it has been found satisfactory. We refer to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters the Vegetable Tonic, which when once tried, has been always recommended, and has proved successful when all other medicine has failed to give rerief, and we may say further, that it has proved permanently beneficial, when other pre- parations at best only gave temporary relief. It is strongly recommended as The Best Remedy of the Age for Indigestion in its different forms, such as Sick Headache, Pains in the Side Giddiness, Loss of Appe- tite, also for Nervousness and Nervous Disorders, Sleep- lessness, Neuralgia, Low Spirits and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness either after an illness, long confinement to ill-ventilated rooms or any other cause. It strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengthens those parts as the system which have been weakened by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it is a Natural Tonic, and suited to all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledged to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease. If you suspect that your health is beginning to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking now a course of this excellent Tonic, which is sold verywhere in bottles at 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, but would any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the proprietors will forward same for the above prices. carriage free. Avoid Imitations. When purchasing,see the name, " Gwilym Evans," on Label,Stamp, and Bottle Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Com- pany Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON MAN'S GROSS LAZINESS-WIFE AND</p>
        <p>FAMILY STARVING.</p>
        <p>A bad case of neglect of family was heard at the Warrington Police Court yesterday when William Vero, Dial-yard, was charged with neglecting his four chil- 41ren.—Mr. Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. and stated that the prisoner served in South Africa during the war. Sincele returned he had done eight weeks work and the neglect of family was due purely and simply to his laziness. Mr. Hackett had had the case under supervision since April. Prisoner was in the habit of 1 ing in bed until noon and,of course, could not find work when he behaved like that. The wife and children had been starving for want of food and they had to go out begging. Prisoner went to North- wich whete he was arrested. He was brought to the, Warringtbn Police Station and had the impud- ence to ask for food to be sent to him from home. Hiq wife had not received a halfpenny _ .</p>
        <p>from him for weeks and when apprehended prisoner had Hs. 2d. in his possession—One of prisoner's daughters stated that her father had not done any regular work for ten years.—lnspector Hackett said that when be visited the house in July the family were starving and there was no food in the place. Prisoner said he wanted the wife and children to go into the, Workhouse, but he did not want to go there.—Prisoner- who stated that he could not obtain work in Warring ton and went to Northwich in search of employment was sentenced to six weeks' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. F. Monks (presiding) and J. W. Hayward, Alderman Sraethurat and Councillor</p>
        <p>Thorns:On. A MIDNIGHT CHASE.—Thomas Murphy, last from Liverpool, was charged with being a person of suspic- ious character and frequenting Bridge-street at 12.30 on Sunday morning.—The Chief Constable said prisoner was a convicted thief and had been in prison at several places. At 12.30 on Sunday morning P.C. Gilchrist was in Bridge-street and saw the prisoner moving about ina very suspicious manner. He was examining windows and doors. The officer secreted himself in a doorway and saw the prisoner violently kick Messrs. Pierpoint's window, with the evident intention of breaking it. When he saw the constable prisoner ran away. The officer followed and eventually prisoner slipped and fell and was taken iato custody. On the way to the Police Station he said"it was not the first time he had been talten down; he had been in gaol many a time." He (the Chief Constable) had ascertained that the prisoner had recently come out of prison in 'Scotland. and had been in custody at Lancaster for theft.-'-He was now sent to gaol for a month. SCENE IN COURT.—Michael Hennessey. 5, Pig-hill, was summoned for committing a breach of the peace by having been disorderly in Horsemarket-street on Sep- tember 7th.—He pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Clarke said defendant vvas leaning againsb one of the Central Hotel windows, and on being requested to move he refused. Subsequently he went into the hotel and had to be ejected. Witness had some difficulty in preventing the man from getting inside the hotel again. Defendant said that if he could get inside he would cut the landlord's head open, and that if he (witness) interfered, he would do the same for him.—Defendant became excited during the hearing of the case, and was very insolent to the i Chief Censtable.—He was remanded n custody until the following day. DRUNK IN CHARGE OF A CHlLD.—Louisa Smith,widow 12, Hall street was fined 5s for having been drunk whilst in charge of a child in Rose and Crown street on September Bth.—P. C. Whitby. said the defendant was drunk and carrying the child ma careless manner. She nearly fell to the ground—The Chief Constable said the case had been brought under the new Licensing Act. DISORDERLY BEHAVIOUR.—Peter Ward, 4, Lither- land's yard, was fined 5s and costs for having committed a breach of the peace by being disorderly in that yard on September 6th.—P. C's Reddington and Phillips proved the case. CHIMNEY ON FIRE.—For allowing the chimney at their works to be;on fire Messrs Reynolds and Company tanners,Mersey street, were fined Is.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Aldermen Sutton (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Smethurst and Dr. Fox.</p>
        <p>A PENITENT PRISONER.—MichaeI Hennessey, of Pig Hill, was charged on remand with committing a breach of the peace in Horsemarket-street.—Prisoner was remanded on Monday owing to his unseemly behaviour before the magistrates. He now appeared very penitent and said he was extremely sorry for what had occurred. He lost his temper on Monday.—P.C. Clarke proved the case.—The Chief Constable said this was prisoner's 38th appearance. The last occasion was on Mafeking night, 1900. Prisoner was bound over to keep the peace for six months, and ordered to pay the costs (Bs.) THE RECENT THEFTS OF BRASS.—On ,the name of Alfred France being called, the Chief qonstable said there was a charge against the man, 'who did not appear, of stealing brass Nteps, the property of the Whitecross Company,. He did not, however, propose to offerany evidence. France was recently sentenced to two months imprisonment for stealing brass and only came out of gaol on Monday. As the evidence for which he wa.sconvieted was committed after the offence which was now alleged against him it would be con- trary to all custom to proceed with !the case. He had suffered two months' imprisonment for a continuation of practically the same offence. It was open to the Whitecross Company to take out a warrant if they esired to do so.—The case was accordingly with drawn. THIIRSDAY.—Before Mr. Charles White (presiding) and Messrs. J. Hepherd, T. Birchall and J. R. Young AN OBSTINATE PnrsoNsa.—Ernest William Holden, an inmate of the Workhouse, was charged with stealing from the storeroom at the Workhouse,a handkerchief, of the value of61(1.0n being asked whether he would be tried at Warrington or at Itiverpool,prisoner emphatically're- marked that he would" be tried at Liverpool Sessions. —The Chief Constable stated that on September 10th, at 10.30 a.m., a man named Loftus was admitted to the Workhouse. He removed his clothing, prior to receiv- ing the Workhouse, dress and at two o'clock the porter, John Constible, took an inventory of the articles, and had them re *loved to the storeroom. When. Loftus took his dischar,3 on Wednesday morninftthe handkerchief was missed. It was found in the podsession of an in- snate named Groves and he in turn said he bought it from the prisoner. Inquiries were made, and circum- stances pointed to prisoner having stolen it. Prisoner '</p>
        <p>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT. A DOCTOR'S TESTIMONY. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is a scientific remedy, remarkable for its brilliant cures of coughs, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, influenza and children's coughs, far superior to ordinary cough mixtures, or any of the emulsions. George Knox, M.D.' 256, Second Avenue, Pittsburgh, Pa., writes " Veno's Lightning Cough Cure excels in chronic coughs and bronchitis, and is especially safe for children." Price is. nd. and 2s. 9d. Sold everywhere by chemists and medicine vendors.</p>
        <p>said a tramp gave him the handkerchief. Although prisoner had elected to he tried at Liverpool he did not think he quite understood what it meant,as the sessions were not until November. He, therefore, asked for a remand until Saturday, and by that time wiser counsel would probably have prevailed.—The remand was granted. FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Hepherd (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Thornton and J. Roberts. ALLEGED WIFE DESERTION.—George Pennington, Mill-lane, Newton-le-Willows, was summoned,for having deserted his wife Mary Pennington, now living at 12, David-street, Warrington.—Mr. Browne appeared for the complainant, and Mr. J. W. Knowles defended.—lt was alleged that complainant had occasion to complain of her husband's conduct, and came to Warrington to consult Mr. Browne. When she returned to Newton she could not obtain admittance to the house,defendant having locked the door. Defendant is an ex-soldier,and has only been married to the complainant abotit 12 months.—The Bench held that desertion was not proved and Mr. Browne withdrew the case. A NIIISANCE.—Mary Cullen, 12, Allen-street, was summoned to show cause why an order should not be I made to abate a nuisance arising from defective spout- ing, ashpits &amp;c. at houses owned by her.—lnspector Flood gave evidence and an order for the abatement of the nuisaw.ce within seven days was made. I CASE DrsmissED.—William Clarke, 15, Phillip-street, was summoned for assaulting his wife Margaret Clarke by striking her in the face and inflicting a black eye.— Complainant said defendant also threatened to cut her throat and to drown her.—She admitted throwing a bucket of water over her husband while he was in bed and striking him on the head with a bottle.—The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions. WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. S. R. Armitage (presid- ing), and T.-Halton. SUNDAY GAMBLING AT BURTONWOOD.—Three lads named Joseph Matthews, James Darbyshire, and Fred Williams, of Burtonwood, were summoned for playing pitch and toss in Bold-road, on Sunday afternoon, August 30th.—Matthews denied that he was out that day,and,Darbyehire said he was not present.—The police stated that all the boys were there, while 15 others ran laway.—Matthews was fined 7s. 6d., and the other two defendants were warned on account of their youth and ordered to pay the costs only. No LIGHT.—For having no light on his vehicle at Burtonwood, Mark Cooper, Thatto Heath, was fined 2s. 6d and costs. OBSTRUCTION.—Aaron Souther and Thomas Forrest, of Haydock, were each fined 20s. for causing obstructions with their horses and carts at Burtonwood, on Septem- ber 7th and 10th. CHARGE OF THEFT AT EARLESTOWN.—MichaeI Keat- ing of no fixed abode, was charged with stealing a chamois leather, valued at 28. 6d., the property of John Alexander Churchill, Cross-lane, Earlestovvn. On the 11th inst. Mr. Churchill was using the leather to clean the shop windows, but a customer entering the shop he left it, on the window led&amp;. On his return it was missing. A constable subsequently found defendant in bed atForshaw's lodging-house closeby,and the leather was in his coat pooket.—Defendant stated that he found the' leather on the footpath. He did not intend to steal it.—The Bench dealt with defendant under the First Offenders' Act and bound him over to appear for judgment if called upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>IT WASN'T A SECRET.</head>
        <p>IT WASN'T A SECRET.</p>
        <p>MANY mothers with growing daughters will (says the "Burnley Gazette) appreciate he thankfulness of Mrs. Mary Turner, of 23, Lyndhurst-road, Burnley. She said she had used Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people for her two daughters, with the best results. "They began ailing,' she said, "with anmmia and general debility, and had to get off their work. I was recommended to try these pills, and a few doses soon put them all right. That was about four years ago, when they would e, respectively, about sixteen and fourteen years old. Ever since, if theyhave felt run down, or off their food, and not fit for work, I have given them these pills, which have always put them all right again." Mrs. Turner added that she thought it was better to keep her daughters well inthis way than to pay doctor's bills. She made no secret of a piece of information shared by thousands of mothers. Nothing else is as good for growing girls. Dr. Williams' pink pills have an effect like no other medicine. It is unmistakable; you see the benefit in a week and it lasts a lifetime.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB. The Season's Averages.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET CLUB. The Season's Averages.</p>
        <p>On the whole the members of the Warrington eleven may congratulate themselves upon having had a successful.season.Of 21 matches played nine were won two lost, and ten drawn. The victories include Sefton (2), Heaton Mersey (2), Newton-le-Willows, Earlestown, Bootle, St. Helens' and South Manchester. Defeats have been incurred by Ashton-under-Lyne and Bootle, and drawn games have been played with Northern (2), Manchester (2), Bolton, Cheadle, Hulme (2), Earlestown, Newton-le-Willows,and Ashton-under- Lyne. Matches with Bolton, St Helens, and South Manchester were abandoned through rain. The averages for the first eieven ace as follows: _</p>
      </div>
