      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WRITER OF OUR NEXT "SPECIAL."</head>
        <p>THE WRITER OF OUR NEXT "SPECIAL."</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Paul, who will contribute our next special article on "Retaliatory Tariffs," is just fifty years old, andis probably one of our ablest writers on political questions. Educated at Eton and at Corpus Christi College, Ox- ford, even as an Undergraduate Mr. Paul shewed signs of exceptional ability. He was President of the Ox- ford Union in the days of Mr. Asquith and Lord Milner, and after graduating 8.A., he proceeded to the Bar, and is a member of Lincoln's inn. From 1892 until 1895 Mr. Paul represented South Edinburgh in Parliament, and his speeches always com- manded the careful attention and respect of Liberal audiences. He is a fighting speaker, who never hesitates to give back as good as he gets. Mr. Paul has published a capital Life of the late Mr. i Gladstone, and s closely engaged in literary work. For many years he contributed some of the most telling leaders in the London" Daily News." He is usually to , be found at the great gatherings of the Eighty Club in I, London, and may always be relied on for an impromptu ' speech which will interest, amuse, and stimulate the audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>21 THE EDUCATION QUESTWit. The Rev. Morley Stevenson and the Opposition to the New Act.</head>
        <p>21 THE EDUCATION QUESTWit. The Rev. Morley Stevenson and the Opposition to the New Act.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Morley Stevenson, M.A. Principal of the Warrington Training College, was the preacher at St. Paul's Church on Sunday 'morning, when the offertory was on behalf of the day schools. The rev, gentleman took for his text the words "Verily, I say unto you, if ye have faith as a grain of mustard seed, ye shall say unto this mountain,remove hence to yonder place,and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you." At the conclusion of a thoughtful discourse on faith, the preacher directed the attention of the congregation to the vast importance of religious education in day schools. It was the early inculcation of the true,beautiful, noble life, he said, which generally helped a man to be what he ought to be. When a little child had been brought up in misery and wickedness they had a very hard task to turn it into a good man or woman. But bring uplthe child in truth, purity. and .goodneas, and they wouldfind that even if achild fell away for a time, they would have in afterlife somtthing upon which they could build in the assurance thane would end right at the last. It was only that morning that he received a letter from one of his past students wtio was working in a school in the slums of one of our great cities. She told him of her difficulties and said that the little children in,that great city seemed to have no idea of right and wrong. Their creed seemed tp be that it did not matter if they did wrong, provided they were not found out. Was not that terribly sad, and did they not see it even in Warrington round about them—that growing spirit. that "it does not matter very much so long as I am not found out ?" There was no idea in the child's mind of doing a thing because it was right. As they followed this up he thought there came before then&amp; the immense import- ance of the atruth being put before the child's mind from the very beginning. .He did not think there was any need to impress upon any Christian congregation further than by such thoughts as those, he would not say the duty,. but the privilege of supporting the schools of then parish which gave the religious education which was so all- important. He must, however, in justice, answer oneof the questions which had been put to them. Peoplesaid that since the passing of the Education Act the expenses of the schools were being provided out of the public funds, and therefore ,why should they be asked to sub- scribe to them any longer? That was a perfectly fair question, and he would try to give them a perfectly fair answer. In 1870 the Government of this country, for the first time, said it would have a law that every child in the country should go to school. Of course, a very large demand for increased school accommodation was necessary—a far larger demand than could be met by the voluntary contributions of those who were interested in education. Therefore Board schools were provided in our large towns, and they were built and supported out of the public funds. Then began a very difficultitime for the old-established Church or voluntary schools. The Board schools were equipped out of the public purse and to that purse, as they all knew, there hardly seemed to be a bottom,at all events,if there was it was a very long way down. The consequence was that the Board schools were built in the best possible way. equipped with the best possible apparatus, and staffed with the best possible teachers. The effeot upon the old class of schools was, that if they were tg, keep pace with the very just and proper progress othe times, they must be brought, as far as was possible, up to the level of the new rate-built and rate-supported schools. Consequently;the cost of education per child rose very rapidly to a very large sum. Then came upon the old kind of school, what the Prime Minister called the "intolerable strain." It was impossible that the friends of education should any longer furnish the very large stuns that were required for the up-keep and develop- ment of their schools. Was it just that the old schools, which had borne the burden and heat of the day, which had risen out of voluntary con- tributions, and were largely supported by them, should go on being penalized because they taught the religion of the ,Church of England, Wesleyans, Roman Catholics or anybody else ? Was it fair that be- cause they taught such religion as was undoubtedly held by a large proportion of the population, who sent their children to the schools, they should be penalized and denied support from the public funds? The Government thought it was not just, and so they had the Education Act of last year which, to a certain ex- tent, had removed that intolerable strain and provided that the maintenance and upkeep of every school which was doing good and satisfactory work, whatever its de- nomination might be, should be supplied for the future out of the public funds. One thing remained for the supporters of voluntary schools. They must provide the buildings and maintain them in efficient and proper repair. i If it became necessary to add to those buildings or to alter or improve them, the cost would come upon the supporters of the schools. It was for that, that the collection was needed that day and 'could be needed every year, he supposed. They would see at once that if no immediate repairs were required, it would be very unwise not to form a fund out of which the repairs could he met when necessary. Repairs were an expensive business, and if they were called upon all at once to , alter, improve, or modify in accordance with the sug- gestions of the education body it would be very difficult if not impossible to do so. Therefore it would be a wise thing to at 9nce set aside what could be set aside year by year in order that a fund might be formed, out of which the repairs, alterations, additions and improve- ments could be made. They knew that the act had met with a great deal of opposition, and that many of their Nonconformist friends especially were strongly opposed to it. He was sorry to say that a great many things had been said about the clergy and about the Act which would not bear investigation. He did not for a moment mean to impugn their veracity, but he thought the opponents fo the act had not studied it and had not realized always exactly what it meant. They said It gave the schools over to the clergy. On the contrary it had taken the schools largely out of the control of the clergy. It had placed upon every body of managers two managers who were appointed by the town and who need not be, in most cases would not be, of the denomination of the school. It hadplaced the whole of the religious instruction, not in the hands of the clergy- man but in the hands of the managers, of whom the clergyman need not necessarily be one. Therefore they would see that so far from the schools being placed in the hands of the clergy—though for his part he should think the clergy were very suitable people to have them —there had been certain conditionsintroduced which had given the clergy far less power over the control of their schools than they had had in the past. He thought this opposition would die down. He did not think there was very much heart in it, or very much back- bone. He was afraid it savoured more of politics than of religion, and he thought the good sense which dis- tinguished our countrymen would come to their aid and help them to see that the opponents of the Act had been fighting a shadow. He did not think we had come to the ideal system yet. He hoped, please God, to live 1 to see the day when we should have a more just system of religious education than we had at the present time. He wanted to see every child brought up in the faith of his parents. That was the _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>only really just and fair system of religious education. He hoped we should do in England what they now did in Germany, that was to say that there would be differ- ent schools in each town—Church of England, Roman Catholics, Wesleyans and so on—so that every child could be brought up in the religion of his parents. That, he thought, was just and he hoped they would work and strive till they got it. In conclusion he asked them to support a cause than which none, he thought, appealed with greater strength or justice to every Christian person. A children's flower service was held in the afternoon and the Rev. J. Wright Williams, vicar of Farnworth, preached in the evening. The collections for the day amounted to £8 17s. 3d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PECULIAR DEATH OF A CHILD AT EARLESTOWN. Unlabelled Drugs.</head>
        <p>PECULIAR DEATH OF A CHILD AT EARLESTOWN. Unlabelled Drugs.</p>
        <p>1 Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, conducted an in- I quest at the Town Hall,Earlestown, on Monday touching ithe death of William Macdonald, aged seven weeks, son of James Macdonald, of 26, Haydock street, Earlestown. It seems that the child had been ill since birth,suffering from windy spasms. Dr. Gange was called in and pre- scribed for the child on one occasion. Frequently however, the child had been given a medicine called "Mother's Friend," and on the 10th inst. the grand- mother of deceased administered a dose of this. The medicine had been obtained from Mrs. Mee, chemist, of Bride-street, Earlestoivn. The child died on Friday morning last at 11.30. Previous to death Dr. Gahge was sent for, but as he was away from home Dr. Henry Salomon attended, and he ordered the child to be put in a hot bath, but death ensued as stated. The coroner ordered an examination of the child to be made, andthe result was that it was found that death was due to narcotic poisonint. Mrs. Macdonald, mother of deceased, said that owing to her having suffered from a gathered breast, she had been unable to attend to the child as she would have liked. The "Mother's Friend "was brought for her by Harriet Ellis, a girl, eighteen years of age. Harriet Ellis said the drug was given to her by Mrs Mee, without either label or directions on the bottle. After having heard the medical testimony, the coroner-adjourned the inquest until the 29th inst., to enable the county analyst to make an analysis of the Mother's Friend."</p>
        <p>Poaching during the past few weeks has been carried on extensively in North-East Cheshire, and several large landowners have complained to the police of the raids that have been made upon game preserves. Rabbits, hares, pheasants, and partridges have been taken in great quantities, this being more noticeable on land adjoining Macclesfield. In consequence of the com- plaints the police have received special instructions to deal with poachers.</p>
        <p>CORRIE GRANT.</p>
        <p>[This article forms one of a series which we have arranged with leading members of the Eighty Club to contribute to our colunms.Book copy-right strictly reserved. The next article will be byMr. Herbert Paul on "RETALIATORY TARIFFS:I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>GOOD NEWS FOR MANY.</head>
        <p>GOOD NEWS FOR MANY.</p>
        <p>It is very satisfactory to know that a seemingly per- fect remedy for all Kidney troubles has at last been discovered. It is called Dodd's Kidney Pills and many cures are being reported in the newspapers every day. One of the most striking illustrations of what this medicine will do follows : Mr. David Lines of Cromer Hyde, Hatfield, Herts, was a victim to Kidney Trouble for many years. Every- thing he tried was the same, it gave him no relief, in fact, he gave up trying, and thought he would have to continue suffering an the same way to the end. He was in this state when a friend advised him to try Dodd's Kidney Pills. He took the advice, began taking them and soon found that they were doing him good. He continued,and after taking two boxes, he was well enough to get up and go out to work. Now, after a complete course, he is quite cured and free from pain. Every case published gives the full name and address and may be completely verified. They all appear to be the truthful statements of honest men and women so that it is safe to say that this remedy—Dodd's Kidney Pills—is worth a trial by all those who suffer from any form of Kidney Trouble.</p>
        <p>CYCLING ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>John Abbott, aged fifteen, of 4, Haydnck-street, St. Helens, went with a companion to Southport on Sun- day, and when cycling along the promenade he is alleged to have been run down by a motor-car and left bleeding and unconscious. Some bystanders removed him to the Southport Infirmary, where he is recovering from his injuries.</p>
        <p>CASES AT WALLASEY. • At Wallasey on Wednesday two cases fornon-payment of the education sate were heard. The defendants stated their conscientious objections to the rate. The magistrates said they had no alternative but to direct payment, and orders were accordingly made. CONGREGATIONALISTS AND THE GENERAL ELECTION. _</p>
        <p>The resolution to be ;submitted- to the forthcoming Assembly of the Congregational Union of England and Waleq at Bournemouth expresses the Assembly's thank- fulness at the unanimous opposition of Congregational- ists to theEducatiou Act, and its profound sympathy with those brethren who suffer the spoiling of their impending election for conscience sake. In view of an election the resolution urges all Congregationalists to make their support of any Parliamentary candidate con- ditional on kis being pledged at least to abolish all religious tests on the teaching profession, and to secure public control and management of all schools main- tained by public funds. Lastly, the resolution asserts the Assembly's conviction that the only final solution of the question will be found in a complete system of State-paid education from which all denominational teaching is excluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>The Proposed New Church at Orford.</p>
        <p>The boxes in aid of the Church Building Fund have been opened and found to contain £8 13s. id. In addition promises to the amount of £2,625 12s, have been already acknowledged. To this must be added the promise of a site by Colonel Ireland-Blackburne, which is at the present being carefully examined with a view to finding a good foundation for building purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Golborne _ Street _Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Golborne _ Street _Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday at the above clauroil the Rev. T.Holehouse, a blind pastor of Middleton, preached morning and evening. At the latter service the preacher suggested some lessons which could be learned from "The Ladder" which Jacob saw under the headings of" God's presence is not confined to any place", "How we may mount to God,"" Earth's solitudes reveal Heaven's ladder." In conclusion he urged his hearers to let Jacob's God be their God,adding that in doing so they need fear nothing that the future can have in store.</p>
        <p>Christ Church, Pktigate. Special services were held and ellertories taken on behalf of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts, on Sunday. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. Dr. Durham. of Dublin, who served as Government chaplain in 'the presidency of Madras, India, for over twenty-three years. The anthem, " Praise the Lord all ye heathen "was sung by the choir, the treble solo being sweetly rendered by Master Wilfred Fryer. In the evening the sermon was preached by the Rev. G. Vale Owen, of Orford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Wycliffe _ P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe _ P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>' At the mee-ting of this society on Sunda, the chair was occupied by Mr. B. Farrar, the hon. secretary. In referring to the objects of the P.S.A. Society he said the meetings were not meant to provide a means of entertaining people but to be a means of leading people to something higher. The P.S.A. was meant to be soul- lifting and soul-inspiring. In reviewing the work of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society, he mentioned that many men had expressed their indebtedness for the good they had received at the meetings. During the past half year, 362 prizes had been won, making a total of 9,608 prizes distributed. The membership now exceeded 500. The Sick Club and the Savings Club were both in a satis- factory condition. There NVOTO now 61 members of the Savings Club and it had been decided that withdrawals could be made at Christmas and Walking Day,—Mr. Fooks, of Manchester, delivered an earnest address on "Sowing and Reaping."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Ellesmere-street Methodist Mission. Harvest Festival.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Methodist Mission. Harvest Festival.</p>
        <p>The plain and humble interior of the home of this mission was transformed into a veritable bower of beauty by the efforts of a large band of the workers, and through the kindness of friends who contributed offerings of fruit, corn, flowers' food, and decorations. When filled as it was twice on Sunday it must have pre- sented an encouraging sight to the Rev. G. H, Carnson, who conducted the services. His plain, homely conver- sational sermons were fully appreciated. The services throughout were hearty and helpful. The collections were in advance of previous years. In continuance of the festival,a, fruit banquet and social meeting took place on the following evening, when the chair was occupied by Mr. John Barlow. The room was again crowded. An attractive programme of a miscellaneous character gave every satisfaction several items being encored. Fruit and other refreshments were handed round by the lady workers rat intervals. Songs were well rendered by Miss Beatrice Carriage and Mr. J. Hannon; a recita- tion was ably given by Miss Annie Furness; 'cello and mandoline solos were contributed by Mr. J. Jackson and Miss Bella Broomhead, and the phonograph was also a welcome contributor. A most enjoyable evening was brought to a close by the sale of produce, compre- hensive votes of thanks, and the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Customer First</head>
        <p>Customer First</p>
        <p>The wpy io do business is to put one's customer's interest first: be faithful to him.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptba does that; it goes far to make a grocer a good one.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>BETTER THAN MONEY_ IN THE</head>
        <p>BETTER THAN MONEY_ IN THE</p>
        <p>SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>The working man to-day gives more attention to the food he has to eat than his forefathers gave as he realises that his health—the bank—depends verilargelf upon the properties of what he eats and drinks. It follows, then, that the popular article of food is that ;which is easy to digest; but more popular still are preparations which are found to aid and strengthen the digestive organs, and also to act as an energiser for the whole system. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is such a food. It places a means in the hands of everyone to build up and maintain a sound constitution, which enables its possessor to travel his life's journey without the aches and pains which are in many cases preventable. Thus we come round again to sound common sense based 00 experience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WYCLIFFE TEMPERANCE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE TEMPERANCE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Speeches by the Revs. John Yonge</p>
        <p>and J. L Milne. A social temperanoe gathering, the first of the season in connection with Wycliffe Temperance Society, was held in Wycliffe Memorial Buildings,Edgeworth-street, on Wednesday evening. There was a large attendance, the'room being filled. The chair was occupiedby theßev• John Yonge and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. J. L. Milne (pastor of St. John's Presby- terian Church), Mr. R. Wyllie, aud Mr. F. :Vivaldi (hon. secretary.) The CHAIRMAN, in the course of a few introductory remarks, said he was sure those present desired ° address themselves to a campaign in Christ's name' behalf of temperance and total abstinence. Last 3Ter some very successful meetings were held in connection with the Wycliffe !Temperance Society, and the Com- mittee were determined to do their heel to see,that the meetings shouldlbe evenfbetter than those of the previonc year. He would like to suggest in regard t° the character and aim of the meetings, that little more heed should be paid to the practical side of temperance work. Whilst strengthening the'n; selves in the principles to which they were pledge° they must try to enlist new recruits for the temPer" ance cause. (Hear, hear.) Their influence and example he hoped would tend to the furtherence of that whzdif they believed was a valuable and important part o Christian life and Christian service. They needed n revival in the temperance cause. He was not quite sur° whether they had been quite as militant and aggressiv,! as it was desirable they should become. Efforts shotif be made to reach and influence people outside We Wycliffeinstitutions, who were not connected with onl 1$ place of worship. They must endeavour to save peOP-,. who might be in danger from this particular evil whleu all so much deplored. As temperance people thought they should also do a little met° towards guiding public opinion on the question. They could not say that Warrington wail exemplary town in the matter of temperance. Th„°„ja had tool much evidence in the records of their pol"; courts and the history of their townspeople to lay on' such flattering unction to their souls as that. Therefore they must speak out on public topics affecting temPer" ance Mr. Balfour's remarks with referent* to licensing matters were nothing less than a publi° scandal. The PrimelMinister had practically given his' self into the hands of the brewers. It seemed 2' political dodge of Mr Balfour who was anxious to woe all things nice and sweet and safe for his Poi, in the coming general elect i on, but ....t1.1".0. question of licensing ought not to be a P64"1 matter at all. It was a matter affecting the welfare °t the nation and it would be avery great evil to the gr.° 14" cause of temperance, and a great hindrance to the 0 fare of this nation if Mr. Balfour's suggestion of core' ulv pensation were earn ied out. He hoped that as a res A of the Wycliffe Temperance Society their temper°" " backbone was strengthened and their temperance thusiasm very largely increased. The Rev. J. L. MILNE in the obsence of the Re,s: J. F. Matthews delivered an addrees. He was qualified, he said, to speak of the medical aspect ot question, but there were two points in connection v it that should not be everlooked. It was well kn°7-f that alcohol was injurious in its effect to the germs life and could not therefore be good for the hwers system. Then everyone knew that insurance societte accepted temperance people at smaller premiums tii-1 drunkards, and this pointed to the fact tit, total abstainers were more healthy than those w... indulged in intoxicating liquor. He could testify ire„Ta his own experience that drink was not necessarY• had had, perhaps, as rough a time of it, and had hau work as hard as the average man. And yet he had l'eL% taken six-pennyworth of intoxicating liquor. its believed he was as well and strong as anybody' Warrington, and he could prove that if alcohol did 004 harm it did no good. One could get along very `, without it. He was a total abstainer, he continurf because he was afraid of strong drink. He had men of strong wills break down before the teuiptat.iA°7; of drink. If he became a moderate drinker how ti,l,uch? know that he would be able to continue as '—olf Even if he continue moderate drinker he didnot ab. , whether his children who copied his example, iv°u, aog able to do the same. In many a home, mother i,abit° father's hearts had been broken by the drinking hod of a son or daughter and perhaps these childreeinter- first tasted intoxicating drink at home. He Ives hiS mined that no children of his should taste any 0'.43 it home, and if they fell before this terrible cor°l.oo,d would not be through his example. The only safe sale was to leave strong drink alone. He wanted t 0 0o those who were falling before it and he couldnot tint if he indulged in it himself. He believed strong 'titbit to be morally harmful. The poverty and nuiser,ere existed in consequence of drink was appaling. L was a great deal of talk about the libgtho of the subject. Recently he was looking through the list of inmates in an asylum, and he found that ni majority of the inmates were there in consequene°_,a their indillgence in intoxicating drink. It was the o°Zi • with regard,to their workhouses and gaols, the ;,,he of the inmates having been ruined by strong drink._-",:ic brewers and publicans had pocketed the profits,and._t4. the ratepayers and taxpayers were called upon to tam n the victims of strong drink. The cost was mous and the burden was increasing. It was tinaegrno the people as a whole rose up and oast the evil to their midst. This wouldhave to be dohe if we wore continue the nation we had been. During the evening an interesting prograninlrio vocal and instrumental music was rendered. `lia Bertha Bouchier very efficiently contributed two solos. Solos were sung by Miss Lucy Travis ann, William Brown. Mr. Thomas Wallington plaven pia.nofocte accompaniments. Miss Furness recited. Mr. C. Morrell contributed graraaphone selectl°o- - was distributed during the evening and an B°J,ol able evening's entertainment concluded with the 11°- , votes of thanks.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN CHAPEL' lace The anniversary sermons in conneetion with dill ee- of worship were preached on Sunday by the Rev.l4-1-1,.. by man whose discourses were listened to interest Wit.,ice good congregations. In the afternoon a musical seiryto was given by the members of the Bold-street Weeleilod Choir, under the conductorship of Mr. F. Hickmaol log acquitted themselves very creditably. In the ergice the Sankey Bridges choii rendered the .anthems foot, ear 0 Lord "and "Daughter of Zion" with good 0 tbe Mr. D. R. Dunn conducted. The collections for day amounted to £5 4s. Porridge means Power</p>
