      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>RIOTOUS IRISHMEN. Convictions at Earlestown.</head>
        <p>RIOTOUS IRISHMEN. Convictions at Earlestown.</p>
        <p>-- Messrs. T. J. Gillespie and J. Unsworthhelda special oourt at Newton Polia Station, on Monday, to hear cases of riotous conduct on the part of a number of Irishmen- Thomas Melody and Patrick Flaherty were charged Lith having been drunk and disorderly,and further with ng assaulted P.C. Sephton whilst in the execution of his duty on Sunday evening. P. C. Sephton stated that at about 10.15 on Sunday 'yelling he was called to Mr. Longton's farm, owing to disturbance having broken out amongst the farm labourers. When witness got there he found the two trisoners, who were very drunk and threatening Mr. -14nugton. On taking them into custody Flaherty struck tness on the shoulder and got him by the neck. He nad to obtain the assistance of three civilians. Melody truck him and got hold of Flaherty to try and rescue He had a great deal of trouble to get him to the station. One of the prisoners had no right on the farm at.all. •</p>
        <p>--..- Mr. William Longton, farmer, stated that at about 10.15 p.m. on Sunday he heard a disturbance in the yard 4.24 the farm. He went out to the men and told them that ,• there was any bother he would send for a policeman. ineY quietened down a little. Shortly afterwards he :44 Cause again to go out to them, and owing to the ROugh behaviour and the bad language used by prisoners 4,41 had to send for the police. Melody had no right on farm. He saw them each assault P.C. Sephton. He Wes told that Melody had presented a revolver at some of the men, and had threatened to shoot them. They both pleaded guilty, and were fined 10s. and costs each, or in default a month's imprisonment. Three rough-looking Irishmen, who gave their names as John Coffey, Patrick Scarry, and John Garrity, were • h charged with having been drunk and riotous at OUrtOnWOOd.</p>
        <p>.11.-Eiain-ford stated that the prisoners, in company ...With several other men, were drunk and making a dis- ,.‘llrbance in Broad-lane, Buttonwood, on Sunday. They "ez., e fighting each other, and using very bad language. InsPector Foster stated that oa Sunday evening there re about 50 or 60 Irishmen engaged at the various jras at Buttonwood, and they were all from different .3,k'uttA sof Ireland. They had visited the public-houses then collected together in Broad-lane. They corn- ed to fight and carry on in a disgraceful way; in _act it was little short of a riot. Fortunately, there Zre,_ three constables in the vicinity, or else there •a gut /lame been a very serious affray. The three pri- .oners + assaulted the police, but three other men taken 411,.:_° custody did not do so. Coffey was further charged Vita assaulting P.C. Rainford. Evidence was given to ~}1 effect that whilst arresting prisoner he violently e.b.-"'uoli the constable. Scarry and Garrity were also 17"rgocl, with assaulting P.C.'s Bateman and Dugdale esPectitelv. All three prisoners were fined 10s, and costs each.. The money was paid in every case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
