      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PADGA rE.</head>
        <p>PADGA rE.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION.—An interesting ceremony took plac ;z1 the Cottage Homes Padgate, when Mr. William eury Antrobus, who has acted as clerk and storekeeper .10r the past six yeast, was presented with a handsome 4ressing-caose and a leather hand-bag., which had been 44, bscribed for by the officers of the Institution. The ,zuperintendent (Mr. Arthur Wright)made the presenta- tion and in a few well chosen words, spoke of the high esteem in which Mr. Antrobus was held, and wished him svery success in his new sphere of labour. Mr. Antro- b_us, who has been appointed storekeeper at the Cottage homes of the Manchester Guardians at Culcheth, suit- ably responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WORTHLESS CHEQUE. Ingenious Fraud on a Warrington Jeweller.</head>
        <p>A WORTHLESS CHEQUE. Ingenious Fraud on a Warrington Jeweller.</p>
        <p>A MUCH " WANTED " MAN.</p>
        <p>Another extraordinary case was brought before the notice of the magistrates at the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday, when a well-dressed young man named George Ernest Morris, a native of Exeter, was charged with obtaining by means of false pretences a gold watch and chain of the value of £2O from Thomas Domville, jeweller and pawnbroker, Knutsford-road, on September 4th. The Chief Constablesaid this case was likely to be a very important one, and he would ask for a remand for a week in order to prepare the evidence. On Friday, September 4th, prisoner went to the National Provin- cial Bank in Bridge-street and told the manager he had an account of £BOO at Barclay's Bank, Middlesbrough, which he wished to transfer to the National Provincial Bank. He asked for a cheque form and made out a cheque for £BOO, which he gave to the manager as part of the formalities of the "transfer." The manager took it in perfect good faith, and the prisoner, saying he had a purchase to make in the town, asked the manager for a ibla,nk cheque form. This was given to the prisoner, who, at twenty minutes past one the same day, went to Mr. Domville's shop and arranged for the purchase of a gold watch and albert guard, which came to £2O 7s. Mr. Domville, seeing that the man .was going to be a cash customer, agreed to take £2O for the articles. Prisoner then said: "Will you take my cheque for the amount and your assistant can accompany me to the bank and see that my account is all right." This seemed. so feasible andreasonable that Mr. Domville agreed,but he made a fatal mistake in giving prisoner the watch and chain. Mr. Domville sent his assistant with the prisoner to the bank, and whatever was said to the young man there by the manager or his representative seemed to have satisfied him because he returned to Mr. Domville and said the matter was all right. What occurred at the bank was extraordinary. Police- Sergeant Williamson, who had been instructed by him (the Chief Constable) to call at the various banks in the town respecting another bank fraud which had been committed, was in the manager's room at the time, and when the prisoner went into the bank on the second occasion the manager said to him: "There is a police officer in the bank making inquiry about a bank fraud. Would you like to see him?" Prisoner replied: "It does not concern me in the least," and, thinking the game was up, bolted Prisoner had the watch and chain in his possession at the time and he appeared to have gone straightaway to the pawnshop of Mr. Houghton and pledged the guard for £4los. The police ware very hot upon the prisoner's track. He avoided all railway stations, and hired a cab, in which he drove to Widnes railway station. On the same day the police traced him as booking from there to Liverpool. Before five o'clock they traced the watch to a pawnshop in Liverpool, where it had been pledged for £B. The man's scent was, however, lost by the police for the time being, but, fortunately, he (the Chief Constable) was enabled to unearth the prisoner's past history, although he' was a stranger to Warrington, and to obtain from other police forces that knew more about him than he did, his photograph and description, which he circulated throughout the length and breadth of the British Isles. The photograph was in the hands of every police officer in the kingdom within a very short space of time after the transaction. This was only one of a long series of frauds by which the prisoner had maintained himself in comparative affluence for, probably years. He was " wanted " in various places in the country • in fact, he waslone of the" most wanted " men he country; Chief Constable) knew. The result of the circulating of his description and photograph was that he turned up at a bank in Chester on Monday and attempted to commit a similar fraud. The police were notified, and they recog- nized the prisoner as the man wanted at Warrington. He was arrested and handed over to Sergt. Williamson. The warrant Mr. Doraville had taken out in the meantime) I for his arrest was read over to the prisoner, who re- plied: "Yes, it is right ; I intend to plead guilty." Prisoner was remanded until Thursday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SCHOOL BOY'S SHOCKING DEATH.</head>
        <p>A SCHOOL BOY'S SHOCKING DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Coroner and the Dangers of Warrington Bridge.</p>
        <p>A school boy named Percy Houghton Bennett, the nine-year-old son of 31r. Ernest Bennett, provision dealer, 554, Knutsford-road, lost his life under parti- cularly distressing circumstances on Tuesday afternoon He was returning home from People's College and when crossing Arpley-road was knocked down by a horse attached to a sand cart in charge of William Brereton, of Stockton Heath. The wheel of the vehicle caught deceased's head which was so severely crushed that death ensued a few minutes afterwards. The inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Wednesday afternoon by Coroner Brighouse. Mr. Ernest Bennett, father of the deceased, stated that his son was nine years of age. He left home to go to school at one o'clock on Tuesday afternoon. .Later in the day witness heard that lie had met with an accident and afterwards saw him dead.</p>
        <p>In answer to the Coroner, witness said he had heard nothing that would lead him to believe that Mr. Brereton was to blame in the matter.</p>
        <p>Replying to the foreman of the jury (Mr. Shillcock), witness said his son attended People's Collage. William Brereton, contractor, The Mill, Stockton Heath, said that at 4.30 on Tuesday afternoon he left Stockton Heath with his horse and cart in order to take a load of sand to Liverpool-road. When in Arpley- road he saw the deceased and two other children en the footpath. Two of the children ran across the road between the rear of a cart and witness's horse's head. Deceased then looked round and also started to cross the road. He ran against the horse's head and was knocked down, and one of the horse's feet caught him and knocked him under the near side wheel of the cart. Witness at once pulled up and with assistance got the boy from under the wheel. His neck was badly injured and he died on being taken to the Packet Houke Inn. Dr. Bowden was sent for, but on his arrival life was extinct.</p>
        <p>In answer to a juror witness said he was five or six yards away from the bridge. Witness was walking with the horse, with his right hand at the horse's head. Thomas Griffiths, insurance agent, 60, Oxford-street, said he was standing against the rails at Warrington Bridge at ten minutes to five on Tuesday evening. He saw Brereton coming along with the horse and cart. There were two other carts in front. Two boys crossed the road between the first and second carts. Deceased turned round to see where the others had gone and was going between the back of the socond cart und Prete- ton's horse, when he walked backwards right into the horse's head. He was knocked down, and en trying to scramble up he fell backwards under the cart. Witness ran to his assistance, but could not reach him in time. He carried deceased into the Packet House, where he died in his arms. .Brereton did all he could to prevent the accident. The Coroner then read the written statement of Mrs Jolley,:wife of Mr. Harold Jolley 17, Arpley street who described the accident. She felt very strongly that the accident would never have happened had it not been for the narrowness of Warrington Bridge which was a standing disgrace to the townand altogether too narrow for the amount of vehicular traffic passing over it daily The Coroner: Had the bridge really anything tci do with the accident?</p>
        <p>Detective Barlow (Coroner's officer) : No sir, the accident occurred in Arpley road. The Coroner: If it had should have something to say about it.---</p>
        <p>Juror:--- The bridge has been a nuisance for many 11 years. I The Coroner said he passed over the bridge many times and was surprised that he was not holding in- quests every week on people who met their deaths on that bridge. A tremendous amount of traffic passed over it and on the Cheshire side several roads diverged from it. He was absolutely astonished. The other Sunday he saw two lady cyclists fall on the bridge and he thought there was going to be a terrible accident. He himself had had narrow shaves on the bridge. They were much obliged to Mrs. Jolley. Although he was extremely sorrylfor deceased's father he could not shut his eyes to the habits of lads when they came out of school. They were like bullets out of a gun and were across the street before one knew where they were. These accidents were bound to happen so long as there was the vivacity and life in English boys that there was at the present time. The Foreman of the jury said the jury were unani- mously of opinion that deceased's death was entirely due to ad accident, after the emphatic manner in which the last witness had given his evidence. They wished to express their sincere sympathy with the parents of the boy in their sad bereavement. No blame whatever was attached to Brereton. The Coroner said they were all sorry that their friend had lost his lad in the way he had. Neither the jury nor himself blamed Brereton, and he was sure the father would not. - .</p>
        <p>Much sympathy has been expressed with the parents in their sad loss, and deceased's school mates subscribed for a wreath as a token of affection.</p>
        <p>UP to the present Mr.. Chamberlan, -how- ever, has not abandoned his propoi:als to tax food. Instead of that, he is busy trying to shew how he can balance the hoinekeeping accounts by taking taxes off other things. I.3aid he: Suppoiin:z the tax on bread eosts the working man thretTenc6 More liar wcek, I can very easily make that up to him by taking the taxes off his tea, sugar, and tobacco. ..Now, can he ? The taxe3 that were put on these thinfrs were levied in order to raise money to carry on the. Government of the country. If Mr. Chamberlain is .!:oing to take them off, where is the ,Chancellor of the Exchequer to .get his money from ? Oh, says Mr. Chamber- lain, I will put a tan on foreign goods. But supposing he what w;11 happen ? The British peopitk will have to pay more for these goods: The money would have to come out. of their pockets all the same. What difference does it mike to them whether they pay taxes for the tea they drink or for the cups they drink the tea out of ?.</p>
        <p>HOUSekeepiiig. OF course the Colonial Secretary will not admit he is going to tax the poor. If a tax O n bread costs 3d. a. week, says he, we will ttAio .that 3d. off tea, sugar, and tobacco. But that is only half the story. In the first place, BE IS GOING TO TAX NOT ONLY BREAD BUT FOOD —that is meat, butter, pudding, cheese, fruit, &amp;c. But supposing he was going to tax bread only. can he balanc:e the account by taking the tax off -L.:a, sugar,4and tobAcco Our ruply is that it is impQssible to do so. Everybody, from the infant., bby or girl, to the old man or woman tottering to the grave, would tuffer by a bread tax. 'How many of them would benefit by a reduction in the cost of tobacco ? 'What difference will that make to the widow and her children ? Tobacco is not a food, and a reduction on its price cai not be balanced against the cost of food ? What about sugar ? Last year the Government put a tax on sugar to help pay for the war. This year the Government have shut out cheap sugar to help the West Indian planter! If Mr. Chamberlain takes the war tax off, it would on ;y just about balance the increase in cost owing to the Sugar Convention, and the price woula remain stationary. Thus tobacco and sugar will • produce no balance to set off agaii:st the food tax. Last of all we come to tea., The present tax on tea is 6d. per lb. But a considerable part of this is also really a war tax, and should be remitted at no distant date by the exercise of wise govern- ment and economy. Suppose that 3d. per lb. would remain which Mr. Chamberlain's scheme would enable him to remove, how much would this save the workman ? If he earns 20s. a week his wife may buy 4oz. a week. If he earns 255. to 30s. a week they may have half a pound. Now if all the duty were taken Off the man who_earns Ws. a week would save three farthings, and the man who earns 255. a week would get 10. This is not much to set against a tax on all foods, on bread, meat, butter, fruit, cheese, pudding, which would cost each family not less tbat is. per week. The loss of a few pence(; a week may seem a small matter to some. But in thousands of British homes it Means that somebody would have less food. The father of the family must have his food all the same, or he would break down. At least, so the wives of the workers often say. It is on the-women and the children the curse of dear food would ultimately fall.. Fancy an Empire which is so mighty, and yet, according to Mr. Chamber- lain, can only be kept together by a tax on the food of its people, by the hunger and want of its women and children! A pretty sort of Empire Mr. Chamberlain's policy has brought us to!</p>
        <p>A serious outbreak of typhoid fever has occurred in the Merthyr district, thirty patients baying been removed from their homes to the several isolation hospitals of the Merthyr District Council. One •of them, Miss Camille Williams, of Albert-street, where several other cases have occurred,died on Monday night. The medical officer, having made a thorough investiga- tion of thecircumstances, is convinced that the source of the epidemic was the consumption by the patients of contaminated cockles purchased in Merthyr market six weeks ago, but he cannot determine the source of the contamination.</p>
        <p>-.11117;1';</p>
