      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday September 19, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday September 19, 1903.</p>
        <p>IS A. FOOD TAX POPULAR?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>HOW IT WORKS OUT.</head>
        <p>HOW IT WORKS OUT.</p>
        <p>LIAS MR. CHAMBERLAIN ANOTHER</p>
        <p>PLAN ?</p>
        <p>Public Opinion. Pueuc opinion is decidedly against Mr. Chamberlain's proposals to tax food for the sake of the Colonies. In spite of his protests that he will not Increase the cost of food, the public will not accept his scheme at any price. The Unionist party and the Unionist press are sharply divided on the question. Even if there were a certain amount of popularity behind the food tax, Mr. Cham- berlain could not go on with a divided party. But when in addition to a divided party you add gteat unpopularity, it is clear that the chances of Mr. Chamberlain ever being able to persuade us to tax food are remote.</p>
        <p>Be on Your Guard.</p>
        <p>BUT because Mr. Chamberlain is apparently beaten over the food tax it is by no means certain that he will abandon his scheme. If he cannot get our direct consent to tax food he may try to secure our approval in a round- about way. But in the first place he will probably try to make us believe that he can tax our food without increasing the cost. He will be forced to do this because it is difficult to see how he can abandon his proposed food taxes. The sole reason and object of raising this question, he has told us, is to help the Colonies. Says he: We must give the Colonies a preference or lose them. To give them a preference we must put a tax upon food. If he drops the food tax, then he drops the Colonies as well. Some people think he will drop the food question and turn his attention to putting a tax on foreign manu- factures. But that cannot help the Colonies, or do anybody any good except a few manu- facturers. At the same time,Mr. Chamberlain 'knows full well that if we start d by putting a tax on manufactures we shouLd have to end by putting a tax on food. The farmers would immediately begin to cry out if they had to pay more for everything they bought but could get no more for what they sold. Itided, in common justice if you were to put a tax on foreign goods—that is to say, if we protected the British manufacturers against fcreign com- petition—we should have to protect the farmer as well. We must, therefore, be on our guard. We must recognise that it will be possible for Mr. Chamberlain to secure his tax on food by means of a preliminary tax on manufactured articles. We must face the fact that in certain manufacturing districts a policy of Protection against foreign goods, may be popular. Each industry would think of getting more money for the goods it !old, forgetting that it would have to pay more for all the good's it bought. Mr. Chamberlain knows a tax on. foreign goods might prove popular, and that once you started putting a tax on foreign goods you would very soon have to put a tax on the food as well. He is a clever electioneering band, and he may drop the food tax for the present, take up the cry of a tax on German goods, and hope to ride back into power on a strong wave of anti-foreign feeling. Once in power ,he would soon carry out his original scheme of putting a tax on food to give the Colonies a preference. We must, therefore, be on our guard, especially as we see Mr. Chamberlain trying each day to escape from the conse- quences of his food-tax proposals. It looks as though Mr. Chamberlain has another plan—to tax goods first and food afterwards.</p>
        <p>Taxes on the Poor.</p>
      </div>
