      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE.—On Saturday a girinamed Fisher had a narrow escape from drowning. She was gather- ing blackberries from a bush along the brook side, when she overbalanced herself and fell into the water. Owing to the recent heavy rain the brook was very much in flood, but singular to state, the girl managed to get to the bank and appeared none the worse for her im- mersion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WEDDING STOPPED AT THE ALTAR.</head>
        <p>A WEDDING STOPPED AT THE ALTAR.</p>
        <p>A romantic affair has just been brought to light at Belfast. A well-to-do widower, considerably advanced in years, some time ago fell in love with a lady of good appearance and address, who, it is said, is at least thirty -ears his junior. After a courtship of many months,the lady accepted an offer of marriagerveld arrangements were made for the wedding in one of the local churches. A house was taken and furnished,and everything seemed to be running smoothly until the morning fixed for the ceremony arrived, when a surprise took place. All the parties had assembled at the altar and just at the moment when the officiating clergyman, had invited any person who could show "just cause and impediment" to the union to speak, a figure emerged from the vestry and forbade the ceremony. In reply to a question from the clergyman, the newcomer said he was the sort of the bridegroom, to whom he advanced, indplacing his hand on his shoulder, formally objected to tne marriage. The clergyman pointed out that he had no legal right, to intervene in such a fashion, and cautioned him against continuing to do so. The son, however, held strong views as to the duty which he owed to his father in the situation, and insisted upon the latter's immediate withdrawal. The father then left the sacred edifice. Friends consoled the young lady. IS is rumoured that legal proceedings may follow.</p>
        <p>Charles Whittaker, fish dealer, stated that it was usual to buy whiting by sample. The Chairman said the Bench would give the defendant the benefit of the doubt, but they thought he had asted foolishly in not looking at the fish when he bought them. The case would be dismissed on payment of oosts.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. Wilson, the Bench intimated that they thought Inspector Flood acted rightly in seizing the fish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WARRINGTON FISH HAWKER ! SUMMONED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FISH HAWKER ! SUMMONED.</p>
        <p>What he was Liable to.</p>
        <p>THE BENEFIT OF THE DOUBT.</p>
        <p>Peter Cumerford, fish hawker, 56, Pinner's Brow, ap- peared before the magistrates at the Warrington Police Court on Monday in answer to a summons charging him with exposing for sale in Liverpool road on September sth a quantity of fish which was unfit for human food. Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk) conducted the prosecution, and Mr. T. S. Steel represented the defendant.</p>
        <p>In relating the facts of the case, Mr. Wilson said that at 10.4.5 on the night in question P. Cs Woods and Sankey were on duty in Liverpool road when they received a complaint, in consequence of which they wont up to the defendant, who was in the roadway with a handcart, containing fish. They at once detected a very offensive smell coming from the cart. After looking at the fish they asked the defendant if he was trying to sell it. He replied "Yes, if I can." The officers described the fish as being "almost rotten.." Defendant admitted Pint it was not very good. They suggested that defendant should bring the fish to the Police Station where Inspector Flood could examine it, and he agreed to that course. Consequently the fish was brought to the Police Station and examined by the Inspector. He described 244 whiting, which were in a box, as being in the early stages of putrefaction, and there was an offensive smell arising from the fish which were quite unfit for human food. Inspector Flood told the defendant he would seize the fish, and defendant said: "I know they are bad; but I have not offered them for sale." P.C. Wood asked defendant why he was shouting out" Whiting" in the streets along with the other fish, and he replied that he had got so used to calling out that he "did not take any notice of the fish he called." On the following day the fish were examined by a justice of the peace who gave an order for their destruction. Defendant had rendered himself liable to a penalty of £4,880—£20 for each fish. P.C. Woods gave evidence and said the heads were nearly dropping off the fish.—ln answer to Mr. Steel, witness said he was not an assistant inspector under the Public Health Act. The box containing the whiting was three parts full, and the lid was partly closed. P.C. Sankey said the defenilant stated that he had been trying to sell the fish, but he had not sold any. Inspector Flood stated that he examined the fish, and the whiting were rotten, especially those at the bottom of the box. The heads were dropping off. Defendant stated that he had been taking the fish round. to sell it, but on being further questioned he said he did not offer the whiting for sale. • Mr. Steel submitted that the seizure was wrong. The Act of Parliament said that the seizure must take place by an inspectorlunder the Public Health Act or his assistant, and that it must take place when the fish was exposed for sale or deposited in a place for the purpose of sale. They had it from the evidence'that the fish was found at Sankey Bridges by two constables, and they had no right to seize it beeause they were not inspectors or assistants. They asked the defendant to go down to the Police Station and he went. The Bench could not hold that the fish was exposed for sale while it was on the handcart outside the Police Station. He submitted that the seizure was wrong, as the police should have kept the defendant at Sankey Bridges and sent for the inspector. The summons must fail on that ground. Mr. Wilson said the constables were quite within their rights because section 28 of the Public Health Amend- ment Act of 1890 empowered any person to apply for the condemnation of any unwholesome food. He sub- mitted that there was no seizure until the defeadant came down, of his own free will, to the Police Station. The seizure really took place when the inspector arrived.</p>
        <p>The Bench ruled against Mr. Steel, who then sub- mitted that defendant had no intention of exposing the fish for sale. He had been a licensed fish hawker for about four months, and on the Saturday morning he bought the fish in question from the market. The first place he visited was Sharp-street, where he opened the box of whiting and found they were unfit for food. He at once put on the lid again, and he (Mr. Steel) would call a customer to whom defendant refused to sell the whiting because they were not fit for food. He thought that would satisfy their Worships that he did not ex- pose the whiting for sale. Not one whiting was sold. Again, the case would not have been heard of had it not been for a complaint made by a man to P.C. Wood about his wife having purchased some bad mackerel. On inquiries being made the woman herself stated that de- fendant was not the man from whom she purchased them.</p>
        <p>Defendant gave evidence in support of his solicitor's statement, and pointed out that it was customary to buy fish by sample. That was the reason the box was not opened at the market. John Pownall, Lord Nelson-street, who accompanied defendant on his rounds, corroborated. Samuel Dolphin, 84, Winwick-road, said defendant told him he would not sell the whiting as they were not tit to eat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LIBERALISM AT WIGAN.</head>
        <p>LIBERALISM AT WIGAN.</p>
        <p>The announcement of Colonel W. Woods, J.P., that he will not stand as Liberal candidate for Wigan so long as the Liberal party in the town is divided into two camps, has set the whole town agog. The Colonel was the candidate of the Liberal party at the election in 1900, when he was defeated by Sir F. S. Powell by over 600 votes. Since then he has always been re- garded as the Liberal candidate for Wigan at the next election, and his decision has caused much surprise. This is not the first time Colonel Woods has issued a sensational placard. When he was Mayor of Wigan in 1892 he addressed a letterfrom Whitley Hall to the Town Clerk resigning the office. The letter was printed and posted on the walls of Wigan, and the people learned that Mr. Woods, the Conservative Mayor, was unable to sup- port the policy of the Wigan Conservatives in their determination to resist a legislative eight hours day, a question which he believed to be of the most vital interest to the Wigan district, Elections were they pending in Wigan and in the Ince Division, and Sam Woods won the seat for Labour in Inca and Thomas AspinwaLl pulled Sir F. S. Powell's majority down to 110. As Mayor, the Colonel would have had a casting vote had the polling been equal, and in a letter of explanation he asked what would Wigan have said if he had given that vote to the candidate who advocated legislative limita- tiun of hours,q.which, with his views on Labour,ihe cer- tainly should have done. Colonel Woods is the son of the late Mr. Henry Woods, cotton spinner, Wigan, who, along with the late Mr. John Lancaster, won Wigan for the Liberal party in 1868. Unlike the father, the son identified himself with the Conservative party till the Labour movement became prominent. Though a capitalist himself, being connected with collieries, he championed the miners in their demand for an eight hours day, and this led to him finally severing himself from the Conservative party and throwing ill his lot with the party for which his father foughtVeEtions on several occasions. During the war in South Africa, Colonel Woods, who commands a Hampshire horse regiment, equipped a troop for active service.</p>
