      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must Mall -cases Bead their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only-of the paper and sent, in, if possible,not later than Thursday. _ _</p>
        <p>Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly A short letter has a much greater chance of insertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinary circumstances undertake to return themanuscripts which are not used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Corporal Punishment in Schools.</head>
        <p>Corporal Punishment in Schools.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Great interest has been aroused on the question ~of corporal punishnient in schools, by a report in the Press of a charge of assault brought against a teacherin one of the schools at Birkenhead.</p>
        <p>It cannot be denied that there is a great desire on the part of parents and members of Education Committees, to have cerporal,punishment abolished from the schools, as it is in almost every other civilised country; and whilst many teachers have abandoned its use in British -schools, many yet declare that it is impossible to main- tain discipline without it.</p>
        <p>Such a statement is generally made by teachers who are unaware that this difficulty has been practically solved by the adoption of the School City System of maintaining discipline. From every point of view, this -satisfactory solution has absolutely proved that it is easily possible to maintain discipline without the use of corporal punishment, as well as to impress thepupils with the highest duties of citizenship and good conduct. I shall be pleased to send a copy of reports of this -system of maintaining discipline in schools (by school- masters and schoolmistresses) to any wishing the same on receipt of stamped and addressed envelope.— I am, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>LLEWELLYN W. WILLIAMS, B.Sc. Hon. Sec. of the Society for the Reform of School Discipline. 3, Park Terrace, Crosshill, Glasgow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Education Act and Passive Resistance.</head>
        <p>The Education Act and Passive Resistance.</p>
        <p>Sir,—A State-made and State-endowed human system of religion in the midst of a free people holding different faiths is a grievous anomaly, and a standing offence and menace to all who, by blessed experience, have learned that where the Spirit of the Lord is there is liberty. Such is the so-called Church of England—a dominant sect ever on the move to extend its dominion by invading the rights and liberties of those who reject its authority and eschew its questionable doctrines. The latest development of the unrighteousness which underlies this activity is the new Ellucation Act which imposes Sectarian tests upon the teachers in our schools, and compels dissenters of all kinds to pay for the teach- ing of the Church Catechism. This is manifestly a great injustice, and I frilly sympathise with all who feel aggrieved by it. But I cannot join the "Bassive Resisters" in their refusal to pay the school .rate because an infinitely nobler example than theirs compels me to obey the law. And this I find in no less a personage than the Founder of Christianity Himself, an authority conclusive in all matters pertaining to the gospel. In the days when Jesus lived, His country was under the rule of a foreign power, and the Jews were compelled to pay taxes to support a Roman govern- ment and Roman idolatry. And here a most important question arises, the true answer to which is dead against all lawbreakers. Did the Saviour who was brought up mall the traditions and hopes of His people, and was endowed with the purest conscience that ever regulated the actions of &amp;human being, put Himself in opposition to the Roman law and command His fol- lowers to do likewise? No. On the contrary, He will- ingly paid the tribute •demanded of Him as a Jew, and exercised His omniscience to procure the money, although .He knew the cruel and unholy purposes to which it would be applied. In the light of this incident then is it conceivable that the same Jesus would act differently were He Amongst us now? 'Would He jbin the "Passive Resisters" in opposing an English Act of Parliament, passed by men bearing His name, not for pagan pur- poses, but for the education of our children, even although it contained clauses that were objectionable to certain sections of the people? The only answer that can be honestly given to this question is, emphatically, No. Then why do the " Passive Resistors" refuse to follow their Master's leading in rendering unto Cmsar the things that are Cmsar's ? Is it because they have a liner moral sense than He had,"and can detect a greater iniquity in a rate laid presumably\ in the interest of Anglicanism than in a tax for maintaining an idolatrous Government? As this question cannot possibly .be answered in the affirmative, there must be somewhere else cause for the passive resistance movement. Might I venture to suggest that possibly pastoral agitationand a peculiar view of the spirit and piirpose of the self-denying and unresisting Saviour of the world may have something te do with it. There is, however, one ,case in which it would be not only lawful but imperative for Christians to resist "the powers that be." Such a case would arise were the State and its priests to ignore God and command the people to burn incense to Csar. Then the conscientious objectors would have ample .opportunity, not only for tasting prison life," but for resisting the impious ordinance even unto death. How many of the opponents of the school rate would be pro- pared branch an ordeal is known only to the Searcher hearts.—Yours truly, September, 1903.</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>MUNICIPAL TRADING. THE INVESTIGATION. First Meeting of the Commissioners.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL TRADING. THE INVESTIGATION. First Meeting of the Commissioners.</p>
        <p>The inquiry into the trading transactions of the Warrington Corporation was commenced on Wednes- tlay at the Town Haal. The Commissioners present were:: Messrs. T. Birchall, J.P., A. J. Jolley, and L. -Greening (representing the Town Council), and Messrs. Norbury Williams, J. Pemberton and A. Horrocks (representing the Municipal Non-Trading Association). The proceedings were strictly -private, but we understand that the Commissioners were chiefly occu- pied in the appointment of official shorthand writers and defining their duties. We regret to learn that the proceedings were interrupted through one of the Commiseioners (Mr. Pemberton) being seized With a fit. He has been out of town since for the benefit .0f his health and is now much.better. The next meeting of the Commissioners will be held on Titesdri. There will be three sittings next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>AN INQUIRY DEMANDED AT</head>
        <p>AN INQUIRY DEMANDED AT</p>
        <p>MACCLESFIELD.</p>
        <p>kl The elective auditor of Macclesfield has discovered what appears to 'of) either a most serious affair or a mare's nest. Recently in going through the accounts of the Highways Committee the elective auditor states that he came across an item of £24 for repairs to cer- tain public steps in the town, and he alleges that the work was not carried out at the steps, but to certain private propetty owned by a well-known gentleman in the town. He demanded an inquiry, but no reply was made to his demand._</p>
        <p>Tho Macclesfield Property Owners' Association, a, 'rather powerful, if quiet, organisation, have been dis- cussing the matter, and are supporting the elective auditor in asking for an inquiry, and their support will take the form of pecuniary aid in having the matter 'ventilated. _</p>
        <p>- lt is believed that, failing a corporate inquiry, an application will be made to the Local Government Board to appoint a commissioner to investigate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>REVIVING WEST AUSTRALIANS.</head>
        <p>REVIVING WEST AUSTRALIANS.</p>
        <p>One of the features in Westralians, says " Money " has been the renewed demand which has sprung up for Fraser South Extended; the August return is a very good one, showing a value of £2,400. When the pro- posedadditions to the cyanide plant are purchased and erected, the net monthly profit will be increased by at ! least £5OO, which'slaould put the company in a position , to pay dividends. Under these circumstaeees it is not surprising that there has been some steady buying of the shares, and a purchase at the present price of , should show an ample prafit in the comae of the next I two or three months.</p>
        <p>Bfr. Balfour glances at another kind of injury which tariffs,workinginalliance with modern methods of trade combination, may inflict. He instances the case of any manufacturers who have, with the help of Protection, obtained a command of their hOme market. They are, he points out, at an immense advantage compared with their rivals in a Free Trade country. The unprotected manufactureris compelled either to restrict his plant to a point well within what sometimes may be required of it. or in ordinary times to leave it partially idle. Even a Small excess of supply may lowerthe price of his goods out of all proportion, and if it does he not only loses heavily in respect of this small margin of over produc- Local Shepherdry. I tion, but in respect of his whole output. The Premier regards it as worthy of note that in On Saturday the quarterly meeting of the Late 'theory it is not only possible that the foreign prices Spring Rose Lodge, 828, was held at the Barley Mow charged by the quas-monopolist should be less than the Inn. The Secretary, Bro. P.M. Joseph Booth, in pre- home prices, but even that they should be less than the seating the quarterly balance sheet- informed the cost of production, and it has often been so inpractice. members that it was a pleasure to him to be in a German steel, for Instance, has been sold in this country. position to inform them that the past quarter had been at a price for which no English manufacturer could pro- duce it. He was told the other day of a shipbuilder a great success, for after paying the sum £lO2 Bs. 11d, who was able to obtain contracts solely because he had for sick pay and other levies, they had been able to secured a consignment of German steel at a price lower add to their funds the sum of £5l 15s. 21d., making than it could possibly have cost either to a British or a the total worth of the lodge £2,032 4s. 3d., with a total German ironneaster. Why should we refuse to our ship- membership of 406. After the us