      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>MR. BALFOUR'S TARIFF VIEWS. MANIFESTO 01 THE FIS:AL</head>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR'S TARIFF VIEWS. MANIFESTO 01 THE FIS:AL</p>
        <p>PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Retaliation Recommended.</p>
        <p>We understand that the Prime Minister circulated to his colleagues in the early days of August a series of economic notes on the subject of insular Free Trade. Thinking that perhaps a larger circle than that for which the paper was originally written might like to have the opportunity of reading it, Mr. Balfour decided upon publishing it, and Messrs. Longmans, Green, and Co.on Tuesday evening furnished an advance copy of the pamphlet, which,is dated "Downing-street, September, 1903." A few alterations have been made, "chiefly," says the right hon. gentleman, "for the sake of clear- ness," and two brief illustrative tables have been added in an appendix showing (1) the amount of exports to protected and unprotected markets of all articles of British production, except coals, machinery, and ships, in the years 1880, 1890, 1900, and 1902; and (2) the value of the exports in 1881, 1886, 1891, 1896, and 1901. The importance of rhe present publication is increased by the fact that it is issued immediately after the Cabinet discussed the fiscal question. Mr. Balfour admits that his arguments are occasion- ally abstract and occasionally somewhat compressed, but he points out that it is one of the difficulties inherent in the present controversy that some import- ant points can hardly be made interesting, or even :intelligible, on the platform without an amount of expansion and illustration which would render it impossible to put them in their proper setting within ' the limits of a single speech. The more general, and in some respects more theoretical treatment of the prob- i lam attempted in the essay now published may, there- fore, in Mr. Balfour's opinion, be a fitting preliminary to the statement whichit will be his duty in a few days to make before a public audience—an obvious reference to the speech he has promised to deliver at Sheffield at the annual meeting of the National Union of Conser- vative Associations on Thursday, October 1. "A FREE TRADER, Bur-."</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour asks his readers to premise that he approaches the subject from the Free Trade point of view though he remarks:— "The Free Trade is perhaps not always that which passes for orthodox in the House of Commonsor on the platform. There is, indeed, a real danger of the controversy 'degenerating into an unprofitable battle of watchwords besides which there is nothing to be called independent reflection at all. •• Popular disputation insists on labels,and likes 'lts labels old. It therefore divides the world for purposes of fiscal policy into Protectionists and Free-Traders. Those who are Protectionist, are assumed to be Protectionists after the manner of Lord George Bentinck. Those who are Free Traders are assumed to be Free-Traders after the manner of Mr. Cobden. Does a man question the dogma that taxation mast always be for revenue? Then evi- dently he hankers after the fiscal system of 1841 anda twenty-shilling duty on corn. Does he admire the tariff reform of sixty years ago? Then evi- dently- he regards the simple and unqualified doctrine of Free Trade as so fundamental in its character, so universal in its application, so capable of exact expression, that every conclusion to which it logically leads must be accepted without hesitation or reserve. I am a Free Trader, but not, it must be acknowledged, precisely after this pattern." THE nom OF '46. •</p>
        <p>The Premier starts with a general proposition on economic forces. He holds that nations, as we know them, are economically possible only because, for various reasons, mankind is both unable and unwilling to turn the natural resources of the world to the best economic account. He recognises in the fight which, so far as Great Britain is concerned, came to an end in 1846, not a fight over an economic theory merely, but a struggle between two opposing ideals supported by two rival interests. Was this country—such was the ques- tion at that time—to become a more and more manu- facturing community or was agriculture to be maintained, at whatever cost, in its ancient predomin- ance. His view is that the country rightly decided in favour of the first of these alternatives. The conclusive argument in its favour, to his mind, is that a predominantly agricultural Britain could neither have supported the men nor furnished the money necessary to her Imperial Mission. COBDEN'S UNFULFILLED PROPHECY.</p>
        <p>The manufacturing ideal is, however, only tolerable and realisable if two conditions are satisfied. The first ,1 of these is that, as luxuries and necessities must bet imported, a large export trade is necessary in order tha these things shall be paid for; and the second is that sufficient capital shall always be forthcoming for home investments, so that this end may be attained and employment provided for our growing urban population No anxious thoughts were suggested by these corollaries to those who worked in the cause of fiscal reform half a century ago, but Mr. Balfour points to what he con- ceives to have been two mistakes on their part,neither of which would have perhaps greatly mattered withoutthe other. "They failed to foresee that the world woul reject Free Trade, and they failed to take full account of the commercial possibilities of the British Empire. If they had been right on the first point—if Free Trade had indeed become a universal creed—no controversy about our commercial rela- tions with any fiscally independent community could possibly have arisen. If, on the other hand; they had succeeded in giving us Imperial Free Trade the Protective tendencies of foreign nations would in the long runhave been but of secondary importance. The double error has established insular Free Trade, with its inevitable imitations, and left its bearing all the burden but enjoying only half the advantages which should attach to empire." The line of thought here indicated leads Mr. Balfour up to the salient question "whether a fiscal system suited to a Free Trade nation in a world of Free Traders remains suited in every detail to a Free Trade nation in a world of Protectionists." He repeats that he approaches the question as a Free Trader, with the desire to promote Free Trade as far as contemporary circumstances permit, and he throws no doubt on the Free Trade theory when expressed with due limitations. _ OUR EXPORT TRADE. _</p>
        <p>Discussing variations of the problem now before the country and citing one or two imaginary cases of economic dependence, the right hon. gentleman urges that in actual fact Great Britain is seen hampered by foreign tariffs, yetable in spite of them, to carry on an export trade which increases rather than diminishes, and an import trade of unexampled magnitude. But the reasons adduced why Great Britain holds her place are that foreign countries owe us a great deal of money (not freights or commissions), which they pay by means of imports into the United Kingdom. Large areas remain which are not protected at all, and exist- ing protected areas are not completely protected. But the point to be considered here is not the mone- tary position of trade, but its tendency absolutely. Our export trade may have increased, but its rate of increase has on the whole seriously diminished. Insome import- ant departments no increase is perceptible, and this is food for thought. In others there are symptoms of decay. If the bide of international commerce is not following in proportion to the growth of wealth and population, is it because of the operation of some inevi- table law? Mr. Balfour thinks not, but because it has been made impossible by the operation of hostile tariffs. "National industries (he goes on to say) have not been allowed to become mutually Isupplemen- tary. They have been compelled to become mutu- ally exclusive. Fiscal contrivances have forced them out of co-operative into competitivechannels. That this is the true theory on ordinary Free Trade principles it seems to me impossible to deny." Is there reason to anticipate any improvement? Mr. Balfour sees no satisfactory symptoms, while the less developed Protectionist communities like Russia and some of our own self-governing colonies are busily occu- pied in buiping up protected interests within their borders, a process doubtless costly to them, but not on that account 'less &amp;ilia's' to us. The injury tends t o become a double one, and why ? For the reason that the effect of any artificial stimulus to manufacturers in a country like the United States of America or Russia or Canada is to antedate the period when their food supplies will be required for international consumption. THE "DUMPING" SYSTEM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday September 19 1903</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday September 19 1903</p>
        <p>points on which I have commented in these notes— the injury which foreign Protection is calculatbd to inflict on a Free Trade country. its needs for open markets the threatened contraction of existing Free trade areas, the increasing severity of tariffs in Protectionist areas, the building up of vested pro- i tected interest innew countries, which may be dis- couraged now but not hereafter; the effect of this protection on our future cornsupply,the uncertainty andloss which tariff-protected trusts are inflicting and may hereafter inflict upon British capital invested in Britain—one and all of these evils' actual and prospective are due to Protection. The man who says that their cumulative effect is so small asto ba negligible canhardly describ3 himself as a free trader—at least, he can attach but a very small value to Free Trade. The man who,admitting their reality, does not anticipate their increase has (it seems to me) not learned the lesson which theory and experience agree in teaching. The man who admits their present realityand the probability- of their increase, and yet is too contentedly pros- perous even to consider whether any mitigation is practicable appears little short ot reckless. "I corniest accept any of these positions. It seems to me clear that we are bound to seek for some mitigation and that in one direction only can we hope to find it. The source of all the difficulty being protective tariffs imposed by fiscally independent communities, it is plain that we can secure no concession in the direction of a free exchange ex- cept by negotiation, and that our negotiators can but appeal to self interest or, in the case of our colonies, to self interest and sentiment combined. Now, on the Free Trade theory, self-interest should have prevented these tariffs being origi- nally imposed, but it did not and if argument failed before powerful vested interests were created, it, is hardly likely to be effective now. The only alter- native is to do to foreign nations what they always do to each other, and, instead of annealing to econ- omic theories in which they wholly disbelieve, to use fiscal inducements which they thoroughly under- stand.</p>
        <p>"We, and we along among the nations, are unable to employ this mews of persuasion not became in our hands it need be ineffectual, but because in obedience to 'principle' we have deliberately thrown it away."</p>
        <p>of the 9th, which I received shortly before my depar- ture from Scotland for the Cabinet meeting, as I knew that we should within a few hours have an opportunity of talking over the important issues with which it deals. The reply, therefore, which I am now writing rather embodies the results of our conversations than adds to them anything which is new. Agreeing as Ido with you that the time has come when a change should be made in the fiscal canons by which we have bound ourselves in our commercial dealings with other Governments, it seems paradoxical indeed that you should leave the Cabinet at the same time that others of ray colleagues are leaving it who disagree on the very point with us both. Yet I cannot but admit, however reluctantly, that there is some force in the arguments with which you support that course, based as they are upon your special and personal relation to that portion of the controversy which deals with colonial preference. You have done more than any man, living or dead, to bring home to the citizens of the Empire the conscious- ness of Imperial obligation and the interdependence be- tween the various fragments into which the Empire is geographically divided. I believe you to be right in holding that this interdependence should find expression in our commercial relations as well as in our political and military relations. I believe with you that closer fiscal union between the mother country and her colonies would be good for the trade of both, and that if such closer union could be establishedon fitting terms its advantages to both parties would increase as the years went on and as the coloiiies grew in wealth and population. If there ever has been any difference between us in connection with this matter it has only been with regard to the practic- ability of a proposal which would seem to require on the part of the colonies a limitation in the all-round development of a protective policy and on the part of this country the establishment of a preference in favour of important colonial products. On the first of these requirements I say nothing, but if the second involves, as it almost certainly does, tax- ation, however light, upon foodstuffs, lam convinced with you that public opinion is not yet ripe for suchan arrangement. The reasons may easily be found in past political battles and present political mis- representations. If, then, this branch of fiscal reform is not at present within the limits of practical politics, you are surely right in your advice not to treat it as indissolubly connected with that other branch of fiscal reform to which we both attach importance, and which we believe the country is prepared to consider without prejudice. I feel, however, deeply concerned that you should regard this conclusion however well founded, as one which makes it difficult for you, in your very special circum- stances, to remain a member of the Government. Yet I do not venture inn matter so strictly personal to raise any objection. If you think you can best serve the interests of Imperial unity, for whichyou have done so much, by pressing your views on colonial preference with the freedom which is possible in an independent position but is hardly compatible with office, how can I criticise your determination?</p>
        <p>The loss't-o the Government is great indeed, but the gain to the cause you have atheart may be greater still. If so, what can I do- but acquiesce ?—Yours very sincerely,</p>
        <p>ARTHUR JAMES BALFOUR. P.S.—May I say with what gratification, both on personal and on public grounds, I learn that Mr. Austen Chamberlain is ready to remain a member of the Government? There could be no more conclusive evi- dence that, in your judgment as in mine, the exclusion of taxation on food from the party programme is in existing circumstances the course best fitted practically to further the cause of fiscal reform.</p>
        <p>prices. It is well-known that from 1898 to 1900 the German and Belgian works were fully occupied with</p>
        <p>their own home trade, and had no surplus material to dispose of. It was this, coupled with the general im- provement in trade which enabled all the English works to run full time, at increased prices, and show such a satisfactory result in the way of profits. Again, the writer says," the iron and steal industries of the country have no serious complaint to make as to present prosperity." I have no hesitation in saying that when the figures for last year are available it will be found that the profits made were' no larger, if indeed so large as in the three years 1893-4 to J. 895-6. Both last year and this have seen the worst the trade has experienced since 1886-7, and to a large extent, if not indeed almost entirely, the depres3inn has been due to the large importation of Belgian and German material. What makes the position so mach worse is the fact that German makers are'assured a profit upon what they sell at home by their protective-duties, and part of this profit they are willing to sacrifice on their sales in this country, thus making the price lower than would other- wise be the case. In this way English works are pre- vented from getting a fair price even for their reduced Output. Take the case of my own trade. The quantity of bar, angle, bolt, and rod iron imported last year was 171,915 tons, while the total exports of the same articles were only 125,063 tons. The makers could therefore well afford to lose the export trade in these goods if they were given a fair chance of the home trade, and the same thing applies in other branches of industry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>"AN ACCURSED THING." . _</head>
        <p>"AN ACCURSED THING." . _</p>
        <p>The "principle "to which we pay this strangely in- congruous tribute is, of course, the principle of Free Trade," but the right hon. gentleman asserts that a curious view of Free Trade is implied simply because fiscal inducement my, if it fails in its effect and not otherwise, involve duties not required for revenue pur- poses, or, in certain cases, even carry with it some element Of protection to _home industries. . . .</p>
        <p>The nation is invited, he says, to turn away from it as from an accursed thing. This has seemed to him extraordinarily foolish and quite inconsistent with rational Free Trade. By only one standard can the Free Trade merits of any policy be judged, and that is the degree to which it promotes Free Trade. "Thou shalt not tax except for revenue "is held by some to be a moral imperative, and could it be proved to those who took this standpoint that by risking the imposition of the most trifling protective tariff at home it was possible to secure the greatest relaxation of protective tariffs abroad they would only answer," We must not do ill that good may come." To the writer this atti- tude of mind seems absurd. He holds himself to be in harmony with the true spirit of Free Trade when he pleads for freedom to negotiate that freedom of ex- change may be increased. It ought not to be difficult to devise a method of turning it to the most useful account. Finally, says Mr. Balfour, "Were I proved to be wrong, my opinion on the fundamental question would remain unchanged. Where we fail others may succeed. It cannot be right for a country with Free-Trade ideas to enter into competition with Protectionist rivals, self- deprived of the only instrument by which tlaeir policy can conceivably be modified. The first and most essential object of our national efforts should be to get rid of the bonds in which we have gratui- tously entangled ourselves. The preoise manner in which we should use our regained liberty is an important, yet, after all, only a secondary issue. What is fundamental is that our liberty should be regain6d."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>BREAK-UP OF THE CABINET.</head>
        <p>BREAK-UP OF THE CABINET.</p>
        <p>THREE RESIGNATIONS.</p>
        <p>The following official announcement was made on Thursday.night Mr. CIMIBERIAIN, Mr. RITCHIE, and L 3rd GEORGE HA3IfLTON have resigned their seats in the Cabinet. Their resignations have been accepted by the PREIH Mt and the KING." R. CHAMBERLAIN'S REASONS FOR RESIGNING.</p>
        <p>The accompanying correspondence has passed between the Prime Minister and Mr. Chamberlain :</p>
        <p>Highbury, Birmingham, September 9th, 1903. My dear Balfour,—ln anticipation of the important Cabinet which is to meet on Monday I have most care- fully considered the present situation as it affects the Government and also the great question of fiscal reform. When you, in replying to the deputation on the Corn Tax, and I, in addressin&lt;, my oonstitnantg at Birming- ham, called attention to the changes that had taken place in our commercial position during the last fifty years and suggestedan inquiry into the subject I do not think that either of us intended to provoke a purely party controversy. We raised, not for the first time, a ques- tion of the greatest national and Imperial importance, in the hope that it would be discussed with a certain impartiality by both friends and opponents, and that the Inquiry thus initiated might lead to conclusions accepted by a majority of the people of this country and represented accordingly in the results of the next generakelection.</p>
        <p>Whether our view was reasonable or not, it was cer- tainly not shared by the leaders of the Liberal party. From the first they scouted the idea that a system which was generally accepted in 1843 could possibly requireany modification in 1903, and the whole resourcee of the party organisations were brought into play against any attempt to alter or even to inquire into the foundatiens of our existing fiscal policy. Meanwhile the advocates of reconsideration were at a great disadvantage. Owing to admitted differences of opinion in the Unionist party the political organisa- tions of the party were paralysed. and our opponents have had full possession of the field. They have placed in the forefront of their argument their objection to the taxation of food, and even to any readjustment of the existing taxation with a view of securing the mutual advantage'of ourselves and our colonies and the closer union of the different parts of the Empire. A somewhat unscrupulous use has been made of the old cry of the Dear Loaf, and in the absence of any full public discussion of the question I recognise that serious prejudice has been created, and that while the people generally are alive to the danger of unrestricted competition on the part of those foreign countries that close their markets to us while finding in our market an outlet tor their surplus production, they have not yet appreciated the importance to our trade of colonial markets, nor the danger of losing theta if we do not meet in some way their natural and patriotic desire for preferential trade. The result is that, for the present at any rate, a pre- ferential agreement with our colonies involving any new duty., however small, on articles of food hitherto untaxed is, even if accompanied by a reduction of taxa- tion on other articles of food of equally universal con- sumption, unacceptable to themajority in the constitu- encies. However much we may regret their decision, and however mistaken we may think it to be, no Govern- Iment in a. democratic country can ignore it. I feel,therefore, that as an immldiate and practical policy the question of preference to the colonies cannot he pressed with any, hope of success at the present time, although there is a very strong feeling in favour of the other branch of fiscal reform, which would give a fuller discretion to the Government in negotiating with foreign countries for freer exchange of commo- di ties, and would enable our representatives to retaliate if no concession were made to our just claims for greater reciprocity. If, as I believe, you share these views, it seems to me that youwill be absolutely justified in adopting them as the policy of your Government, although it will necessarilyinvolve some changes in its constitution. As Secretary of State for the Colonies during the last eight years I have been in a special sense the representa- tive of the policy of closer union, which I firmly believe is equallynecessars in the interests of the colonies and of ourselves, and I believe that it is possible to-day— and may be impossible to-morrow—to make arrange- ments for such a union. I have had unexampled oppor- tunities of watching the trend of events and of appreci- ating the feelings of our kinsmen beyond the seas. I stand, therefore,in a different position to thatTof any of my colleagues,and I think I should be justly blamed if I remained in office, and thus formally accepted the exclusion from my political programme of so important a part of it. I think that,with absolute loyalty to your Government and its general policy, and with no fear of embarrassing it in any way, I can best promote the ; cause I have at heart from outside,and I cannot buthope 'that, in a perfectly independent positioi, my arguments may be received with less prejudice than would attach to those of a party leader. Accordingly I suggest that you should limit the present policy of the Government to the assertion of i our freedom n the case of all commercial relations with foreign countries, and that you should agree to my tendering my resignation of my present office to his Majesty and devoting myself to the work of explaining and popularising those principles of Imperial union which my experience has convinced me are essential to our future welfare and prosperity.—Yours very sin- ' cerely, J. CHAMBERLAIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>PREMIER'S REPLY.</head>
        <p>THE PREMIER'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>: Mr. Balfour sent the following reply, which it will be seen is dated a week later :</p>
        <p>16, Downing-street. Whitehall, 5.W.,11 September 16, 1903. My dear Chamberlain,—l did not ansvter your letter</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>LOCAL OPINIONS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL OPINIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. Guest and the Cabinet Crisis.</p>
        <p>M. E. J. GUEST writes :</p>
        <p>I have not had leisure to grasp Mr. Balfour's manifesto and do not think that I should be justified in attempting to deal with it, and I am content to take the judgment of those who ara in a better position to point out its fallacies. I have an impressionthat Mr. Balfottr is too academic and too sentimental to lead the nation in such an important question as the fiscal policy of the country. As a than of business he is not comparable to Mr. Chamberlain whose training for many years and whose ability have fitted hint to make a pronouncement on matter aof Trade and Commerce. It is Mr. Chamber airt's strength in training and ability that has given such weight to the policy that he has foreshadowed,and which has as it seems to me led Mr. Balfour astray. Unfor- tunately for Mr. Balfour and others who have been misled by the astuteness of the Colonial Secretary Mr. Chamberlain's stability is not as great as his acute- ness, and he seems ever to be shifting his position. And I do not wonder inasmuch as the work he has set before himself is totally beyond even his power to accomplish. Some people appear to have an idea that when he de- clares himself more fully he will show us how it can be done; but I have no such expectation. have been much interested in reading the able letters which have appeared in your columns from the pens of Messrs. A. H. Crosfield, Path!' Ryland&amp; C. E. Parlzar and otke,r. and have endeavoured to sift the evidence for and ' against Free Trade. I can of course see that the " dump- ing " down into this country of goods at less than cost price has a very disturbing effect on manufacturers who cannot compete without serious loss. And I can quite understand that such manufacturers looking to their own interests alone hail Mr. Chamberlain as aprotector and benefactor. But the question is one that must be looked at as it affects thelwell-beingnot ofsay 25 per cent of the Community but of the other 75 per cent. as well. The issue, of the Government fiscal inquiry has in a succinet manner set besore the country a comprehensive survey of thelnation's trade. To those who would like England to monopolise the trade of the world, it may not be pleasant to read of the fierce competition to which we are exposed by the United States, by Ger- many and other countries. Competition may not be pleasant, hub it cannot be avoided, but must be met not by artificial walls of Protection; but by knowledge, by skill, by energy, and by adaptability. To foster a spirit of jealousy because other countries areprospering is not worthy of Englishmen. It is not only unworthy it is unwise. it appears to me that not only are indi- viduals in the sanae community bound by mutual dependence, but nations may be mutually helpful. Tf so then the prosperity of the United States and of Ger- many will be helpful to England. Let 'us put' away jealousy and see wherein their strength lies, imitating. them as far as in us lies mall that will fit us to compete with them ; not with the view of hindering their pros- perity, but whilst wishing theta well to seek our own well-being. In my opinion their prosperity is not due to Protection. And our Free Trade has not hindered, but has materially assisted us in doing as well as we have done. "As well as we have done."</p>
        <p>The followers of Mr. Chamberlain seem to ignore the wonderful advance that has been made by this country in wea,lthand in comfort duringlhe past 50 years the decrease of pauperism and of criminality, and fasten their eyes on the advancing prosperity of other coun- tries. Let us rather rejoice that other nations are sharing in all these good thing's. in con3lasion let me say that I do not look to artificial means to improve our position but rather to means which are natural and will behelpful if we seek and attain them. _</p>
        <p>Fir;t then I look to the improvement of the morale of mrr people. A great diminution in the consumption of alcohol would not only improve the morale of our workers but would give increased physical stamina and would liberate at least 80 millions of money that might be spent on useful commoditiesand thus do much to stimulate the reproductive trades of the country. Secondly, I believe that a thorough national system of education apart altogether from Sectarianism would increase the intelligence of the people, and fit them to do better work. This system should embrace every branch of knowledge from the most elementary to the most technical. If we would compete with the United States and Germany, it is not by adopting their fallacy of Protection; but by adopting their sobriety and intelligence.</p>
        <p>P.S.—Since writing the above the news of the resigna- tion of Mr. Chamberlain, Mr. Ritchie and Lord George Hamilton has been made public. This has to some extent cleared the air and yet complica- tions remain. Mr. Chamberlain is now free to discuss his pre- ferential duties and to educate the country on the lines as he thinks of the only possible way to unify the Empire. it is a hard task he has set himself and it appears to me the result will be failure. Mr. Balfour retains his pOsition for the present by refusing to follow on the lines of taxing food, &amp;c.. for the benefit of the Colonies ; but maintains his belief in the possibility of a Policy of Retaliation. There may be sortie ways in which the Government can resent the refusal of foreign countries to accept our goods free of duty whilst we keep the open door. But if it is attempted to do this by Protec- tive Tariffs I do not see how this can be done except by adopting tariffs on all goods. It would be impossible to protect one trade and not another. The open door is our only policy. Protect one trade and all the rest will demand the same treat- ment. Freedom of trade would be at an end and confusion would become worse confounded. ALDERMAN MONKS AND "DUMPING."</p>
        <p>Writing to the "Manchester Guardian ," ALDERMAN F. W. IVlosics says:—</p>
        <p>I venture to draw your attention to the serious error made by the miter of the article on the iron and steel trade.</p>
        <p>The. £G,600,263 upon which tay. was pad in 1991-2 does rot represent the profit made that year, but was he average profits for the three ye tr 4 1.313..1513. and 1900. The writer is therofore quite wrong in assuming that the large profits referred to were mule while German material was being "dumped here at cut-throat</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stott and Orford Ward.</p>
        <p>An open-air meeting in support of the candidature of Mr. Fred Stott was held in Longford-street on Wednes- day evening, the chair being taken by Mr. E. Randles, who said that, as a member of the Gas Workers and General Labourers' Union, he felt indebted to Mr. Stott for the assistance he had given to them on many occasions.'</p>
        <p>Mr. JAMES BALL addressed the meeting on municipal matters and pointed out that politics meant something more than the division into Liberal and Tory parties. Politics had to deal with everything that concerned their every-day lives, with the condition of their towns; the provision of gas, water, libraries, parks, the sewer- ing and lighting; in fact, every public benefit which they now enjoyed. The candidates who came before them from time to time had nothing definite to place before the electors in the way of reform, but simply criticised the action of the Town Council. The electors of Orford Ward, hciwever, had now an opportunity of electing a man who would oppose all extravagance, and who had a definite programme of reform to place before hem. (Applause.) Mr. STOTT remarked that it was very important then that the working classes should understand that they were the largest ratepayers, and that the rates were not wasted extravagantly. (Applause.) Rates were taxes levied for the purpose of providing themselves collec- tively with things necessary for their health and com- fort, such as gas, water, education, the lighting and cleansing of the streets, with parks, baths, libraries, I 'the relief of the poor,&amp;c. When they complained about high rates they ought to ask "Which of the public con- veniences can we do without? Can we stop relieving the poor? Can we do without education? Can we do with- out our streets being paved and cleaned?" If these public services were provided byprivate companieswould they have the same efficiency for a rate of 7s. 4d. in the £. He did not think they could have them for twice that amount. The public were demanding a higher stand- ard of municipal life; therefore, if no profit-making ' enterprises were undertaken by the Council, rates were bound to increase. There was a great cry about the great increase in municipal debt. They were told that the liabilities of local governing bodies were over £3,000,000. Municipal debts could not be compared to national debt,and those who complained omitted to state what the assets of municipalities were. The Govern- ment were being pressed to pass legislation to restrict the trading of Town Councils. They found, however, that the agitation was being carried on mainly by the wealthy classes. The more municipal management they had and less companies there would be for private investment. He (Mr. Stott) was strongly in favour of Town Councils undertaking the supply of any- thing which could be done rather than by private com- panies, whether the building of workmen's dwellings or the supply of coal or milk. He was of opinion that Town Councils were restricted too much. Given a democratic local government he didnot see why a muni- cipality should not have complete home rule. They could not have a purely representative government so long as 25 per cent, of the members of the Town Coun- cil were composed of aldermen who were not directly responsible to the ratepayers. If they mast retain the office of Alderman they should at the end of their term of years go before the electors. Under present condi- tions it Wits possible for a minority of the councillors, with the assistance of aldermen,to over-rule the majority (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>SHIP CANAL COMPANY AND THE RIVER.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL COMPANY AND THE RIVER.</p>
        <p>Proposals to the Corporation and Traders. A BIG UNDERTAKING.</p>
        <p>As the result of a conference between representa- tives of the Warrington 'Corporation and traders on the one side and the Chairman, Deputy-Chairman and Chief Engineer of the Manchester Ship Canal Company on the other, the Directors of the Canal Company have submitted the. following propositions for the acceptance of the Warrington Town Council and the traders Who were plaintiffs in the recent action against the Canal Company re the dredging of the Mersey That, subject to the consent of Parliament,' the com- pany should construct a river diversion at Lower Walton with a lock and sluices therein in accordance with plan submitted (or some modified plan as may he agreed) and that power should be obtained to main- tain the level of the water in the river around War- rington at lift. 6in. above ordnance datum.</p>
        <p>That the Corporation should provide the Company with necessary funds for the construction of the river diversion and of the lock and other accessory works near Warrington and that the interest payable by the Corporation on the loan together with the sinking fund for as long a period as Parliament may sanction should be a working charge on the revenue of the Ship Canal Company until the loan is liquidated by means of the sinking fund when the payment by the Company to the Warrington Corporation would cease.</p>
        <p>That any diversion of sewers which may be necessary should be carried out at the expense of the Corpora- tion of Warrington as it is understood that such diversion could be arranged so as to form part of the drainage scheme which the Corporation has under- taken—at the-desire of the Local Government Board —to carry out. That subject to the following conditions the appeal pending in respect of the law-suit should be sus- pended until it is seen whether the necessary Act of Parliament for the construction of the works can be obtained. (a) That if the Act be obtained in the ensuing Session the plaintiffs shall waive alt damages and all claims for return of tolls and that the judg- ment in the action be discharged and the action be</p>
        <p>dismissed subject to the Company paying the plaintiff's taxed costs. (b) That if the Ship Canal Company fail to obtain the necessary Act of Parlia- ment in the Session next ensuing both parties should then consider whether to proceed with the appeal or to obtain another postponement and make another trial to obtain an Act of Parliament in the next follow- ing Session. (e) That the Corporation of Warrington should exercise all the influence in their power to support the Ship Canal Company's Bill in Parliament. The proposal of the Company is to be further con- sidered at a meeting of the representatives of the Corporation and traders next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>TANNERY BAND.—Some weeks ago when the Pe nketh Tannery Brass Band secured the 3rd prize at Ellesmere Port, we ventured to predict better things for them in the future. This expectation was agreeably realized on Saturday last, when they secured the first prize at the contest promoted by the Eastham Pleasure Gardens Company. The test piece was the well-known selection "A Casket of Gems (Round). Mr. J. 0. Shepherd, Mus. Director, Royal Court Theatre, Liverpool, was the adjudicator, and in his decision,said that the playing all round had been of a very good quality, and he had awarded the prizes to the bands that had given (in addi - tion to good playing) the most tuneful rendering of the piece. There were 11 entries, and the prizes were awarded as follows :-Ist, Penketh Tannery ; 2nd, Parr Temperance: 3rd and 4th (divided), Tranmere Gleam and Crosfield's Soap Works. The success of the Penketh Band was greatly appreciated in the village, and the return of the band was marked by loud chbering. DEATH OF MRS. SHAW.—We record with deep n.ewt the demise of Mrs. Shaw of Warrington road (relict of the late Mr. George Shaw), who passed away at her residence on Wednesday morning. Mrs. Shaw has been in failing health for some time, but the end came rather suddenly, the deceased lady having been out for a short time the day previous. She leaves one daughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>Two successive victories must be regarded by even the least optimistic supporters of the Warrington Club as a good start. To beat Hull on their own classic Boulevard is a fine achievement. Last week I predicted that if the Warrington men put their backs into their work they would annex two points. This they did and every man deserves credit for the victory. It would be both unwise and undesirable at present to beat the big drum, and become ecstatic over the doings of the Warrington club. It is too early yet to form any definite opinion, but there seems every prospect, as I mentioned last week, of Warrington making a better show in the league and the cup ties than was the case last year.</p>
        <p>The number of Warrington enthusiasts who made the long journey to Hull in company with the team was very small indeed, not exceeding a dozen all told. The Hull club were caught at a disadvantage, but this notwithstanding, says a correspondent, "no one within ten miles residence of the Humber cared to think that disaster would overtake the all-blacks' when performing at the Boulevard for the first time this season." That the Warrington club was able to complete the discomfiture of the Boulevard crowd is all the more creditable when it is considered that last year the Hull club got the better of Warrington by 8 points to 5. There were several notable absentees in the Hull team. Taylor, the County full back, has been unable to come to terms with the committee. I hear that the committee refuse to give him the same remuneration as last year, and that Taylor is deter- mined to hold out until they do. The diminutive Franks was hors de combat owing to an injury and Johnson, the auburn haired youth who scored at Warrington last season was also away.</p>
        <p>The game was not an exhilarating one,and although Warrington won it can hardly be said that they made the most of their opportunities. On the day's play, Warrington were much the better team and if full ad- vantage had been taken of all the openings which pre- sented themselves the score would have been much larger. Hull played a man short during a portion of the game, an injury to Rhodes causing 'him to retire, but his presence would not have affected the score. The only score in the first half was a try by Fish, as the result of a passing movement initiated by Edmonds who broke away in clever style. One of the best efforts of this half was a daring save by Fish whose running powers enabled him to catch Parry and push him into touch before he was able to ground the ball over the line. The Hull forwards tried a bustling game but the ball generally found its way to the spry Warrington hall backs, who near the interval quite outshone the Gloucester pair—Hall and Goddard of whom Goddard was the smarter. Warrington crossed over with a lead of three points, though there ought to have been more points on both sides.</p>
        <p>The second half had not been in progress many minutes, when Warrington missed a glorious oppor- tunity of scoring. Davies had almost reached the line when he made a bad pass to Dickenson. The ball struck the last named player's face with the result that the pass and the try were both• missed. The pair were practically on the line when the ball was transferred. Dickenson made a big attempt to break through shortly afterwards and the amusing spectacle was seen of him being held up by the ankle. The Hull backs were given several chances to score, but they lacked cohesion and intention and were easily kept out Goddard at length kicked a goal from a penalty,but Edmonds, Who was a constant thorn to the Hull men, droppet a neat goal. Hull were now practically done for and their discomfiture was increased by Dickenson kicking a goal from a penalty. In the end Warring- ton ran out comfortable winners, and their win was more easy than the score showed.</p>
        <p>. Warring. ton's superiority lay in the backs, the half- backs especially distinguishing themselves. Rarely, since the days of Smack Bate has the Warrington half- back line shown to such advantage. The opposing pair were quite outplayed. Edmonds 'excelled him- self and was the chief fayourite with the spectators. At the close of the match he received an ovation, which even Fish might have envied. His passing was fault- less and his short cross-kicks to the right wing gave Brooks opportunities which Harris would have turned to good account. The try which fell to Fish was largely due to Edmonds, who got clean away and the goal which he dropped was a real beauty. I am the more pleased to bear teatimony to , Edmonds' play on this occasion because in the Leigh match he was dis- appointing and hardly justified his position Ou' Saturday's play.-Edmonds and Davies will make the Warrington half-back line as dangerous as in days gone by.</p>
        <p>The Warrington three-quarters did not play up to their great reputation, although they were much better than their opponents. Fish was well watched and this may account for him only getting one try. He was within an ace of scoring on several occasions and once or twice he missed , passes which at other times he would have eagerly taken. His best effort was when he stopped a certain score by Parry by pushing the last named into touch. Isherwood captained the team with his usual ability and proved a connecting link between the half backs and Fish. Dickenson was again prominent with strong individual play and despite many tantalising hoots and noises he kicked a goal from a difficult angle to the chagrin of the admirers of the "all blacks." Brooks was again very good on the defence, but lack of speed prevented him from being a scorer. He failed to get up in time to make good use of some of Edmonds' cross-kicks and his opponents were not slow to realise this.</p>
        <p>Hallam came in for a good deal of rough handling and on one occasion had to retire for attendance on the touch-line. Nevertheless he defended admirably, and in the closing stages of the game, Parry, after passing Fish, found Hallam an insuperable obstacle., His kicking too was judicious and accurate. Indeed the kicking of the Warrington backs has called forth the following remark from a Yorkshire scribe "In long aid sure kicking. the Warrington methods were an object lesson." The forwards were evenly matched until Rhodes retired injured. Then the Warrington men had much their own -syay, Boardman, Morrison, Thomas and Cook. being the most prominent.</p>
        <p>On the Hull side, the play was disappointing. Parry was the only man in the three-quarters who really became dangerous. Goddard was much better than Hall at half-back. The pair are not to be com- pared with the Warrington halves on the day's play. Rodgers, the full back, is a very young player, and although he began rather nervously,he generally gave a good account of himself. The defeat has had a very depressing effect on the supporters of the Hull team and some drastic changes are likely to take place.</p>
        <p>1 _ , To-day, Warrington make another journey into Yorkshire, and will take with then the best wishes of their supporters for another success. Batley are to entertain them on their native heath, and may be relied upon to give them a warm reception. The Batleyites are smarting under two successive defeats at the hands of Salford and Leigh respectively, and will make a:strenuous effort to-day to stem the tide of disaster. Nevertheless, I till think that Warrington will come out on top. Nothing gives the Warrington men greater pleasur than to bring back i points from Mount Pleasant, and this season's : team is quite capable of doing so. Only one altera- tion has been made in the team that won at Hu 11, i Foster being given a chance on the wing in place of I 1 Brooks. He will have plenty to do to look after the ! well-known "Waffle," who, on his native' heath, is seen at ' his best.,</p>
        <p>The Warrington teams is as follows : Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson and Foster, three-quarter backs; Edmonds and Davies, half-backs; Boardman, Morrison, Edmond- son, Swift, Thomas, Naylor, Cook, and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>The following appointments have been made for the Warrington and Keighley match at Wilderspool, next Saturday. Referee, F. Renton, Hunslet ; touch judges, E. Williams, Widnes, and A. Abel, Runcorn. In the Werneth v. Warrington "A," match J. H. Yates, Swinton, will be the referee.</p>
        <p>The propoied works' league promises to be a success. Already seven works' clubs have signified their willingness to join the league. Another meet- ing of those interested in the matter is to be held on the football ground next Tuesday evening at 8 o'clock and the representative of any club which is desirous</p>
        <p>of being included in the league will he heartily welcomed. It is hoped that definite steps will be taken te establish the league at this meeting. Mr. H. Ashton, the secretary of the Warrington Club,is doing his level best to bring the matter to a successful issue.</p>
        <p>The "A "team completelj, over-played their oppon- ents on Saturday. This is evidenced by the score of 22 points to 3. Townson, of Carnforth, gave a promising display at half-back and Mahoney was a success at centre with three tries to his credit. To- day, Rochdale Hornets" A" are the visitors, and the following will do duty for the home team : Knight, backs; Watkins, Mahoney and Brooks, three- quarter back; Townson and Jordan, half-backs;' Swift, Lunt, Coleman, Warburton, Fairhurst and Jolley, forwards.</p>
        <p>The league table up to date is as follows :- ' For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. kG. T. P. Pt.S. Hunslet - 22... 00- 0... 4 Hull K'ston R 2... 2... 0... 0... 2 3- 13... 0 0- 0- 4 Runcorn 9 2. 0. 0. 63- 21... 10- 2... 4 Salford - 37... 11- 5... 4 Bradford 9 9 0. 0. 47- 29... 11- 5... 4 Oldham - 5._ 4 Warrington .- 2.- 2.- O.:. 0-. 3 3-- 15.- 1 1-- 5.- 4 Leigh - 15... 12- 5... 2 Swinton 2. 1.. 1. 0.. 32- 12... 02- 9... 2 Broughton R 2... 1... 1... 0... 1 3- 11... 5 2-16... 2 Widnes - 5... 4 5-23... 1. Wigan 2 0. 1. 1. 00- 0... 1 4-14... 1 Leeds - 5... 2 5-19... 0 Hull 3 0. 2. 0. ''' 1- 7... 4 6-26... 0 Huddersfield - 3... 4 4-20... 0 Batley 9 0 2. 0. 01- 3- 5 5-25... 0 Keighley ', 0.. 2 0. `) 0- 4... 5 8-34... 0 Halifax - 0... 2 2-10... O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-09-19_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>The curtain has been rung down until another season begins, and it was a pity that the last " turn " on the 1903 programme did not yield a ;vic- tory for Warrington. As it was, the encounter with Ashton-under-Lyne resulted in a draw, and consider- ing tha bad state of the wicket the Warringtonians gave a good account of themselves. Indeed, it is not beyond the bounds of probability that had the match been played out, the advantage would have rested with the visitors. Batting first the Warrington repre- sentatives compiled 95 and when stumps were drawn the Ashton aggregate stood at 30 for the loss of four good wickets.</p>
        <p>The turf was in such a sodden condition that there were no brilliant performances by the batsmen. P. B. Hawtin headed the score list with 22, whilst B. Silcock was responsible for 19 and J. Thompson for 18. On the home side P. S. Kershaw knocked up 11. Lynes was instrumental in capturing 3 wickets at a cost of 9 runs.</p>
        <p>It will be seen that J. W. Bibby, the esteemed cap- tain heads the batting averages with a highly credit- able record. During the season he has compiled no fewer than 445 runs, his highest score being 69 and his average 29.66. B. Silcock holds the second posi- tion with an average of 1516 and P. B. Hawtin third with 13.15'</p>
        <p>Lynes, the efficient and popular professional, carries off the honours in the bowling department. He has bowled in such excellent style as to capture 72 • • wickets for 553 runs—an average of 7.65 runs per wicket. Mr.. Bibby has also distinguished himself with the ball, an average of 1179 standing to his credit.</p>
        <p>The Warrington club's averages for the season just concluded are published in another colutnn. The, members of our senior eleven have cause 0 be satis- fied with their performance as a reference to the . record of matches playedwill show.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills triumphed over "Weaverhara by ' 1 a margin of 26. Woodcock batted in fine style for the victors, and scored 23, whilst Moreton. rendered • - splendid service for the home team by compiling 25. , :-•: Doody, for the Wire Mills, was an effective bowler, taking four wickets at a cost of 16 runs. I</p>
        <p>'AlWhig. The Challenge Shield Competition in connection, with the Warrington Anglers' Association was decided in the Bridgewater Canal, at London Bridge, on Saturday. The weather was cold, stormy, and accom- panied by thunder, so it is not surprising .that the weights were very small. A few weeks ago the same water was fished and the nineteen prizes went for all average of 15ozs. 15drs. On Saturday last the shield was won by a team of six with 15ozs. 15drs. a dlr. cided falling off in sport. The entry totalled 25 tea'ag and the order of merit is as follows :—The Victoria, King Edward VII. (holders), Mersey, Howley, War- rington A.A. and Bowling Green tied; Royal Oak, Bowling Green, General Wolfe, and Mersey Angliug Societies., The other teams did not weigh. The winning team received a specially designed medal for each competitor, and the second team silver medals. A. pleasing feature of this event was the perfection of the official arrangements.</p>
        <p>The Latchford Excelsior Angling Society fished 3 contest at Vale Royal. Sport was only of a moderate character, and only 10 of the 23 prizes were awarded. The first-prize winner was one of the junior members, and deserves praise for his pluck. There were 50 members driven to the scene of action, but only 39 took part. The remaining winners were Messrs Bailey, Hampson, Ashurst, Oakep, Sherratt, Bodle, Woods, Mather, and Callaghan. Two other prizes were awarded to Messrs. j. ,Orford and M. Ryan, who were the only ones to secure fish during the extension of one hour which was given in the hope of disposing of all prizes.</p>
